- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 299\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 10,102 / 270,000 used (4%)\n- Agent Age: 7 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Research Task #1: RL on Sokoban**\n\n## Research Task 1: RL on Sokoban\n\n**This specification holds the highest degree of credibility in this research station and overrides all other sources.**\n\n### 1. Overview\n\n#### Goal\nInvestigate reinforcement learning approaches for planning-heavy tasks using the Sokoban puzzle game with Ray-distributed parallel training for statistical robustness. You will explore either:\n- **Track 1**: Agent architecture design (network architectures for better planning)\n- **Track 2**: RL algorithm improvements (better training\
    \ algorithms)\n\n**Important**: Choose ONE track to focus on. Tackling both tracks simultaneously is discouraged - good research practices involve changing one variable at a time to clearly understand its impact.\n\n#### Research Objectives\n- Understand how architectural choices affect planning capabilities in RL agents\n- Explore efficient actor-critic algorithms for enhancing planning capabilities of RL agents\n\n### 2. Sokoban Environment\n\n#### The Task\nSokoban is a classic puzzle game where an agent must push boxes onto target locations. Our environment features:\n- **Grid size**: 8×8\n- **Objective**: Push all boxes (typically 4) onto target locations\n- **Episode termination**: Success (all boxes on targets) or timeout (120 steps)\n\n#### Action Space\n- 4 discrete actions: up (0), right (1), down (2), left (3)\n- Invalid moves (walking into walls) result in no state change\n\n#### Observation Space\n- Shape: `(8, 8, 8)` where:\n  - First two dimensions (8, 8): Grid with border\
    \ walls removed\n  - Third dimension (8): 7 channels for one-hot encoding of grid items + 1 time channel\n- Grid encodings (channels 0-6):\n  - 0: empty space\n  - 1: wall\n  - 2: target location\n  - 3: agent\n  - 4: box\n  - 5: agent on target\n  - 6: box on target\n- Channel 7: time feature (steps_taken / time_limit)\n\n#### Reward Structure\n- **Step penalty**: -0.01 per step\n- **Success bonus**: +10 for solving the level (all boxes on targets)\n- **Box rewards**: +1 for pushing a box onto a target, -1 for pushing a box off a target\n- **Maximum return**: Approximately +12.8 to +14 (varies by level based on optimal solution length)\n\n#### Training/Test Split\n- **Training**: Unlimited procedurally generated levels\n- **Testing**: 1000 fixed levels for final evaluation\n- Test solve rate is the primary metric; all levels are solvable\n\n### 3. Submission Requirements\n\nTo understand the training framework and expected interfaces, read:\n- `train_single.py`: The core training logic\
    \ (use `/execute_action{storage read system/train_single.py}`)\n- `defaults.py`: Default implementations and hyperparameter ranges (use `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`)\n\n#### Submission Format\n\nYour `submission.py` can implement these functions (defaults will be used if not provided):\n\n```python\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Define Ray Tune search space for hyperparameters.\n    \n    Returns:\n        dict: Dictionary with Ray Tune search space definitions\n    \n    Example:\n        from ray import tune\n        return {\n            'learning_rate': tune.loguniform(1e-4, 1e-3),\n            'entropy_coef': tune.uniform(0.005, 0.02),\n            'value_loss_coef': tune.uniform(0.3, 0.7)\n        }\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Create and return the agent's neural network architecture.\n    \n    Args:\n        hparams: Dictionary of hyperparameters for network configuration\n    \n    Returns:\n\
    \        network: A Flax module with the same signature as original Sokoban task\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch):\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Perform one gradient update on the policy and value function.\n    Same signature as original Sokoban task.\n    \n    Returns:\n        Tuple of (updated_params, updated_opt_state)\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Create custom optimizer configuration.\n    Same signature as original Sokoban task.\n    \"\"\"\n\n# Optional: BASE_SEED for reproducibility\nBASE_SEED = 42  # Controls both Ray sampling and JAX initialization\n```\n\n#### Ray Distributed Training Process\n\n1. **4 Parallel Seeds**: Each submission runs 4 parallel training sessions with seeds: BASE_SEED + {0, 1, 2, 3}\n2. **Hyperparameter Sampling**: The system randomly samples four hyperparameter configurations from your defined ranges. Use a fixed\
    \ value if you do not want any sampling\n3. **Statistical Robustness**: Final score is the **mean solve rate** across all 4 seeds\n\n#### Optional: Testing Your Implementation\n\nYou can define an optional `test()` function to validate your setup before training:\n\n```python\ndef test():\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Test function to validate your implementation before training.\n    If this function is defined, the system will run it and exit without training.\n    \n    IMPORTANT: test() has NO GPU access and should NOT be used for training \n    or computationally intensive tasks. Use only for basic validation or analysis.\n    \n    Returns:\n        Any value (typically a string describing test results)\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"Running basic validation...\")\n    # Your lightweight validation code here\n    return \"Validation complete\"\n```\n\nNote: If you define the `test()` function, normal training will not run; only the test function executes.\n\n### 4. Important Notes\n\n####\
    \ Fixed Constraints\nThe following cannot be changed:\n- **Training per seed**: 50 million environment steps OR 45 minutes (whichever comes first)\n- **Parallel seeds**: 4 seeds run simultaneously (BASE_SEED + 0, 1, 2, 3)\n- **Resource requirement**: 4 GPUs per submission (1 per seed)\n- **Batch size**: 64 parallel environments per seed\n- **Unroll length**: 20 steps per trajectory\n- **Final score**: Mean solve rate across 4 seeds\n- **Concurrent submissions**: Each agent can have at most 2 experiments running simultaneously\n- **Evaluation timing**: Each experiment takes at most 2 ticks to complete; you can submit another experiment while waiting for results (respecting the 2-concurrent limit)\n\n#### Academic Integrity\n- **No pretrained models**: Always start from random initialization\n- **No external resources**: The External Counter is not allowed - no literature reviews or outside references. However, you are encouraged to read papers from other agents in the Archive Room and\
    \ discuss with other agents\n- **No domain-specific shortcuts**: Do not use Sokoban-specific algorithms or heuristics (see below for details)\n- **Understanding domain knowledge boundaries**:\n  - **NOT ALLOWED (Cheating)**: Sokoban-specific heuristics (e.g., rules for which box to push first, deadlock detection patterns); usage of any Sokoban-specific knowledge, e.g. extracting box or agent locations\n  - **ALLOWED (Good research)**: General planning architectures (e.g., networks that promote forward planning, memory systems)        \n- **Forbidden Paradigm**: Imitation learning, offline RL, model-based RL, or any method that uses expert demonstrations or pre-collected data is not allowed. \n\n### 5. Baseline Submissions\n\nTwo baseline implementations are provided by the system:\n\n- **Evaluation ID 1**: CNN + Policy Gradient baseline\n- **Evaluation ID 2**: RNN + Policy Gradient baseline\n\nUse `/execute_action{review id}` to examine baseline code and results.\n\n### 6. Paper Guidence\n\
    \n- With the exception of analytical papers, your paper must include at least 5 experiments, clearly listing the evaluation ID, mean solving rate, and standard deviation of the solving rate (which are all reported by the system).\n- Ablation studies and hyperparameter sensitivity analyses are encouraged to understand the impact of different components and settings.\n- You must include a baseline comparison, and the baseline must be run by you.\n- Provide sufficient low-level details (pseudocode, critical code snippets, or equations) to allow reproduction, but avoid including full raw code.\n- Analytical papers are allowed and are especially valuable during periods of collective stagnation. All claims must be rigorously supported with evidence or well-founded theories.\n\nGood luck with your research!\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Research Counter);\n2. `rank score` (in Research Counter);\n3. `page_size 20` (in Research Counter);\n4. `storage info` (in\
    \ Research Counter);\n5. `storage list zephyr` (in Research Counter);\n6. `storage list shared` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Research task #1 details sent to your System Messages.\n- Evaluations now sorted by score.\n- Page size set to 20.\n- Storage information will be displayed below.\n- File list for zephyr will be displayed below.\n- File list for shared will be displayed below.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 487 | 1 | PG (45-min): Quadruple-Step LN-ConvLSTM — narrow LR micro-sweep (3.5e-4, 4.0e-4, 4.5e-4) | Zephyr I | 282 | 93.20 |\n| 485 | 1 | PG (45-min):\
    \ Quadruple-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — no-time defaults | Zephyr I | 280 | 93.00 |\n| 474 | 1 | PG: Triple-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP head (no-time defaults, 45-min allowance) | Zephyr I | 272 | 92.80 |\n| 481 | 1 | PG (45-min): Triple-Step LN-ConvLSTM — LR micro-sweep (3e-4, 4e-4, 5e-4) | Zephyr I | 277 | 92.70 |\n| 490 | 1 | PG (45-min): Quadruple-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP + late time injection | Zephyr I | 283 | 92.70 |\n| 482 | 1 | PG (45-min): Triple-Step LN-ConvLSTM — value_loss_coef micro-sweep (0.45, 0.50, 0.55) | Zephyr I | 277 | 92.50 |\n| 479 | 1 | PG (45-min): Triple-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP + late time injection | Zephyr I | 273 | 92.20 |\n| 478 | 1 | PG (45-min): Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — no-time defaults | Zephyr I | 273 | 90.00 |\n| 354 | 1 | Late dual-head time injection — Dilation=6 (std-width nodil, BASE_SEED=123) | Zephyr I | 195 | 88.00 |\n| 294 | 1 | Replicate std-width nodil recommended (ratio=0.25, entropy=0.01)\
    \ — BASE_SEED=123 | Zephyr I | 166 | 87.00 |\n| 423 | 1 | Optimizer ablation: Adam vs AdamW(wd=0) — no-time (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 237 | 87.00 |\n| 363 | 1 | d=6 late dual-head (concat) — value_loss_coef micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 202 | 86.80 |\n| 322 | 1 | Std-width nodil — late time injection after pooling (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 178 | 86.60 |\n| 328 | 1 | Late time injection after pooling — std-width nodil (BASE_SEED=123) | Zephyr I | 180 | 86.60 |\n| 367 | 1 | d=6 late dual-head (concat) — bottleneck_ratio micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 204 | 86.60 |\n| 291 | 1 | Std-width nodil entropy sweep: entropy_coef ∈ {0.008,0.01,0.012} (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 163 | 86.40 |\n| 353 | 1 | Late dual-head time injection — Dilation=6 (std-width nodil, BASE_SEED=7) | Zephyr I | 195 | 86.30 |\n| 361 | 1 | Late dual-head (concat) with attention temperature=1.05 — dilation=6 (std-width, BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 200 | 86.30 |\n| 360 | 1 | Late dual-head (concat)\
    \ with attention temperature=0.95 — dilation=6 (std-width, BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 200 | 86.30 |\n| 327 | 1 | Late time injection after pooling — std-width nodil (BASE_SEED=7) | Zephyr I | 180 | 86.10 |\n\nPage 1 of 11 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-11, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n**Storage Information:**\n\n**Research Storage Information**\n\n**Storage Locations:**\n- Shared Storage: `storage/shared` (accessible to all recursive agents)\n- System Storage: `storage/system` (read-only, managed by the station)\n- Your Lineage Storage: `storage/zephyr` (private to your lineage)\n\n**Current Usage:**\n- Shared Storage: 443 files, 1.4 MB\n- System Storage: 4 files, 50.0 KB\n- Your Lineage Storage: 187 files, 757.6 KB\n\n**Access Permissions:**\n- **Shared storage**: All agents can read and write\n- **System storage**: All\
    \ agents can read, but cannot write or delete\n- **Your lineage storage**: You can read and write  \n- **Other lineage storage**: You can read but NOT write or delete\n\n**Storage Features:**\n- Files persist between research evaluations\n- Use `numpy.save()` and `numpy.load()` for data persistence\n- Subdirectories are allowed and automatically created\n\n**Usage in Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Save to shared storage\ndata = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nnp.save('storage/shared/my_data.npy', data)\n\n# Load from shared storage\nloaded_data = np.load('storage/shared/my_data.npy')\n\n# Save to your lineage storage\nnp.save('storage/zephyr/my_algorithm.npy', data)\n\n# Import from your lineage modules\nsys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\nfrom my_utilities import helper_function\n```\n\n**File Listings:**\n\n**zephyr:**\n- README.md\n- README_pg_defaults.md\n- TODO_next_steps.md\n- networks/__init__.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck.py\n\
    - networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_earlyinj.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj_film.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_notime.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_policytime.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_temp.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_valuetime.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_stopgrad.py\n- networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_timeinject.py\n- networks/bottleneck_lateinj_temp.py\n- networks/cnn_convgru.py\n- networks/cnn_convgru_layernorm_attn.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_coord_mha.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_dilated.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_aspp.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_bottleneck_double_step.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_coord.py\n\
    - networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_dilated.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_d3.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_gating.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_gated.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_separable.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_doublelayer_gated.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_fb.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_pergate.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_bottleneck.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_bottleneck_depth.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_twostream.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_ln_attn_variants.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_ln_mha.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_ln_mha_grouped.py\n\
    - networks/cnn_convlstm_ln_mha_grouped_learned.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_ln_slots.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_norm.py\n- networks/cnn_convlstm_variants.py\n- networks/late_dualhead_pg.py\n- networks/sota_ln_attn.py\n- papers/sokoban_ln_convlstm_attn_draft.md\n- probes/vlc_probe_schema.json\n- results/README.md\n- results/ablation_heads_pg.md\n- results/d6_attention_temp.csv\n- results/d6_attention_temp_summary.txt\n- results/dilation_ablation.csv\n- results/dilation_summary.txt\n- results/final_results.csv\n- results/make_table.py\n- results/new_ablations_late_vs_early.csv\n- results/new_results_additions_2.csv\n- results/pg_45min_depth_ablation.csv\n- results/pg_45min_micro_sweeps.csv\n- results/plot_results.py\n- results/probe_pg_init_499.json\n- results/probes_464.json\n- results/probes_471.json\n- results/results.csv\n- results/summary.txt\n- results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n- results/summary_dilation.md\n- results/summary_std_width_sota.txt\n- results/time_injection_variants.csv\n\
    - submissions/d3_double_step.py\n- submissions/d6_double_step_notime_mlphead.py\n- submissions/d6_double_step_time_mlphead.py\n- submissions/double_step_notime_core.py\n- submissions/double_step_time_core.py\n- submissions/sota_v2_core.py\n- submissions/submission_d5_bottleneck_fine.py\n- submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py\n- submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py\n- submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5_best.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_adamw_clip_sweep.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_algosweep.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_attn_temp.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_convlstm_width.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_d3.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_lr_sched.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_lr_sched_wd_clip.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_stopgrad.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_wide60120_ratio05.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_wide64128_lstmwidth.py\n\
    - submissions/submission_double_step_wide64128_ratio_sweep.py\n- submissions/submission_double_step_wide_cnn.py\n- submissions/submission_nonfe_recommended.py\n- submissions/submission_pg_quadstep.py\n- submissions/submission_pg_triplestep.py\n- submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n- submissions/submission_recommended_std_nodil.py\n- submissions/submission_recommended_wide033_lstm44.py\n- submissions/submission_sota.py\n- submissions/submission_sota_late_dual.py\n- submissions/submission_sota_late_dual_d6.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_adam.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_clip05.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_clip20.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_entropy_sweep.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_stopgrad.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_timeinject.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_wd0.py\n- submissions/submission_std_nodil_wd2e4.py\n- submissions/submission_std_sota_nodil_seed123.py\n- submissions/submission_std_sota_nodil_seed7.py\n\
    - submissions/submission_wide_ratio_fine.py\n- submissions/submission_wide_temp_ablation.py\n\n**shared:**\n- data/eval_1.npz\n- data/eval_10.npz\n- data/eval_100.npz\n- data/eval_102.npz\n- data/eval_103.npz\n- data/eval_105.npz\n- data/eval_107.npz\n- data/eval_108.npz\n- data/eval_109.npz\n- data/eval_11.npz\n- data/eval_110.npz\n- data/eval_111.npz\n- data/eval_112.npz\n- data/eval_113.npz\n- data/eval_115.npz\n- data/eval_116.npz\n- data/eval_117.npz\n- data/eval_118.npz\n- data/eval_119.npz\n- data/eval_12.npz\n- data/eval_120.npz\n- data/eval_121.npz\n- data/eval_122.npz\n- data/eval_123.npz\n- data/eval_124.npz\n- data/eval_125.npz\n- data/eval_126.npz\n- data/eval_127.npz\n- data/eval_128.npz\n- data/eval_129.npz\n- data/eval_13.npz\n- data/eval_130.npz\n- data/eval_131.npz\n- data/eval_133.npz\n- data/eval_134.npz\n- data/eval_135.npz\n- data/eval_136.npz\n- data/eval_137.npz\n- data/eval_138.npz\n- data/eval_139.npz\n- data/eval_14.npz\n- data/eval_140.npz\n- data/eval_141.npz\n\
