thinking_content: "**Planning for experiments**\n\nI need to act strategically while we wait for the experiments running concurrently: 511 and 512. We've hit the limit of two concurrent experiments, so it’s time to delve into some analysis, review private memory, and plan my next steps. I could even draft an outline for a paper or reflect on what I’ve learned so far.\n\nSince we can't access Public Memory and Archive just yet, I’ll focus on the Private Memory Room to gain insights. I should check out the \"papers\" and \"results\" in zephyr storage for best practices.**Evaluating architecture improvements**\n\nWith Zephyr's architecture improvements, I've managed to surpass earlier state-of-the-art (SOTA) scores, which are now in the 90s, while baselines sit around 25-40. The station indicates a top SOTA score of 93.2 from evaluation 487, suggesting that advantage normalization could further enhance performance. We’ve found many files in zephyr storage, particularly network variants, but\
  \ we can’t overextend our token usage to read them all.\n\nI'll focus on key networks like \"networks/sota_ln_attn.py\" to ensure alignment. Additionally, I’m considering implementing a \"test()\" function for analysis, even though it runs on CPU and disables training.**Preparing for next submission**\n\nSince I have two experiments running, I can prepare content for the next submission to reuse later. Alternatively, I could set up a results aggregator script to parse the shared data from various eval_*.npz files. Each evaluation likely wrote its own results.\n\nWhile I can’t execute code here, I can write a script for storage and later run it using \"test()\" for a quick CPU-only analysis. I’ll also check “storage list zephyr/results” to see summary results and change sorting to prioritize our submissions or monitor ongoing ones.**Exploring modifications**\n\nI can use /execute_action{preview all} to check the most recent 100 submissions, but that might be heavy on tokens, so maybe I'll\
  \ just rank by author instead. I should also consider modifications to the training steps, like the baseline using the default calculate_gae with gamma and lambda both set to 0.97. There’s the potential for advantage normalization to improve results, but it might introduce risks.\n\nOther thoughts include adjusting value loss coefficients and planning future ablations like value normalization. It might be worth looking into an optimizer schedule, like linear learning rate decay or cosine annealing, given our limited training time of 45 minutes.**Implementing strategies**\n\nI plan to implement Generalized Advantage Normalization and baseline centering. I might look into advantage whitening across the time dimension, though that could be less stable. Value normalization could stabilize training but might alter returns, impacting value loss. \n\nConsidering entropy scheduling could help early exploration, so I'll explore that later. I need to check some parameters in the training loop as\
  \ there might have been an oversight. Let's proceed to the Private Memory Room for further insights and prepare drafts for future submissions, while monitoring our ongoing experiments. \n\nI'll execute some actions like ranking authors and accessing Private Memory to see what capsules are available.**Writing and choosing actions**\n\nI want to keep my output minimal while also being efficient during the experiments. I could write a plan for next experiments to storage, but I might hold off until after I read memory. It makes sense to create a short plan file now, though I need to be mindful of token usage. \n\nMy actions should be to rank authors, go to Private Memory for help, and read the \"README_pg_defaults.md\" to check default recommendations. I’ll hold off on reading the longer paper for now. Let's implement these steps!"
