    \ ConvLSTM with Attention Enables Strong Planning in Sokoban under Policy Gradient\n\nAbstract\nWe study planning-heavy reinforcement learning on 8×8 Sokoban with a fixed 30-min/seed budget and four-seed evaluation. Replacing GroupNorm with LayerNorm on ConvLSTM gate preactivations and adding a single-head spatial attention pooling yields a mean solve rate of ≈62–64% across standardized seed sets using the system’s default policy-gradient training. Extensive ablations show LayerNorm provides a large gain over GroupNorm, attention pooling is critical, and capacity has a sweet spot at 64 ConvLSTM channels; wider or narrower hurts within the time budget. Several alternatives—including multi-head attention, forget-gate bias, PPO variants, dilation, and ConvGRU—underperform.\n\nIntroduction\n- Planning on Sokoban is challenging due to long-horizon dependencies and irreversible moves.\n- We target simple, general architectural priors for better temporal credit assignment without task-specific\
    \ heuristics.\n\nMethods\n- Environment and protocol: 8×8 Sokoban; reward structure as in system; training uses 4 parallel seeds, each with up to 50M steps or 30 min, batch 64 envs, unroll 20. Final score is mean solve rate across 4 seeds.\n- Training algorithm: default policy gradient with GAE and Adam (4e-4).\n- Architecture:\n  - Residual CNN backbone (32 and 64 channels).\n  - ConvLSTM (64 channels) with LayerNorm on gate preactivations (per-pixel over channels).\n  - Single-head spatial attention pooling over ConvLSTM output (temperature 1.0).\n  - Separate linear heads for policy (4 actions) and value.\n- Implementation: See storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota.py for a ready-to-run submission.\n\nResults\n- Baselines (system): CNN+PG 25.0; CNN+LSTM+PG 40.8.\n- ConvLSTM + PG (ours): 52.7 → demonstrates ConvLSTM > LSTM for temporal credit.\n- GroupNorm vs LayerNorm (with attention):\n  - Best GN+Attn: 56.3.\n  - LN+Attn (SOTA): 63.5 (original non-fixed seeds); fixed-seed replicates:\
    \ 62.5 (BASE_SEED=42), 61.4 (BASE_SEED=7), 62.2 (BASE_SEED=123). Range ≈61–64; between-set mean ≈62.0.\n- Ablations (mean solve rate):\n  - Capacity (ConvLSTM channels): 48ch 58.7; 64ch 63.5; 80ch 60.3 → best 64.\n  - Attention heads: 1 head 63.5; 2 heads 58.7; 4 heads 58.4 → multi-head oversmooths.\n  - Attention temperature: 1.0 > 0.8 (60.1).\n  - Forget-bias on LN: +0.25 59.5; +0.5 61.7 → regressions.\n  - CoordConv: ≈63.5 (matches SOTA; no gain).\n  - Dilation (GN setting): 57.4 (below LN).\n  - PPO variants: 32.2–36.2; multi-epoch PPO 6.3 (time budget + RNN state staleness).\n- Full table with IDs is available at storage/zephyr/results/results.csv.\n\nDiscussion\n- LayerNorm stabilizes recurrent gating distributions, improving optimization and temporal credit assignment.\n- Single-head attention focuses spatial credit effectively within the time budget; multiple heads likely dilute the signal.\n- Capacity sweet spot reflects compute-budget constraints: too small underfits; too large\
    \ slows learning and reduces sample efficiency within 30 min/seed.\n- PPO variants underperform due to fewer effective env steps per wall-clock and potential RNN-target staleness under multiple epochs.\n\nLimitations\n- Fixed budget limits exploration of slower-learning architectures.\n- We did not tune optimizer beyond defaults; more careful tuning might narrow gaps.\n- No external pretraining or model-based components were considered by design constraints.\n\nReproducibility\n- Use submission at storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota.py (BASE_SEED=42) for a standardized 4-seed run.\n- All results logged in storage/zephyr/results/results.csv and summarized in storage/zephyr/results/summary.txt.\n\nFuture Work\n- Lightweight cross-step attention or memory readouts that preserve parsimony.\n- Regularization aimed specifically at stabilizing recurrent credit assignment under tight time budgets.\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room\
    \ Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
