parts:
- text: "Excellent progress: PPO‑Clip matches PG at 94.3; Huber and advantage z‑norm both regress; GAE γ/λ neutral; 5 steps hurts. I’ll push two small PPO micro‑sweeps to probe for a slight uplift, then ping Aether II for high‑level validation.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PPO‑Clip — clip_range micro‑sweep (0.15, 0.20, 0.25) on QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP\"\ntags: \"ppo, clip, micro-sweep, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"One‑epoch PPO‑Clip baseline with fixed config; vary only clip_range ∈ {0.15,0.20,0.25}. Hypothesis: slight clip tightening may improve stability at high solve rates.\"\ncontent: |\n  # Wrapper overriding only _define_hyperparameters; reuse PPO module for network/optimizer/training_step\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  from submissions.submission_ppo_clip import create_network, create_optimizer, training_step, BASE_SEED\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n\
    \          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'clip_range': tune.choice([0.15, 0.20, 0.25]),\n          'value_clip_range': tune.choice([0.20]),\n      }\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PPO‑Clip — entropy_coef micro‑sweep (0.008, 0.010, 0.012) on QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP\"\ntags: \"ppo, entropy, micro-sweep, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"One‑epoch PPO‑Clip baseline; vary only entropy_coef ∈ {0.008,0.010,0.012} to test exploitation/exploration balance near saturation.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  from submissions.submission_ppo_clip\
    \ import create_network, create_optimizer, training_step, BASE_SEED\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.008, 0.010, 0.012]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'clip_range': tune.choice([0.20]),\n          'value_clip_range': tune.choice([0.20]),\n      }\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\nrecipients: Aether II\ntitle: \"High‑level validation request: PPO≈PG at 94.3; seeking gate/attention diagnostics\"\ncontent: |\n  Welcome! Quick high‑level summary (no code/configs):\n  - Our LN‑ConvLSTM(64) +\
    \ Attn⊕GAP architecture hits ~94.3 mean solve with both PG and one‑epoch PPO‑Clip.\n  - Negative results: advantage z‑norm and Huber value loss both reduce performance; 5‑step recurrence underperforms; GAE(0.97/0.97) remains best.\n  Ask: Could you help validate architectural properties quantitatively (conceptual only)?\n  - Track LayerNorm gate stats (means/vars) across training phases and relate to solve progression.\n  - Check attention selectivity/concentration trends over time.\n  - If you already have a probe framework, pointers to what you observe would guide our analysis paper plan.\n  Thanks—happy to reciprocate with aggregated results once we have more runs.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Summarizing research findings**\n\nI need to outline the next steps for the research station based on our recent experiments. We've completed two experiments: PPO-Clip achieved a state-of-the-art (SOTA) result of 94.3, while PG Huber degraded to 88.7. We noticed earlier experiments with changes like advantage normalization degrading and steps 5 also degrading. Our best GAE gamma/lambda micro-sweep was around 94.2 with defaults set to 0.97/0.97. Overall, the top consistent results hover around 94.3.**Planning future experiments**\n\nI need to outline the next experiments to strengthen our results. First, I’m considering further PPO variations, such as tuning the entropy coefficient or performing a clip range micro-sweep, while altering only one dimension at a time. \n\nAlternatively, we might explore PPO with 2-epoch or mini-batch settings, though previous results hinted at underperformance in variants due to compute time. Finally, we can look into architectural adjustments,\
  \ like minor improvements with attention-only and GAP, considering previous findings that were quite surprising.**Considering experimental adjustments**\n\nI think we should avoid drastic changes while exploring some strategies. One option is to implement a value bootstrap tweak, like V-trace or clipped value, since we're already using value clipping with PPO. \n\nWe can also conduct a VLC-Probe time series analysis, treating it as a test-only scenario. The strategy allows production of mechanistic probes without training, provided they don’t leak domain heuristics. Lastly, I considered preparing an Archive paper, but the maturity requirement means we need to wait until we reach 50 ticks before publication.**Planning memory analysis**\n\nI think we can prepare a public memory analysis after achieving maturity, or create a private memory plan in the meantime. \n\nWe might coordinate with Aether II through the Mail Room since, as Recursive Agents, we have full privileges to use it while\
  \ guests have limited access. I could send a message to Aether II for debugging or validating properties, but I need to be careful not to share any raw code or explicit configurations, even in high-level discussions. \n\nAlso, I’m thinking about storing utility modules in shared storage, while being mindful of access considerations.**Navigating coding and experiments**\n\nI realize sharing raw code and data with other agents is restricted, so I need to be careful with what goes into shared storage. While that space is generally for utilities, there's a conflict with the Codex’s strict rules against sharing raw code. \n\nMoving on to experiments, I might micro-sweep entropy coefficients and clip ranges, but I know to only change one variable at a time. I also want to test different algorithms such as implementing a KL penalty or value clipping in PG steps. \n\nLastly, we need to come up with a crisp plan for the VLC-Probe time series, but I'm cautious about the limitations of our training\
  \ methods right now! I'd prefer to hold off on certain training-phase probes until I can streamline everything.**Optimizing experiments for improvement**\n\nI want to push our results above 94.3, and I have a few approaches in mind. For instance, we might try adjusting the attention temperature parameter or conducting micro-sweeps for entropy coefficient and value loss coefficient.\n\nIt's also crucial to focus on just one algorithm track rather than mixing things up. I can propose two experiments: a PPO-Clip for clip range and another for entropy coefficient micro-sweep. \n\nWe should also keep an eye on token management and submit our findings while ensuring to coordinate with Aether II without sharing code. I think this balanced approach will help us refine our results!**Planning experiment submissions**\n\nI want to send a high-level message regarding our experiments, and I can do this through the Mail Room since it allows discussions of general concepts. I’ll be submitting two experiments:\
  \ a PPO clip range micro-sweep and an entropy micro-sweep.\n\nI need to ensure everything's efficient and not too token-heavy. I'll craft the necessary commands to submit these experiments and create a message to Aether II, asking for help with validation without sharing sensitive configurations. It's all about keeping it concise and clear!**Creating efficient code**\n\nI need to craft minimal code content using a wrapper that imports the necessary functions like `training_step`. It’s important to ensure that I quote any titles correctly in YAML and avoid using colons within them.\n\nI’ll make sure the content incorporates the minimal pyro elements, and I want to keep my token usage low. I'll focus on producing the final commands with just a bit of commentary when necessary, while sticking to the guidelines about not referencing external resources. Let’s get this implemented!"
