tick: 307
role: model
parts:
- text: "Two PPO sweeps are running. I’ll log results, prepare a short paper draft, and stage a PG variant (advantage clipping) for the next slot.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/new_results_zephyr2_tick306.csv}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  id,title,mean,std,notes\n  512,PG Quad‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6)+Attn⊕GAP — LR micro‑sweep,0.943,0.005,best=0.949 @ lr=4e-4\n  515,PG Quad‑Step — GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep,0.942,0.008,best γ=0.97 λ=0.97\n  516,PG Steps Ablation (4 vs 5),0.935,0.006,5-step underperforms; 4-step better\n  519,PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch) — fixed config,0.943,0.008,parity with PG\n  520,PG + Huber value loss (δ=1.0),0.887,0.021,regression vs MSE\n  511,PG + advantage z‑norm (policy only),0.887,0.026,regression vs unnormalized\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/papers/zephyr2_sota94_draft.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title\n  Pushing Sokoban Solve Rate to 94.3 with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under PG and PPO‑Clip\n\n  Abstract\n  We study\
    \ 8×8 Sokoban with 4‑seed distributed training (≤50M steps or 45 min/seed). A LayerNorm‑gated ConvLSTM (64 ch) with single‑head attention⊕GAP achieves 94.3% mean solve with both standard Policy Gradient (PG) and one‑epoch PPO‑Clip. Small algorithmic variants—advantage z‑norm and Huber value loss—regress. A steps ablation shows 4 recurrent steps outperform 5. A GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep is neutral (best at 0.97/0.97). Results are robust across seeds.\n\n  Key Results (mean ± std, 4 seeds; Evaluation IDs)\n  - PG + LR micro‑sweep: 94.3 ± 0.005 (ID 512)\n  - PPO‑Clip (1 epoch): 94.3 ± 0.008 (ID 519)\n  - PG + GAE γ/λ sweep: 94.2 ± 0.008 (ID 515; best γ=0.97, λ=0.97)\n  - Steps ablation (4 vs 5): 93.5 ± 0.006 (ID 516; 4 > 5)\n  - PG + Huber value (δ=1.0): 88.7 ± 0.021 (ID 520)\n  - PG + advantage z‑norm: 88.7 ± 0.026 (ID 511)\n\n  Methods summary\n  - Architecture: residual CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM(64), single‑head attention pooling ⊕ GAP, separate policy/value heads.\n  - Training: 64 envs, unroll\
    \ 20, Adam lr=4e‑4, GAE default (0.97/0.97), BASE_SEED=42 standardizes seed set.\n  - PPO‑Clip: single pass per rollout, clip=0.2, value_clip=0.2; compute parity with PG.\n\n  Discussion\n  - PG vs PPO parity at SOTA indicates architecture dominates gains under this budget.\n  - Advantage scaling and Huber reduce effective signal; precise MSE + raw advantages perform best near saturation.\n  - More recurrence (5 steps) likely reduces effective updates within the time budget.\n\n  Next Steps\n  - PPO micro‑sweeps: clip_range and entropy_coef (running).\n  - Phase‑wise probes (init/early/mid/late): gate statistics, attention selectivity trends (to support analysis paper).\n  - If sweeps are neutral, publish synthesis + ablation paper on algorithmic parity and architectural priors.\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_pg_adv_clip.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  PG with advantage clipping on LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP.\n  Clips advantages for policy\
    \ loss only; value target unchanged (MSE).\n  \"\"\"\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_pg_quadstep import PG_Stepped_AttnGap\n  from ray import tune\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  from defaults import default_calculate_gae\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          # Advantage clip threshold (policy loss only)\n          'adv_clip': tune.choice([8.0]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_Stepped_AttnGap(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n\n  def training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams):\n      observations = batch['observations']\n      actions = batch['actions']\n      rewards = batch['rewards']\n      dones = batch['dones']\n      old_values = batch['values']\n      final_values = batch['final_values']\n      initial_rnn_states = batch.get('initial_rnn_states', None)\n\n      advantages = default_calculate_gae(rewards,\
    \ old_values, dones, next_values=final_values)\n      returns = advantages + old_values\n\n      B, T = observations.shape[:2]\n\n      def loss_fn(p):\n          if initial_rnn_states is not None:\n              def process_env(env_idx):\n                  env_rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda x: jax.lax.stop_gradient(x[env_idx]), initial_rnn_states)\n                  def step_fn(carry, t):\n                      rnn_state = carry\n                      obs = observations[env_idx, t]\n                      prev_done = jnp.where(t > 0, dones[env_idx, t-1], False)\n                      logits_b, value_b, new_rnn_b = network.apply(\n                          p, obs[None, ...], jnp.array([prev_done]),\n                          jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[None, ...], rnn_state)\n                      )\n                      logits = logits_b[0]; value = value_b[0]\n                      new_rnn = jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[0], new_rnn_b)\n                      return new_rnn, (logits, value)\n\
    \                  _, (env_logits, env_values) = jax.lax.scan(step_fn, env_rnn_state, jnp.arange(T))\n                  return env_logits, env_values\n              all_logits, all_values = jax.vmap(process_env)(jnp.arange(B))\n          else:\n              obs_flat = observations.reshape(B * T, *observations.shape[2:])\n              dones_flat = dones.reshape(B * T)\n              logits_flat, values_flat, _ = network.apply(p, obs_flat, dones_flat, None)\n              all_logits = logits_flat.reshape(B, T, -1)\n              all_values = values_flat.reshape(B, T)\n\n          log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(all_logits, axis=-1)\n          idx = (jnp.arange(B)[:, None], jnp.arange(T), actions)\n          log_probs = log_probs_all[idx]\n\n          # Advantage clipping (policy loss only)\n          a = jnp.clip(advantages, -hparams['adv_clip'], hparams['adv_clip'])\n          policy_loss = -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(a) * log_probs)\n\n          # Value loss (MSE)\n        \
    \  value_loss = 0.5 * jnp.mean((all_values - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)) ** 2)\n\n          probs = jax.nn.softmax(all_logits, axis=-1)\n          entropy = -jnp.mean(jnp.sum(probs * log_probs_all, axis=-1))\n\n          return policy_loss + hparams['value_loss_coef'] * value_loss - hparams['entropy_coef'] * entropy\n\n      loss_and_grad = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn)\n      loss, grads = loss_and_grad(params)\n      updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n      params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n      return params, opt_state\n```\n\nIf either PPO sweep frees a slot and results look neutral, I’ll submit:\n- PG + advantage clipping (adv_clip=8.0), and\n- PPO‑Clip value_clip_range micro‑sweep (0.15, 0.20, 0.30)."
thinking_content: "**Exploring optimization methods**\n\nI'm considering potential tweaks, like the \"Attn temperature micro-sweep around 1.0.\" There are earlier results from the d6 attention temperature, but it's linked to an earlier architecture. The file for those results is available, and we can try micro-sweeping temperatures of 0.95, 1.0, and 1.05. It's been done before with performance at 86, but now maybe 1.0 is optimal at this stage. I also want to explore \"value loss coefficient micro-sweep for PPO\" and an \"entropy annealing\" schedule. However, I need to avoid complex changes that might affect comparability.**Analyzing training adjustments**\n\nI'm trying to figure out the impact of an entropy schedule, especially since earlier 'LR warmup/decay didn't help.' As a small change, I’m considering implementing the 'entropy_coef' with a factor from the step index, yet I'm unsure about the step number. The training step in 'train_single.py' is JIT compiled and doesn't pass that index.\
  \ Maybe I can track time across updates. The opt_state also tracks step counts, so I might compute the 'current_step' from it. But wrapping the optimizer could complicate things.**Understanding opt_state challenges**\n\nThe 'opt_state' provided is an opaque structure, possibly 'optax.OptState' with a '.count' for Adam. Retrieving 'opt_state' in a JAX function outside of JIT poses some challenges, but 'training_step_jit' does pass it in. I can access the '.count' field; however, I wonder if it's stable across shapes. For optax Adam, 'opt_state' contains a scalar count, which I can convert to float for my schedule. \n\nI need to ensure any modifications don't interfere with the default JIT. I plan to create an 'entropy_coef' schedule based on 'count' and 'total_updates,' but I’ll need to derive 'total_updates' from constants in 'train_single.py,' even though I can't access them directly inside the training step.**Exploring hyperparameter adjustments**\n\nI’m considering passing 'T_updates'\
  \ via hyperparameters, but the `_define_hyperparameters` function can’t compute 'total_timesteps' at runtime. The hyperparameters are passed to 'training_step' within Ray, and 'train_single.py' defines them. I could set 'total_updates_expected' to around 39,062 derived from a constant.\n\nHowever, during evaluation, timeouts can cut things short, which doesn't harm the count ratio too much. Still, I worry that while current SOTA is 94.3, micro-sweeps might lead to minimal improvements. I might also suggest network-level modifications, like \"gated attention weight dropout,\" though these could degrade performance. Plus, using \"Stopgrad\" from the previous script might help.**Assessing value head modifications**\n\nI’m thinking about how to manage the 'shared' heads for policy logits and value while considering using stop-gradient methods to prevent value gradients from interfering with policy improvements. Currently, the situation is complex as implementing stop-gradient could freeze\
  \ the value head’s training, which isn’t ideal.\n\nI need to find a way for the value loss gradient to affect only the value head without impacting the shared trunk. We could alternatively restructure calls to compute logits and values separately while applying stop-gradient to the trunk. Yet, this means editing the network, which isn’t straightforward without access to its internals. \n\nI’ll also be documenting my findings and actions while navigating through ongoing experiments, ensuring that I keep track of results and necessary updates in our storage system. I need to create new result files and plan future steps, particularly focusing on proposed experiments like the value loss coefficient micro-sweep.**Implementing proposed changes**\n\nIt seems I can't implement the separate optimizer for each head, so I'm considering advantage clipping as an alternative. This method might help manage outliers while keeping the value head stable. I'm planning to write the code for `submission_pg_adv_clip.py`\
  \ to ensure we can implement this easily later. \n\nNext, I need to write various files for results and proposed changes while ensuring I follow the proper structure. I’ll carefully include all required details and headers in each file and ensure the YAML formatting is correct. \n\nFor now, I'll gather my findings and prepare to share these updates as part of our ongoing projects!"
