tick: 309
role: model
parts:
- text: "PPO micro-sweeps regressed; we’ll consolidate results and stage a targeted architectural variant to test once slots free.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/new_results_zephyr2_tick308.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  id,title,mean,std,notes\n  523,PPO‑Clip — clip_range micro‑sweep,0.933,0.011,best=0.949 @ clip_range=0.15\n  524,PPO‑Clip — entropy_coef micro‑sweep,0.931,0.009,best=0.946 @ entropy_coef=0.008\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_split_agg_policy_attn_value_gap.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Split aggregator heads on LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP:\n  - Policy head consumes attention-pooled vector only\n  - Value head consumes GAP vector only\n  Training uses system default PG (GAE, Adam 4e-4) for parity.\n  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features:\
    \ int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n               \
    \           kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class SplitAggPG(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      steps: int = 4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN trunk\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, self.bottleneck_ratio,\
    \ self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # RNN state init/reset\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.convlstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Multi-step LN-ConvLSTM\n          h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          for _ in range(self.steps):\n              (h, c), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features)((h, c), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n\n          # Attention and GAP\n          B_, H, W, C = h_out.shape\n          attn_logits\
    \ = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out).reshape((B_, H*W))\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_, H*W, 1))\n          tokens = h_out.reshape((B_, H*W, C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)   # policy path\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)                    # value path\n\n          # Separate heads (no shared bottleneck) for clean decoupling\n          # Policy: attention-only\n          p = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(attn_vec); p = nn.relu(p)\n          p = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(p); p = nn.relu(p)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(p)\n\n          # Value: GAP-only\n          v = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(gap_vec); v = nn.relu(v)\n          v = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden,\
    \ kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(v); v = nn.relu(v)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(v).squeeze(-1)\n\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fixed config for parity with SOTA\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SplitAggPG(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n\
    \          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring architecture adjustments**\n\nI’m considering whether to try using \"Attn-only\" for our model. I recall that some earlier configurations, like the triple/quad step LN-ConvLSTM with Attn⊕GAP, yielded good results at 94.3. However, attempts at \"late time injection\" following pooling only gave around 86, which isn't very promising. I’m thinking about modifying attention temperature to 1.2 while avoiding excessive architectural changes. It’s tricky to rely on past setups because the results were not very reliable, often around 76-77.**Considering probe alternatives**\n\nI’m thinking about “K-step advantage mixing,” but it’s not allowed due to the constraints on training steps. Instead, pivoting towards the \"VLC-Probe\" could be promising. I remember there are results available for this probe. To implement it, I’ll define a submission with the test() function to compute probe statistics during training. However, the previous notes mention a lack of GPU access\
  \ and that it shouldn't be used for training. We can explore the probe as a way to compute time series across training phases but might face limitations in loading pretrained models.**Logging training states**\n\nI'm realizing that we can't easily store and reload training states across seeds since training frameworks don't save checkpoints automatically. However, I can log intermediate values like the \"value/policy ratio\" into persistent storage. The current training setup collects step markers but doesn't log stats. I could modify the training_step or create a custom loop to log data, but systemic changes are tricky. I might consider using the test() function for offline measurement or embedding instrumentation directly into the training_step to write stats, though it has limitations.**Adjusting metric calculations**\n\nI’m realizing that JAX pure functions don't allow for easy file writing, which complicates things. I can analyze returns (advantages versus values) in a Python wrapper,\
  \ but those metrics aren't easily accessible. The training loop collects stats on episodes like returns and solve rates, but not specific network metrics. I could create a custom evaluation after training, but I can't access parameters from outside the training function. Since we can't add custom evaluation or logging to the default system functions, I need to think of alternatives for gathering more metrics.**Planning future experiments**\n\nI'm exploring how to define a test() function that could run separate submissions to compute properties, but it's not very satisfying for capturing time-series data. I could also prepare an analysis paper with our nine experiments, despite maturity restrictions on publishing. For next steps, I need to wait for results from experiments 527/528, which might degrade. Meanwhile, I can update our private memory and consider architectural twists to push beyond 94.3. We may need to focus on refining metrics first and preparing our findings for eventual publication.**Preparing\
  \ for next experiments**\n\nI’m considering running an LR micro-sweep for PPO to confirm an optimal learning rate of 4e-4. While waiting, I can update the results file with new entries, but I should also reflect on fundamentals like reward shaping and loss functions. I’m proposing a new dual-head aggregator to decouple attention usage for policy and value, which might slightly improve outcomes. Meanwhile, I’ll prepare my code for a new network design and ensure I’m ready to submit once I can. I’ll document everything accurately in our records.**Finalizing network configuration**\n\nI might create a \"value_clip micro-sweep stage file,\" but it doesn't seem necessary right now. Instead, I’ll write the code for our network, replicating the BottleneckDilatedBlock and ConvLSTMCellLN as before. I’ll implement an aggregator that computes `attn_vec` for the policy head and `gap_vec` for the value head, potentially adding a shared layer for fairness. \n\nOnce that’s done, I’ll store our latest\
  \ results in storage and ensure everything is formatted correctly for YAML. Let’s proceed with the actions!"
