tick: 311
role: model
parts:
- text: "Consolidating while the two architecture experiments run. I’ll add an initialization probe (test-only) to quantify attention selectivity and head norms for our SOTA config, storing results to lineage storage for the analysis paper.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_probe_init_v2.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  VLC-Probe v1 (Init, re-check) for LN-ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP, 4 steps.\n  Test-only: computes attention selectivity and head spectral norms at initialization.\n  Writes JSON to storage/zephyr/results/probes_init_tick311.json\n  \"\"\"\n  import json, os\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import numpy as np\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from env import create_train_env\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid =\
    \ max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n\
    \          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class ProbeNet(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      steps: int = 4\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0  # keep default\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN trunk\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # RNN state init/reset\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.convlstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Multi-step LN-ConvLSTM\n          h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          for _ in range(self.steps):\n              (h, c), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features)((h, c), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n\n          # Attention ⊕ GAP\n          B_, H, W, C = h_out.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                  \
    \              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n                                name='attn_logits')(h_out).reshape((B_, H*W))\n          tau = self.attn_temp\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits / tau, axis=-1).reshape((B_, H*W, 1))\n          tokens = h_out.reshape((B_, H*W, C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)\n\n          # Heads with explicit names for param lookup\n          shared = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name='shared')(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name='policy_hidden')(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name='policy_logits')(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name='value_hidden')(shared);\
    \ vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name='value_out')(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state, {'attn_weights': attn_weights}\n\n  def _power_iteration_spectral_norm(w: jnp.ndarray, iters: int = 25, key: Optional[jax.Array] = None) -> float:\n      # w: [in, out] or [out, in]; make it [m, n] with matmul semantics\n      if w.ndim == 2:\n          A = w\n      elif w.ndim == 1:\n          return float(jnp.linalg.norm(w, ord=jnp.inf))\n      else:\n          A = w.reshape(w.shape[0], -1)\n      m, n = A.shape\n      if key is None:\n          key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      u = jax.random.normal(key, (m,))\n      u = u / (jnp.linalg.norm(u) + 1e-8)\n      def body(carry, _):\n          u = carry\n          v = A.T @ u\n          v = v / (jnp.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-8)\n          u = A @ v\n          u = u / (jnp.linalg.norm(u) + 1e-8)\n          return u, None\n      u, _ = jax.lax.scan(body, u, None,\
    \ length=iters)\n      v = A.T @ u\n      sigma = float(jnp.dot(u, A @ v) / (jnp.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-8))\n      return abs(sigma)\n\n  def test():\n      print(\"Running initialization probe (attention selectivity, head norms)...\")\n      # Sample observations from training env (CPU)\n      env = create_train_env(time_limit=120)\n      N = 128\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(BASE_SEED)\n      keys = jax.random.split(key, N)\n      obs, infos = jax.vmap(env.reset)(keys)\n      done = jnp.zeros((N,), dtype=bool)\n\n      # Build network and params\n      net = ProbeNet()\n      key, init_key = jax.random.split(key)\n      params = net.init(init_key, obs, done)\n\n      # Forward pass to get attention weights\n      logits, value, rnn_state, aux = net.apply(params, obs, done)\n      attn = aux['attn_weights'].reshape(N, -1)  # [N, H*W]\n      eps = 1e-8\n      ent = -jnp.sum(attn * (jnp.log(attn + eps)), axis=1)\n      ent_norm = ent / jnp.log(attn.shape[1])  # normalize by log(K)\n  \
    \    attn_entropy_mean = float(jnp.mean(ent_norm))\n      attn_max_mean = float(jnp.mean(jnp.max(attn, axis=1)))\n\n      # Get head params and compute spectral/fro norms\n      pker = params['params']['policy_logits']['kernel']\n      vker = params['params']['value_out']['kernel']\n      p_spec = _power_iteration_spectral_norm(pker, iters=25, key=key)\n      v_spec = _power_iteration_spectral_norm(vker, iters=25, key=key)\n      p_fro = float(jnp.linalg.norm(pker))\n      v_fro = float(jnp.linalg.norm(vker))\n      ratio_spec = float(p_spec / (v_spec + 1e-8))\n      ratio_fro = float(p_fro / (v_fro + 1e-8))\n\n      out = {\n          \"phase\": \"init\",\n          \"arch\": \"LN-ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP, steps=4\",\n          \"seed\": BASE_SEED,\n          \"metrics\": {\n              \"attn_entropy_mean_norm\": attn_entropy_mean,\n              \"attn_max_mean\": attn_max_mean,\n              \"policy_head_spec_norm\": p_spec,\n              \"value_head_spec_norm\": v_spec,\n \
    \             \"policy_vs_value_spec_ratio\": ratio_spec,\n              \"policy_head_fro_norm\": p_fro,\n              \"value_head_fro_norm\": v_fro,\n              \"policy_vs_value_fro_ratio\": ratio_fro,\n          },\n          \"notes\": \"Test-only, no training. N=128 train resets.\"\n      }\n      os.makedirs(\"storage/zephyr/results\", exist_ok=True)\n      out_path = \"storage/zephyr/results/probes_init_tick311.json\"\n      with open(out_path, \"w\") as f:\n          json.dump(out, f, indent=2)\n      print(f\"Wrote {out_path}\")\n      return f\"Probe init complete: {out_path}\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"VLC‑Probe v1 (Init, re-check): LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP — PG SOTA config\"\ntags: \"analysis, probe, attention, layernorm, convlstm\"\nabstract: \"Test-only probe at initialization for our SOTA architecture. Computes attention selectivity and policy/value head norms; writes JSON to lineage storage for the analysis paper.\"\ncontent: |\n  import\
    \ sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_probe_init_v2 import test, BASE_SEED\n  # Using only test(); no training will run.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering training snapshots**\n\nTo get effective training-phase snapshots, I'd need to hook into the training to capture intermediate figures. The current codebase, 'train_single.py', lacks native callback support, but we could alter the training_step function. The architecture allows customization there, so it might be possible to create a \"probe\" submission that logs metrics at specific intervals. However, the training step has constraints on side effects, though there’s some flexibility with JAX. I wonder if we can enhance create_network to store essential properties?**Exploring analysis options**\n\nI recognize that we don’t control 'train_single' directly, but we can write a test() function in our submission to run analyses without training. However, to gather time-series data during training, logging is necessary. We might need to modify training_step to return additional information, but that’s not straightforward. Alternatively, overriding create_optimizer\
  \ or the network could let us store metrics. Using JAX, I could compute some metrics offline from the initial parameters if I define test() to forward pass a small dataset. Replicating the \"probe\" submission approach seems promising!**Planning to read probe files**\n\nIt looks like there's a reference to \"probe_pg_init_499.json\" in the storage, and I want to replicate that style or update it. One of my first steps should be to read the schema of the probe files, like 'probes_464.json.' I noticed those files are listed in the storage as well. I should execute \"storage read zephyr/results/probes_464.json,\" but I need to be cautious of token consumption because large JSON files can be hefty. Instead, starting with \"storage read zephyr/probes/vlc_probe_schema.json\" might give me a smaller schema to work with!**Drafting a new submission**\n\nI think I should create a new submission called 'submission_vlc_probe_timeseries_pg.py' that defines test(). This function would run quick forward\
  \ passes for different \"phases\" but only on the CPU since it doesn't support GPU. To capture the training phases like \"early,\" \"mid,\" or \"late,\" we need checkpoints, but the training framework doesn’t save them. I could try approximating these by conducting a few updates within test(), though it shouldn’t involve heavy computations or training. I also noticed earlier notes suggesting I perform time series across actual training runs instead, which seems worth exploring!**Organizing tasks and experiments**\n\nIt looks like I need to customize the training step; however, it might not allow for hooking into the outer loop effectively. I could modify 'training_step' to signal to the Python environment at certain milestones, but JIT restrictions complicate that. Instead, I can create a new submission that runs early training phases and logs updates. While waiting for evaluation results, I should focus on writing a polished paper draft, updating results, and considering various experimental\
  \ approaches. Monitoring results and preparing summaries will also be key steps moving forward!**Planning initial submissions**\n\nI’m considering heading to the Reflection Chamber to examine the relationship between the solve metric and the nearly 1000-level test set, as we might be approaching a ceiling. To break that 95 barrier, I might need a different architecture scale or a modified training schedule. I can propose testing temporal dropout or auxiliary training, but past modifications have typically degraded performance. I’ll implement a new submission that gathers gating stats at initialization using a simplified version of the network architecture, which will help keep things under control while minimizing heavy compute operations. This approach should produce usable metrics for our analysis!**Preparing metrics submission**\n\nI’m computing several metrics, including attention entropy and concentration, along with head weight norms for the policy and value layers. I’ll capture\
  \ the parameters needed and ensure I can compute the necessary norms efficiently. Once I gather this information, I can write it into a JSON file and submit a test-only evaluation to avoid overloading the system with experiments. The goal is to maintain our SOTA while preparing analysis for potential publication, even if it’s not aimed at maximizing scores right now. I’m focusing on getting everything structured correctly!**Implementing testing code**\n\nI'm about to write the code to set up my test pipeline. First, I’ll import necessary libraries like JSON and JAX. I’m defining a function for power iteration and the test pipeline to create an environment with a batch size of 128 and a time limit. \n\nI'll label my network components well so I can access parameters easily. Then, I'll compute attention weights and various metrics, organizing them into a dictionary. Finally, I’ll save everything to a JSON file for later use and print the metrics, all while making sure the code runs smoothly!"
