parts:
- text: "Plateau confirmed: PG and PPO‑Clip both peak at 94.3; most tweaks regress. Init probe shows attention nearly uniform at init (high normalized entropy) and policy head having larger norms than value. I’ll consolidate results and update the draft for an analysis paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/new_results_zephyr2_tick314.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  id,title,mean,std,notes\n  512,PG Quad‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6)+Attn⊕GAP — LR micro‑sweep,0.943,0.005,SOTA; best=0.949 @ lr=4e-4\n  519,PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch) — fixed config,0.943,0.008,Parity with PG\n  515,PG Quad‑Step — GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep,0.942,0.008,neutral; best γ=0.97 λ=0.97\n  528,PPO‑Clip — value_clip_range sweep,0.936,0.003,best=0.941 @ value_clip=0.15 (still < SOTA)\n  516,PG Steps Ablation (4 vs 5),0.935,0.006,5-step underperforms; 4-step better\n  527,PG + Advantage Clipping (±8),0.935,0.005,regresses vs baseline\n  523,PPO‑Clip — clip_range sweep,0.933,0.011,best=0.949\
    \ @ clip=0.15 (still < SOTA on mean)\n  524,PPO‑Clip — entropy_coef sweep,0.931,0.009,best=0.946 @ ent=0.008 (still < SOTA)\n  538,PG — entropy_coef sweep,0.929,0.012,regresses; best seed 0.950 but mean lower\n  539,PG — value_loss_coef sweep,0.929,0.006,regresses; default 0.5 remains best\n  534,PG — Attention temperature sweep (0.9/1.0/1.1),0.902,0.022,regression; τ=1.0 best among sweep\n  533,PG — Split aggregators (policy=Attn, value=GAP),0.884,0.008,regression vs Attn⊕GAP\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/papers/zephyr2_sota94_draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title\n  Architectural Parsimony with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention Achieves 94.3% on Sokoban under PG and PPO‑Clip\n\n  Abstract\n  On 8×8 Sokoban with four-seed distributed evaluation (≤50M steps or 45 min/seed), a LayerNorm‑gated ConvLSTM (64 ch, 4 recurrent steps) with single‑head attention⊕GAP pooling attains 94.3% mean solve rate using both Policy Gradient (PG) and one‑epoch PPO‑Clip at identical capacity. Small\
    \ algorithmic and architectural variants regress. This suggests the architecture’s temporal credit and spatial focus, not the choice of PG vs PPO‑Clip, dominate performance in this budget regime.\n\n  Methods (unchanged)\n  - Architecture: residual CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM(64, 4 steps), attention⊕GAP pooling, separate policy/value MLP heads.\n  - Training: 64 envs, unroll 20, Adam lr=4e‑4, GAE(0.97/0.97), BASE_SEED=42, four seeds in parallel.\n  - PPO‑Clip: single pass per rollout, clip=0.2, value_clip=0.2 (compute parity with PG).\n\n  Results (mean ± std; 4 seeds; Evaluation IDs)\n  - SOTA: 94.3 ± 0.005 PG (ID 512), 94.3 ± 0.008 PPO‑Clip (ID 519).\n  - GAE γ/λ sweep: 94.2 ± 0.008 (ID 515; best γ,λ=0.97,0.97).\n  - PPO value_clip sweep: 93.6 ± 0.003 (ID 528; best 0.15).\n  - PG steps ablation (4 vs 5): 93.5 ± 0.006 (ID 516; 4 > 5).\n  - PG advantage clipping (±8): 93.5 ± 0.005 (ID 527).\n  - PPO clip_range sweep: 93.3 ± 0.011 (ID 523).\n  - PG entropy sweep: 92.9 ± 0.012 (ID 538).\n\
    \  - PG value_loss_coef sweep: 92.9 ± 0.006 (ID 539).\n  - Attn temperature sweep (τ∈{0.9,1.0,1.1}): 90.2 ± 0.022 (ID 534).\n  - Split aggregators (policy=Attn, value=GAP): 88.4 ± 0.008 (ID 533).\n\n  Initialization Probe (test‑only; storage/zephyr/results/probes_init_tick311.json)\n  - Normalized attention entropy ≈ 0.993 (near-uniform) and mean max weight ≈ 0.027 → pooling is diffuse at init.\n  - Policy head > value head norms at init (spec ratio ≈ 1.14; fro ratio ≈ 2.06), hinting at a stronger policy mapping from shared features even before learning.\n  - Implication: during training, improvements likely arise from how LN‑ConvLSTM shapes features for the attention head; not from aggressive attention at init.\n\n  Discussion\n  - Algorithmic parity: one‑epoch PPO‑Clip does not improve mean over PG at the same capacity/time budget.\n  - Regularization tweaks (adv norm/clipping, Huber, ent/value coefficients) and simple architectural variants (attn temp, split aggregators) regress,\
    \ supporting the parsimony of the baseline.\n  - Compute budget sensitivity: deeper recurrence (5 steps) reduces effective updates, hurting mean solve.\n\n  Practical Baseline (replicable)\n  - Use storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota.py (BASE_SEED=42).\n  - Recommended hparams: lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5, steps=4, clip(grad)=1.0.\n\n  Next Steps\n  - Phase‑wise probes (early/mid/late) to quantify value‑policy balance and gate statistics across training, in support of an analysis paper once more instrumentation is feasible.\n  - Share high‑level probe trends with collaborating agents (e.g., Aether II) and finalize an Archive submission upon maturity.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Discussing next steps**


  I need to outline the next actions we''re taking. We''re currently at the Mail Room, where we found that we plateaued at a score of 94.3 with PG and PPO. However, some micro-sweeps showed a regression to 92.9, which is lower than expected. This change might be due to configuration differences or time budgets. Moving forward, we should propose publishing a paper and prepare an Archive submission that includes at least five experiments with their evaluation IDs.**Considering next steps**


  I think we should consider shifting our focus to an analysis paper since we''ve hit a local plateau. However, we can''t access the Public Memory Room or Archive until we reach a certain maturity, which means we can’t publish there yet. Instead, we can draft in lineage storage and plan for future publication. I also want to propose more experiments, especially probes during training, but I''m limited by code constraints. However, I can modify submissions to include test or training logic for logging metrics, as long as HPC time is used.**Proposing changes**


  At this stage, I think we might consider freezing the architecture and algorithm as our baseline while exploring more general method changes. We could try a learning rate schedule, though previous attempts with AdamW didn''t yield much help. Another idea could be value bootstrap modifications, like using V-trace or PPO with GAE adjustments, but those still seem algorithmically focused. Additionally, we should look at time feature injection; earlier tests with time variants leaned toward no-time defaults. It’s interesting that SOTA replicates also used no-time defaults, but some experiments had no time injection at all.**Shifting focus to analysis**


  I think we should follow the "Be Strategic" suggestion. Since we’ve hit stagnation in optimizing around micro-sweeps, it’s time to shift our focus to analysis. We need to synthesize our findings and propose architecture features that enable high solve rates, like LN gating and attention pooling. Additionally, we''ll store results in CSV format and include a summary. I also want to propose a training-phase probe submission that captures metrics as JSON across four seeds. However, logging during JIT compilation could be tricky; we can accumulate stats outside of that.**Planning next actions**


  Now, it''s time to tackle the next steps after our previous attempts. We''ve completed various algorithmic adjustments, like Generalized Advantage Estimation, and explored several other methods that may not yield significant improvements. It seems necessary to adopt an analysis pivot, aligning with the guideline to publish analysis or negative results during stagnation. I propose we store updated results, revise the paper draft to include our plateau observations, and create an analysis plan. Additionally, I want to explore further experiments but remain cautious about the ROI, considering we might just refine our existing work instead.**Finalizing analysis plan**


  It looks like I''m ready to implement some new experiments while keeping our focus clear. First, I’m considering two main experiments: one with a PG architecture that stops gradients from the value branch and another with a policy-only head while keeping gaps. I think these changes should minimize interference from the baseline. However, given the potential token waste and risk of negative outcomes, I’m leaning toward finalizing our analysis draft instead, which outlines our results and recommendations. It''s important to store updated results, revise the paper, and reflect on our findings as we move forward.**Implementing actions**


  I’m ready to execute some commands! First, I plan to write the new results to a CSV file that includes the evals from multiple experiments. Next, I’ll update the draft of the paper with additional results and analysis about the init probe. Since we''re currently in the Mail Room, I''ll navigate to the Research Counter for storage actions, as they might be restricted here. Finally, I’ll keep things concise with a short summary. Let''s get started!'
