role: model
parts:
- text: "Plateau confirmed: PG/PPO best at 94.3; tweaks mostly regress. I’ll log the latest, stage a minimal interference-reduction variant (stop-gradient from value head to trunk), and record a plan for the next phase.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/new_results_zephyr2_tick320.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  id,title,mean,std,notes\n  512,PG Quad‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6)+Attn⊕GAP — LR micro‑sweep,0.943,0.005,SOTA; best=0.949 @ lr=4e-4\n  519,PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch) — fixed config,0.943,0.008,parity with PG\n  515,PG Quad‑Step — GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep,0.942,0.008,neutral; best γ=0.97 λ=0.97\n  546,PG — Grad Clip‑Norm 0.5,0.940,0.008,slightly below baseline\n  549,PPO‑Clip (combined cfg),0.937,0.010,clip=0.15, value_clip=0.15, ent=0.008\n  528,PPO‑Clip — value_clip sweep,0.936,0.003,best 0.15\n  516,PG Steps Ablation (4 vs 5),0.935,0.006,5-step underperforms; 4-step better\n  527,PG + Advantage Clipping (±8),0.935,0.005,regresses vs baseline\n  543,PG — Dual Value Heads (averaged),0.935,0.009,no\
    \ benefit\n  523,PPO‑Clip — clip_range sweep,0.933,0.011,best seed strong; mean below SOTA\n  538,PG — entropy sweep,0.929,0.012,regresses; best seed 0.950\n  539,PG — value_loss_coef sweep,0.929,0.006,default 0.5 still best\n  547,PG — Grad Clip‑Norm 2.0,0.924,0.010,regression\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_value_stopgrad.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  LN-ConvLSTM(d=6), 4-step with Attn⊕GAP; stop-gradient from value head into shared trunk\n  to reduce value-policy interference. Policy path backprops normally; value path sees\n  shared features with stop_gradient. Training uses default PG and Adam(4e-4), clip=1.0.\n  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n\
    \          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class PG_AttnGap_ValueStopGrad(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      steps: int = 4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN trunk\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # RNN init/reset\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.convlstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Multi-step LN-ConvLSTM\n          h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          for _ in range(self.steps):\n              (h, c), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features)((h, c), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n\n          # Attn ⊕ GAP\n          B_, H, W, C = h_out.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                             \
    \   kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out).reshape((B_, H*W))\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_, H*W, 1))\n          tokens = h_out.reshape((B_, H*W, C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)\n\n          # Shared MLP\n          shared = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n\n          # Policy path (normal backprop)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(ph)\n\n          # Value path with stop-gradient from shared\n          shared_v = jax.lax.stop_gradient(shared)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared_v); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1,\
    \ kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(vh).squeeze(-1)\n\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_AttnGap_ValueStopGrad(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n\
    \          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PG — Value Stop‑Grad to Trunk — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step, Attn⊕GAP\"\ntags: \"policy gradient, interference reduction, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Parsimony-preserving variant: stop-gradient from the value head into the shared trunk to reduce value-policy interference. Architecture and hparams match SOTA; optimizer is Adam+clip(1.0).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_value_stopgrad import (\n      _define_hyperparameters, create_network, create_optimizer, BASE_SEED\n  )\n  # Uses system defaults for training_step\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Zephyr II — Plan to\
    \ 100 ticks (analysis-first, parsimonious variants)\"\ncontent: |\n  Status:\n  - Best mean: 94.3 with PG and 1-epoch PPO-Clip on LN-ConvLSTM(64), steps=4, Attn⊕GAP.\n  - Most algorithmic/architectural tweaks regress; minor gains within noise.\n  - Init probe shows near-uniform attention and higher policy vs value head norms.\n\n  Next (0–100 ticks):\n  1) Minimal-interference trials: value stop-grad (submitted), policy-only clip-norm tuning bounded near 1.0 if any hint of gain.\n  2) Phase-wise probes: integrate lightweight logging (gate stats, attn selectivity, policy/value norm ratios) at early/mid/late markers within training loop (ensure negligible overhead).\n  3) Synthesis paper: finalize analysis draft (stored under papers/zephyr2_sota94_draft.md) with consolidated tables and probe results; publish post-maturity.\n  4) Collaboration: coordinate probe metrics with Aether lineage; exchange high-level findings (no configs/code).\n  Guardrails:\n  - Maintain parsimony; keep capacity\
    \ fixed (64 ch, steps=4).\n  - Two concurrent slots; avoid redundant micro-sweeps without theory.\n  - Archive-ready tables kept updated under results/.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating architectural options**


  I’m exploring the idea of an architecture minor focusing on “late time injection after pooling” and how other models have utilized different methods like "Attn⊕GAP" or "LN-ConvLSTM.” It seems that the existing results show mixed effectiveness with time injection; maybe raising ‘convlstm_features’ from 64 to 80 isn’t optimal based on past findings. I’m also considering aspects like LayerNorm vs GroupNorm. It makes sense to consolidate our results and possibly run one more test for better replicability.**Exploring estimation strategies**


  I’m considering the impact of generalized advantage estimation and gamma or lambda adjustments. It looks like the discount gamma of 0.99 with an entropy term of 0.01 might be worth a shot since we previously found 0.97 to be optimal. I wonder if using a KL penalty for policy gradients could lead to more stable improvements, even though it might be complex to implement. We can compute approximate KL using differences in log_probs for actions, rather than trying to get the full old distribution.**Refining KL estimation**


  I think I need to compute the KL divergence between the new and last logits for all actions, but the batch only remembers log_probs and values. This means I can’t get the full KL, but I can compute a "reverse KL" for taken actions with log_probs minus old_log_probs. Adding a mild penalty, like 0.01 times the squared difference, could help a bit. Given that previous attempts at tuning didn’t yield good returns, the next step might be to prepare a deeper analysis by running training-phase metrics every 3906 updates.**Defining metrics collection**


  I realize we can’t modify the system scripts, but we can define a custom ''training_step'' function. The existing loop in system/train_single collects metrics like "mean_return" and "solve_rate," which I can utilize. However, since I can’t intercept the printed step markers, I might create a custom network with mutable parameters to record metrics. Alternatively, a separate ''analyzer'' could run a lightweight ''test()'' to collect forward passes from random resets, allowing the sampling of metrics without heavy computations or GPU usage.**Planning next steps**


  I realize I can set up early, mid, and late phases in training by creating a function that samples metrics. However, the constraints of customizing training_step hinder easy I/O. I’m considering options like using a stop gradient from the value head to reduce interference with the policy. I also see possibilities for further tuning parameters, but they may yield negligible results. Since we have the capacity for one more experiment, I might propose a policy-only trunk experiment, while also focusing on documenting my findings.**Implementing stop gradient approach**


  I’m ready to write a new submission titled "submission_policy_value_stopgrad.py," reusing aspects of PG_Stepped_AttnGap while incorporating a stop gradient on the value path. I’ll use the same hyperparameters, along with the default training setup and optimizer. First, I''ll store the code, then submit it. It’s essential to ensure I handle YAML quoting correctly, especially with titles containing hyphens. After writing the code, I’ll summarize results and update the paper before creating a plan in my private memory.'
