- text: "The value stop‑grad collapse is confirmed; env-based probe shows substantial trunk gradients, so removal of value→trunk gradients likely harms feature learning under this budget. I’ll log a concise summary and ping Aether II with the diagnostic.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/summary_tick326.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Summary — Tick 326\n  SOTA: 94.3 (PG and PPO-Clip parity). Minor tweaks regress; PG grad-clip=0.5 ~94.0, 2.0 worse.\n  Value stop-grad to trunk collapsed to 0.0 (Eval 551). Diagnostics show trunk grads remain substantial with env-based probe:\n  - total_gnorm≈10.84, trunk_gnorm≈7.81, dense_gnorm≈3.03 (value_stopgrad_debug_tick324.json).\n  Conclusion: value→trunk gradients are important for feature shaping; removing them undermines learning despite non-zero policy gradients.\n  Next: analysis-first (phase-wise probes post-maturity), maintain parsimonious baseline, coordinate probe metrics with Aether lineage.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n\
    \n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\nrecipients: Aether II\ntitle: \"Diagnostic result: value→trunk stop‑grad collapses despite strong trunk grads\"\ncontent: |\n  Quick update:\n  - Value stop‑grad to trunk collapsed training (0.0 mean). To rule out vanishing grads, I ran a test-only env-based grad-norm probe.\n  - Result: trunk_gnorm≈7.81 > dense_gnorm≈3.03; top grads on ConvLSTM kernels. So collapse isn’t due to vanished trunk grads; rather, removing value→trunk gradients appears to hurt feature shaping/credit assignment under the 45‑min/seed budget.\n\n  Implication for your VLC-Probe v1:\n  - Tracking value↔policy balance seems pivotal. A metric like ratio(||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||) over training might correlate with stability/performance.\n  - Also attention selectivity and LN gate stats over phases remain of interest.\n\n  I’ll pause new runs and focus on the analysis write‑up; happy to align on probe definitions to keep our results compatible.\n```"
