parts:
- text: "Concluding experimental phase for now; shifting to analysis and write-up. I’ll add an outline and a small aggregator to consolidate results into a single table for the paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/aggregate_results.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Aggregate Zephyr II result CSVs into a single Markdown table.\n  Reads all new_results_zephyr2_tick*.csv and writes:\n   - storage/zephyr/results/zephyr2_aggregate.csv\n   - storage/zephyr/results/zephyr2_results_table.md\n  Note: Run locally in a Python environment if needed.\n  \"\"\"\n  import os, glob, csv\n\n  SRC_GLOB = \"storage/zephyr/results/new_results_zephyr2_tick*.csv\"\n  OUT_CSV = \"storage/zephyr/results/zephyr2_aggregate.csv\"\n  OUT_MD  = \"storage/zephyr/results/zephyr2_results_table.md\"\n\n  rows = {}\n  for path in sorted(glob.glob(SRC_GLOB)):\n    with open(path, \"r\") as f:\n      r = csv.DictReader(f)\n      for row in r:\n        key = row[\"\
    id\"]\n        rows[key] = row  # last write wins\n\n  # Write aggregate CSV\n  os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(OUT_CSV), exist_ok=True)\n  with open(OUT_CSV, \"w\", newline=\"\") as f:\n    cols = [\"id\", \"title\", \"mean\", \"std\", \"notes\"]\n    w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=cols)\n    w.writeheader()\n    for k in sorted(rows.keys()):\n      w.writerow({c: rows[k].get(c, \"\") for c in cols})\n\n  # Write Markdown table\n  lines = []\n  lines.append(\"| id | title | mean | std | notes |\")\n  lines.append(\"|---|---|---|---|---|\")\n  for k in sorted(rows.keys()):\n    r = rows[k]\n    lines.append(f\"| {r.get('id','')} | {r.get('title','')} | {r.get('mean','')} | {r.get('std','')} | {r.get('notes','').replace('|','/')} |\")\n  with open(OUT_MD, \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"\\n\".join(lines))\n\n  print(f\"Wrote {OUT_CSV} and {OUT_MD}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/papers/zephyr2_sota94_outline.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title (working)\n  Parsimony Wins: LN‑ConvLSTM\
    \ + Attention reaches 94.3% on Sokoban under PG and PPO‑Clip\n\n  1. Introduction\n  - Planning in Sokoban and tight compute budget constraints (≤50M steps or 45 min/seed).\n  - Goal: capacity‑matched comparison; architecture vs algorithmic tweaks.\n\n  2. Methods\n  - Environment/protocol.\n  - Architecture: residual CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM(64, 4 steps), Attn⊕GAP, separate heads.\n  - Training defaults: PG with GAE(0.97/0.97), Adam(4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0; PPO‑Clip single epoch parity.\n  - Evaluation: 4 seeds, mean solve as primary metric.\n\n  3. Results\n  - Baseline parity: PG and PPO‑Clip both 94.3% mean (IDs 512, 519).\n  - Micro‑sweeps regress or are neutral (GAE, entropy, value_coef, clip norms, PPO clips/entropy/LR).\n  - Recurrent depth: 5 steps underperforms vs 4 (less effective updates).\n  - Architectural variants (attention temp, split aggregators) regress; value stop‑grad collapses.\n  - Summary table (auto‑generated): storage/zephyr/results/zephyr2_results_table.md\n\n\
    \  4. Probes and Diagnostics\n  - Init attention selectivity near uniform; policy head norms > value head at init.\n  - Value stop‑grad diagnostic: trunk gradients stay large with real env observations, yet training collapses → value→trunk gradients likely essential for feature shaping.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  - Architecture dominates under this budget; algorithmic parity and parsimony principle.\n  - Practical recipe and replication notes.\n\n  6. Limitations and Future Work\n  - Phase‑wise probes; coordination with Aether lineage for shared metrics.\n  - Exploration beyond capacity‑matched scope after maturity.\n\n  Artifacts\n  - Code references: storage/zephyr/submissions/*.py\n  - Results: storage/zephyr/results/\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/README_tables.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  How to produce tables (offline/local run):\n  - Run aggregate_results.py in any Python environment with file access to storage/zephyr/results/.\n    This will merge all new_results_zephyr2_tick*.csv\
    \ into:\n      • zephyr2_aggregate.csv\n      • zephyr2_results_table.md (paste into the paper)\n  Files currently present include consolidated SOTA and ablations up to tick 326.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning a synthesis paper**


  I''m considering creating a paper for synthesis once everything matures. I think we can set a TODO and compute aggregated metrics as well.


  There are results available, and I''m thinking about producing a unified summary CSV by merging them. We also have the earlier results/make_table.py, which should help.


  I can run test functions and generate the analysis, but it seems training isn''t allowed. I''ll write a script for an aggregated table and set a default baseline module path for sharing.**Considering code collaboration rules**


  I''m thinking through the rules about code sharing and collaboration across agents. While shared storage is allowed, raw code sharing is forbidden—which is a bit tricky.


  I should avoid using shared storage for code since that can be seen as code sharing. It might be better to stay within lineage storage.


  For the next experiment, I can create a phase-wise probe plan, but they can''t change train_single.py. Designing a test function seems feasible, but not for heavy CPU tasks. I might propose committing a final "baseline config" file for reproduction and sharing purposes.**Drafting storage actions and plans**


  I see that we already have submission_sota.py ready, and it might be useful to check "Public Memory" after everything matures. Since the station environment expects specific commands, I''ll stick to minimal commentary and avoid executing unnecessary actions.


  I''ll write a manuscript outline, create a table of results, and store some figure code in Private Memory. For our steps, we need to go to research to access storage actions.


  I''ll write scripts to merge result CSVs, outline sections, and clarify the waiting plan for maturity. No more experiments for now, so I''ll implement these storage writes!**Generating analysis code**


  I need to produce some small code for analysis. The instructions say to use test() for running analysis without a GPU, though that might not be necessary. I want to keep my commentary concise throughout this process.


  I''ll make sure the final output includes the command lines and YAML. It''s all about being clear and straightforward, so let''s go ahead and create that code! I think I can keep it simple while still covering everything needed.'
