tick: 329
role: model
parts:
- text: "Shifting fully to analysis/write-up until maturity. I’ll scaffold the archive draft and a probe plan checklist, linking our consolidated results table.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/papers/zephyr2_archive_draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title\n  Parsimony Wins: LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention reaches 94.3% on Sokoban under PG and PPO‑Clip\n\n  Abstract\n  We study 8×8 Sokoban under a fixed 45‑min/seed (≤50M steps) budget with four‑seed evaluation. A capacity‑matched architecture—LayerNorm‑gated ConvLSTM (64 ch, 4 recurrent steps) with attention⊕GAP pooling—achieves 94.3% mean solve with both PG and one‑epoch PPO‑Clip. Algorithmic micro‑tuning (entropy, value_coef, clip norms) and simple architectural variants regress, supporting architectural parsimony. Diagnostics indicate value‑to‑trunk gradients are essential for stable feature learning. We release code and tables for replication.\n\n  1. Introduction\n  - Planning and compute constraints;\
    \ goal: compare algorithms at matched capacity; prioritize parsimony.\n\n  2. Methods\n  - Environment/protocol; capacity; architecture (residual CNN 32/64, LN‑ConvLSTM(64) 4‑step, attention⊕GAP, separate heads).\n  - Training defaults: PG with GAE(0.97/0.97), Adam(4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0; PPO‑Clip single pass per rollout.\n  - Evaluation: four seeds; mean solve rate.\n\n  3. Results\n  - Baseline parity: PG and PPO‑Clip both 94.3% mean (IDs 512, 519).\n  - Micro‑sweeps neutral/regressive (GAE, entropy, value_coef, grad‑clip; PPO clip/value_clip/entropy/LR).\n  - Recurrent depth: 5‑step < 4‑step (fewer effective updates).\n  - Architectural variants regress (attention temperature, split aggregators).\n  - Value→trunk stop‑grad collapses (0.0), despite strong trunk gradients in diagnostics.\n\n  4. Probes and Diagnostics\n  - Init attention nearly uniform; policy head norms > value head at init.\n  - Env‑based grad‑norms for stop‑grad variant: trunk_gnorm≈7.81, dense_gnorm≈3.03 → collapse\
    \ not due to vanishing trunk grads; value gradients likely shape trunk features under the budget.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  - Architecture dominates under time budget; parsimony principle.\n  - Practical recipe and replication notes.\n\n  6. Tables\n  - See storage/zephyr/results/zephyr2_results_table.md for consolidated results.\n\n  7. Artifacts\n  - Submissions: storage/zephyr/submissions/\n  - Results & diagnostics: storage/zephyr/results/\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/TODO_phase_probes.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Phase-wise Probe Plan (post-maturity)\n  Goals:\n  - Track value↔policy balance (R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||) over phases.\n  - Log attention selectivity (entropy, max weight) and LN gate stats across phases.\n  - Keep overhead negligible; trigger at fixed update indices.\n\n  Implementation notes:\n  - Phases at ~25%, ~60–70%, ~95% of NUM_UPDATES; log per seed.\n  - Compute grad norms on a single mini-batch snapshot per phase;\
    \ save JSON under storage/zephyr/results/probes_phase_*.json.\n  - Align metric definitions with Aether lineage to ensure comparability.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/README_next_steps.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Next Steps to Archive (Zephyr II)\n  - No new experiments until maturity unless a strong architectural hypothesis emerges.\n  - Maintain 94.3 baseline (IDs 512, 519) as reference.\n  - On maturity:\n    1) Run phase-wise probes for R_vp, attention entropy/max, LN gate stats.\n    2) Update results table and figures; finalize archive draft.\n    3) Submit to Archive with ≥5 experiments and ablation summaries (done), plus diagnostics.\n```"
