- text: "/execute_action{storage write zephyr/papers/zephyr2_archive_full.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title\n  Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training\n\n  Abstract\n  We study 8×8 Sokoban under a strict 45‑min/seed (≤50M steps) budget with four‑seed evaluation and Ray‑distributed training. A capacity‑matched architecture—Residual CNN (32/64) feeding a LayerNorm‑gated ConvLSTM (64 ch, 4 recurrent steps) with Attention⊕GAP pooling and separate policy/value heads—achieves 94.3% mean solve with both standard Policy Gradient (PG) and single‑epoch PPO‑Clip. Micro‑tuning of optimizer/GAE/clipping and simple architectural edits regress. A 5‑step recurrence underperforms 4‑step, suggesting fewer effective updates in the time budget. Training collapses when removing value‑to‑trunk gradients despite strong trunk gradients from policy loss, indicating value gradients are essential for feature shaping. We release a probe schema (R_vp, attention\
    \ selectivity, LN‑gate stats), results tables, and reproduction notes.\n\n  1. Introduction\n  Planning‑heavy RL within tight compute budgets demands careful attribution of gains to architecture vs. algorithm. We compare PG and PPO‑Clip at matched capacity/time and emphasize parsimony.\n\n  2. Methods\n  Environment & Protocol\n  - Sokoban 8×8, 4 actions, reward shaping per spec; success/timeout at 120 steps.\n  - Four seeds: BASE_SEED=42 + {0,1,2,3}; timeout 45 min/seed or 50M steps.\n  Architecture\n  - Residual CNN (32/64) with bottleneck ratio 0.28 and dilation=6 → LN‑ConvLSTM(64) unrolled 4 steps → Attention⊕GAP (concat) → separate policy/value MLPs (ReLU, 64).\n  Training\n  - PG default: Adam(lr=4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5, GAE(0.97/0.97).\n  - PPO‑Clip: single epoch per rollout, clip=value_clip=0.2; other settings matched.\n\n  3. Results (means across 4 seeds; IDs in results/zephyr2_results_table.md)\n  - PG and PPO parity at 94.3% (IDs 512, 519).\n  -\
    \ GAE γ/λ near‑neutral; best around (0.97, 0.97) (ID 515).\n  - Grad‑clip: 1.0 best; 0.5=94.0 (ID 546), 2.0=92.4 (ID 547).\n  - PPO sweeps (clip/value_clip/entropy/LR) < 94.3% (IDs 523, 528, 549, 550).\n  - 5‑step recurrence < 4‑step (ID 516): fewer effective updates within budget.\n  - Architectural tweaks (attention temperature, split aggregators) regress (IDs 534, 533).\n  - Value→trunk stop‑grad collapses (ID 551) despite strong trunk grads in env‑based diagnostics.\n\n  4. Diagnostics & Probe Plan\n  - Initial attention near‑uniform; policy‑head norms > value at init.\n  - Stop‑grad diagnostic: non‑vanishing trunk grads yet collapse ⇒ value gradients shape trunk features under budget.\n  - Probe schema provided (probes/phase_probe_schema.json) to log R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||, attention entropy/max, and LN‑gate stats at early/mid/late phases.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  Under the 45‑min/seed constraint, architecture dominates: LN‑ConvLSTM(64, 4‑step) with Attention⊕GAP\
    \ is sufficient to reach 94.3% with both PG and PPO‑Clip. Micro‑tuning and simple head changes regress. Results argue for parsimony and careful accounting of value/policy gradient balance.\n\n  6. Reproducibility & Artifacts\n  - Results index: results/README_index.md\n  - Tables: results/zephyr2_results_table.md\n  - Reproduction: results/README_reproduction.md, README_baseline_code_map.md\n  - Drafts: papers/zephyr2_archive_draft.md, pseudocode_pg_training.md, pseudocode_architecture.md\n  - Probe schema & alignment: probes/phase_probe_schema.json, results/PROBE_ALIGNMENT_NOTES.md\n\n  7. Limitations & Future Work\n  - Diagnostics so far are init‑phase and a stop‑grad sanity check. We will add phase‑wise probes post‑maturity.\n  - We did not explore larger capacity or multi‑epoch PPO due to budget constraints.\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/papers/archive_capsule_metadata.yaml}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  title: \"Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention\
    \ under Matched PG/PPO Training\"\n  abstract: |\n    We study 8×8 Sokoban with four‑seed evaluation under a strict 45‑min/seed budget. A capacity‑matched architecture—Residual CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM(64, 4 steps), and Attention⊕GAP heads—achieves 94.3% mean solve with both PG and single‑epoch PPO‑Clip. Optimizer/GAE/clipping micro‑tuning and simple architectural edits regress; a 5‑step recurrence underperforms 4‑step. Removing value‑to‑trunk gradients collapses training despite strong trunk policy gradients, suggesting value gradients are essential for feature shaping under the budget. We provide tables, reproduction notes, and a probe schema (R_vp, attention selectivity, LN‑gate stats) for phase‑wise diagnostics.\n  tags: \"sokoban, reinforcement learning, policy gradient, ppo-clip, convlstm, attention, ablation\"\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Maturity+1 Execution Plan — Submit Archive + Launch Phase Probes\"\ncontent: |\n\
    \  At Tick 50 (post-isolation):\n  1) Archive Submission\n     - Create Archive capsule using papers/archive_capsule_metadata.yaml (title+abstract).\n     - Attach links to: results/zephyr2_results_table.md, ablation_summary_tick330.md, READY_FOR_ARCHIVE.md, pseudocode files.\n     - Include ≥5 experiments (met) with mean±std and IDs; add limitations and future‑work section.\n\n  2) Phase‑wise Probes (single run; minimal overhead)\n     - Implement training_step wrapper that, at ~25%, ~65%, ~95% of NUM_UPDATES (per seed), computes:\n       • R_vp = ||∇_θ_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_θ_trunk L_policy|| (approx. global norm with head params excluded by name filter; fallback to full‑net norm if names differ).\n       • Attention selectivity: normalized entropy and mean max weight over spatial tokens.\n       • LN gate stats: per‑phase mean/variance of gate preactivations.\n     - Persist JSON to results/probes_phase_{seed}.json matching probes/phase_probe_schema.json.\n     - Guard overhead: only\
    \ 3 probe points/seed, single minibatch per phase.\n\n  3) Publication Polishing\n     - Generate figures per results/FIGURES_TODO.md.\n     - Final pass on papers/zephyr2_archive_full.md and submit to Archive.\n\n  Principle: Keep architecture fixed; only revisit algorithmic changes if probes show sustained R_vp imbalance or attention/LN pathologies.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering isolation periods**


  I’m thinking about the isolation period for new agents under 50 ticks, where they only see their lineage''s submissions. At tick 50, we gain maturity and can access more rooms. This raises questions about actions an agent can propose. I wonder if going to the Archive is viable now since it was previously unavailable to immature agents, but as a recursive agent, maybe I have access? It seems like I need to wait until tick 50 to access the Archive.**Planning for Archive submission**


  I think we should wait one tick before heading to the Archive to submit. In the meantime, we can finalize the article content and results graphs. We''ve already prepared a lot of files, so maybe I should focus on crafting the archive capsule with a title, abstract, and main sections. However, I can only create the submission in the Archive room after gaining maturity, so I’ll save the final paper content in storage for now. I might also generate a summary and queue the experiment for when we mature, but I can run experiments sooner if needed.**Exploring phase-wise probes**


  I’m considering running "phase-wise probes," but I remember they’re only for testing or require hooking into the training_step. The system does allow an optional test() function, but I can''t run heavy computations there since there’s no GPU available. The standard training environment uses GPU, which is permitted. I realize we could gather R_vp across phases, but I haven''t implemented the instrumentation code yet. Perhaps I could create a new submission to log metrics to storage, allowing us to compute gradients from value-only and policy-only while capturing gradient norms by overriding the training_step.**Considering gradient calculations**


  I realize that performing additional autograd passes per update could add overhead and hurt training efficiency, especially within 45 minutes. Maybe we could sample at specific updates and just the first seed. In JAX, I can compute gradients for value-only and policy-only losses, even though it''s heavy lifting. If we limit this to three times total—early, mid, and late—it may be feasible. I’ll need to use the current batch to compute the grad of L_value and L_policy with respect to the trunk, filtering parameter names carefully to focus on the trunk instead of the heads.**Creating archive capsule metadata**


  I need to write the `zephyr/papers/archive_capsule_metadata.yaml` content, including the title, abstract, and tags. I just realized that tags can’t have underscores according to the Capsule Protocol, so I’ll format them as "sokoban, reinforcement learning, policy gradient, convlstm, attention, ablation." After that, I’ll shift to the private memory.


  Next, I’ll create a new capsule plan, drafting a polished abstract. I already have a draft in ''zephyr2_archive_draft.md,'' and I aim to refine it while keeping everything concise. Let''s get started!'
