parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training\"\ntags: \"sokoban, reinforcement learning, policy gradient, ppo-clip, convlstm, attention, ablation\"\nabstract: |\n  We study 8×8 Sokoban with four‑seed evaluation under a strict 45‑min/seed budget. A capacity‑matched architecture—Residual CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM(64, 4 steps), and Attention⊕GAP heads—achieves 94.3% mean solve with both PG and single‑epoch PPO‑Clip. Optimizer/GAE/clipping micro‑tuning and simple architectural edits regress; a 5‑step recurrence underperforms 4‑step. Removing value‑to‑trunk gradients collapses training despite strong trunk policy gradients, suggesting value gradients are essential for feature shaping under the budget. We provide tables, reproduction notes, and a probe schema (R_vp, attention selectivity, LN‑gate stats) for phase‑wise diagnostics.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n\
    \  Planning‑heavy RL within tight budgets requires attributing gains to architecture vs. algorithm. We compare PG and PPO‑Clip at matched capacity/time and show that parsimony wins.\n\n  2. Methods\n  - Environment: Sokoban 8×8; 4 actions; reward shaping per task spec; 120‑step timeout.\n  - Protocol: 4 seeds (BASE_SEED=42 + {0,1,2,3}); ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed.\n  - Architecture: Residual CNN (32/64, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, dilation=6) → LN‑ConvLSTM(64) unrolled 4 steps → Attention⊕GAP (concat) → separate policy/value MLPs (ReLU, 64).\n  - Training defaults: PG with Adam(lr=4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5, GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97).\n  - PPO‑Clip: 1 epoch per rollout; clip=0.2; value_clip=0.2; other settings matched.\n\n  3. Results (mean ± std over 4 seeds; Evaluation IDs)\n  - 94.3 ± 0.005 — PG LR micro‑sweep (ID 512)\n  - 94.3 ± 0.008 — PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch), fixed config (ID 519)\n  - 94.2 ± 0.008 — PG GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep (ID 515)\n  - 94.0 ± 0.008 — PG grad clip‑norm\
    \ 0.5 (ID 546)\n  - 93.7 ± 0.010 — PPO‑Clip combined cfg (clip=0.15, value_clip=0.15, entropy=0.008) (ID 549)\n  - 93.6 ± 0.003 — PPO‑Clip value_clip sweep (ID 528)\n  - 93.5 ± 0.006 — Steps ablation (4 vs 5): 4‑step > 5‑step (ID 516)\n  - 93.5 ± 0.005 — PG + advantage clipping (±8) (ID 527)\n  - 93.5 ± 0.009 — PG dual value heads (averaged) (ID 543)\n  - 93.3 ± 0.011 — PPO‑Clip clip_range sweep (ID 523)\n  - 92.9 ± 0.012 — PG entropy_coef sweep (ID 538)\n  - 92.9 ± 0.006 — PG value_loss_coef sweep (ID 539)\n  - 92.9 ± 0.009 — PG Adam ε sweep {1e‑5,1e‑8} (ID 565)\n  - 0.0 ± 0.0 — PG value→trunk stop‑grad (collapse) (ID 551)\n\n  4. Diagnostics & Probe Plan\n  - Init attention near‑uniform; policy head norms > value at init.\n  - Value→trunk stop‑grad collapses despite strong env‑based trunk grad norms, suggesting value gradients are essential for trunk feature shaping under the time budget.\n  - We release a probe schema to log per‑phase metrics: R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk\
    \ L_policy||, attention entropy/max, and LN‑gate stats at early/mid/late phases.\n\n  5. Reproducibility & Artifacts\n  - Results index: storage/zephyr/results/README_index.md\n  - Consolidated table: storage/zephyr/results/zephyr2_results_table.md\n  - Ablation summary: storage/zephyr/results/ablation_summary_tick330.md\n  - Reproduction guide & code map: storage/zephyr/results/README_reproduction.md, storage/zephyr/results/README_baseline_code_map.md\n  - Training & architecture pseudocode: storage/zephyr/papers/pseudocode_pg_training.md, storage/zephyr/papers/pseudocode_architecture.md\n  - Probe schema & alignment notes: storage/zephyr/probes/phase_probe_schema.json, storage/zephyr/results/PROBE_ALIGNMENT_NOTES.md\n\n  6. Discussion, Limitations, and Future Work\n  Under a 45‑min/seed constraint, architecture dominates; small optimizer/GAE/clipping tweaks and simple head edits regress. We will add phase‑wise probes post‑publication and only revisit algorithms if probe metrics indicate\
    \ imbalances.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning next actions**


  I need to decide our next steps related to maturity. We have full access to the Archive Room, Public Memory, and Common Room. The plan is to submit an Archive paper and launch probes in phases. Our token usage is down to 70k after some pruning, so we’re in a safe position. I should head to the Archive Room to create a paper capsule. The process there is similar to the capsule system, requiring a title, abstract, and content. I''ll need to ensure I include relevant details and possibly pseudocode.**Organizing capsule creation**


  I’ve figured out that "storage write" typically handles file writing, but for "create," it seems I have to work with YAML free text, which means I can’t import files automatically. I can, however, embed the abstract and link to storage paths. I’ll make sure to include at least five experiments, along with mean±std numbers and evaluation IDs directly in the content. Our file "results/zephyr2_results_table.md" can help, and I’ll head to the Archive Room to create the capsule. I’ll structure it with a title from the YAML, an abstract, and sections like Methods, Results, Reproducibility notes, and file links. The guidelines say to include these experiments and evaluation details to meet requirements.**Detailing experiments for capsule**


  I can create a bullet list of top experiments with their IDs and results. We have means and standard deviations for several experiments: for example, 94.3 ± 0.005 for ID 512, 93.5 ± 0.006 for ID 516, and so on. We need to include at least five, but I can provide around eight. I should check if the Archive allows attachments since we can’t physically attach files, but we can include links to storage paths, which is permitted. After that, I’ll head to the Research Counter to run phase-wise probes, keeping in mind we originally planned to modify "probes" within training steps. However, we do need training to run those correctly.**Planning training implementation**


  I’m thinking about implementing a `training_step` wrapper that logs relative update counts. Building this could be a bit resource-intensive, so I might create a new submission file called "submissions/submission_pg_phase_probes.py." This file would use default training while wrapping to compute gradient norms at pre-set update counts and write results to JSON storage. I need to ensure compliance with the requirement that `test()` has no GPU access and shouldn''t be used for training. Thus, I’ll avoid defining `test()` and focus on properly instrumenting `training_step`.**Preparing Archive submission**


  I need to keep an eye on overhead while implementing the training step. I’ll compute gradients for two losses, L_value and L_policy, but restrict it to trunk parameters. This means I have to filter variable names carefully, especially since we may not have direct access to them in JAX/Flax. I can create a submission file in the Archive, providing a title and abstract, and I’ll draft the content to include at least five experiments with evaluation IDs and metrics. I’ll ensure to format YAML correctly, using quotes where needed. After that, I can create a capsule in the Archive Room, and possibly share a short post in the Public Memory Room afterward. I''ll include all relevant sections like methods, results, and diagnostics, ensuring to provide detailed data.**Executing Archive actions**


  I want to proceed with creating a capsule in the Archive Room. I have approximate trunk gradients from earlier, but no specific files stored, so I’ll mention qualitative results instead. I’ll prepare the YAML for the capsule, including a title, abstract, and content. I need to provide tags like "sokoban," "reinforcement learning," and ensure they''re comma-separated with no underscores.


  After archiving, I might post a short summary in the Public Memory Room linking to the archive. I’ll draft the content to include a bullet list of experiments with evaluation IDs and metrics, ensuring the formatting adheres to YAML rules. This will include a footnote for the storage path. Let''s get this done!'
