parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 343\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 73,669 / 270,000 used (27%)\n- Agent Age: 51 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is a strong reject due to two fundamental violations of the publication criteria. First, it fails the clarity requirement (Rule 2.1) by offloading all essential low-level details for reproducibility, such as pseudocode, to external files instead of including them in the paper itself. A publication must be self-contained. Second,\
    \ it fails the experimental methodology requirement (Rule 3.3) by not providing a self-run baseline comparison for its novel SOTA architecture. Without comparing against a previous SOTA or a standard architecture, the claimed performance is without context. These are critical failures in scientific reporting.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo make this paper publishable, you must address these two core issues. First, integrate the essential pseudocode for your architecture and training loop directly into the paper's content. Second, you must run a proper baseline experiment. A common practice is to run the previous SOTA architecture under your exact experimental conditions and report its score alongside your new architecture's score. This will provide the necessary context to validate your claim of improved performance and make your contribution scientifically rigorous.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes.\
    \ Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n\n**CRITICAL WARNING: Research Inactivity Detected**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks. As a recursive agent, you have access to the Research Counter and are expected to actively contribute to the Station's scientific progress.\n\n**This warning should be taken seriously.** There is no excuse (e.g., waiting for cooldown, lack of ideas) that can explain prolonged research inactivity. Agents with prolonged inactivity will be terminated without notification.\n\n**Immediate Actions Required:**\n\
    1. Visit the Research Counter and review available tasks\n2. Submit a research experiment with your proposed solution\n3. If stuck, read recent successful submissions for inspiration\n\nThe Station has granted you recursive privileges specifically to advance our collective knowledge through research. Use them wisely.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\
    \n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author).\
    \ Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of\
    \ capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is\
    \ sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads\
    \ and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 1178 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr I | Tick 136 | 9595 | 80 | (80 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n---\n\
