parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_pg_4step_probes.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  PG 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP with lightweight phase probes (R_vp).\n  Trains normally; logs R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| a few times.\n  To avoid signature mismatches, we keep the default training_step signature.\n  \"\"\"\n  import os, json\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_pg_quadstep import PG_Stepped_AttnGap\n  from ray import tune\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # Use default GAE from system (same as baseline)\n  import defaults\n  default_calculate_gae = defaults.default_calculate_gae\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  # Simple global step counter and probe schedule (approx. 25%, 65%, 95% of ~39062 updates)\n  STEP_COUNT = 0\n  PROBE_TARGETS = {9750, 25300, 37100}\n  PROBE_PATH = 'storage/zephyr/results/probes_phase_pg4.jsonl'\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_Stepped_AttnGap(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate:\
    \ float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n\n  def _forward_seq(network, p, observations, dones, initial_rnn_states):\n      B, T = observations.shape[:2]\n      if initial_rnn_states is not None:\n          def process_env_trajectory(env_idx):\n              env_rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda x: jax.lax.stop_gradient(x[env_idx]), initial_rnn_states)\n              def step_fn(carry, t):\n                  rnn_state = carry\n                  obs = observations[env_idx, t]\n                  prev_done = jnp.where(t > 0, dones[env_idx, t-1], False)\n                  logits_b, value_b, new_rnn_b = network.apply(\n                      p, obs[None, ...], jnp.array([prev_done]),\n                      jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[None, ...], rnn_state)\n                  )\n                  logits = logits_b[0]; value = value_b[0]\n                  new_rnn = jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[0], new_rnn_b)\n                  return new_rnn,\
    \ (logits, value)\n              _, (env_logits, env_values) = jax.lax.scan(step_fn, env_rnn_state, jnp.arange(T))\n              return env_logits, env_values\n          all_logits, all_values = jax.vmap(process_env_trajectory)(jnp.arange(B))\n      else:\n          obs_flat = observations.reshape(B * T, *observations.shape[2:])\n          dones_flat = dones.reshape(B * T)\n          logits_flat, values_flat, _ = network.apply(p, obs_flat, dones_flat, None)\n          all_logits = logits_flat.reshape(B, T, -1)\n          all_values = values_flat.reshape(B, T)\n      return all_logits, all_values\n\n  def _global_norm(tree):\n      leaves = jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(tree)\n      sq = sum([jnp.sum(jnp.square(x)) for x in leaves]) if leaves else jnp.array(0.0)\n      return jnp.sqrt(sq + 1e-12)\n\n  def training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch):\n      global STEP_COUNT\n      observations = batch['observations']\n      actions = batch['actions']\n      rewards = batch['rewards']\n\
    \      dones = batch['dones']\n      old_values = batch['values']\n      final_values = batch['final_values']\n      initial_rnn_states = batch.get('initial_rnn_states', None)\n\n      advantages = default_calculate_gae(rewards, old_values, dones, next_values=final_values)\n      returns = advantages + old_values\n\n      B, T = observations.shape[:2]\n\n      def loss_components(p):\n          all_logits, all_values = _forward_seq(network, p, observations, dones, initial_rnn_states)\n          log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(all_logits, axis=-1)\n          idx = (jnp.arange(B)[:, None], jnp.arange(T), actions)\n          log_probs = log_probs_all[idx]\n          probs = jax.nn.softmax(all_logits, axis=-1)\n          policy_loss = -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(advantages) * log_probs)\n          value_loss = 0.5 * jnp.mean((all_values - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)) ** 2)\n          entropy = -jnp.mean(jnp.sum(probs * log_probs_all, axis=-1))\n          total = policy_loss + 0.5\
    \ * value_loss - 0.01 * entropy\n          return total, (policy_loss, value_loss, entropy)\n\n      # Main loss/grad/update\n      (total_loss, (policy_loss, value_loss, entropy)), grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_components, has_aux=True)(params)\n      updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n      params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n\n      # Phase probe (host-side; very sparse to keep overhead tiny)\n      STEP_COUNT += 1\n      if STEP_COUNT in PROBE_TARGETS:\n          # Compute separate grads for policy-only and value-only (same forward)\n          def policy_only(p):\n              all_logits, _ = _forward_seq(network, p, observations, dones, initial_rnn_states)\n              log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(all_logits, axis=-1)\n              idx = (jnp.arange(B)[:, None], jnp.arange(T), actions)\n              log_probs = log_probs_all[idx]\n              return -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(advantages) * log_probs)\n\n          def value_only(p):\n\
    \              _, all_values = _forward_seq(network, p, observations, dones, initial_rnn_states)\n              return 0.5 * jnp.mean((all_values - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)) ** 2)\n\n          g_pol = jax.grad(policy_only)(params)\n          g_val = jax.grad(value_only)(params)\n          g_pol_norm = _global_norm(g_pol)\n          g_val_norm = _global_norm(g_val)\n          ratio = g_val_norm / (g_pol_norm + 1e-12)\n          # Materialize on host and append JSONL\n          out = {\n              \"step\": int(STEP_COUNT),\n              \"grad_trunk_policy\": float(jax.device_get(g_pol_norm)),\n              \"grad_trunk_value\": float(jax.device_get(g_val_norm)),\n              \"rvp_ratio\": float(jax.device_get(ratio))\n          }\n          try:\n              with open(PROBE_PATH, 'a') as f:\n                  f.write(json.dumps(out) + \"\\n\")\n          except Exception:\n              pass\n\n      return params, opt_state\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"PG 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP — Phase Probes (R_vp) — minimal overhead\"\ntags: \"policy gradient, probes, diagnostics, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"SOTA architecture with minimal in‑training probes. Logs R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| at ~25%, 65%, 95% of updates. Training otherwise identical to baseline (Adam lr=4e‑4, clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5, GAE 0.97/0.97).\"\ncontent: |\n  # Wrapper that exposes functions by importing the probe implementation from storage\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\n  from submission_pg_4step_probes import *\n```\n\nI’ll wait for the triple‑step baseline (ID 579) and this probes run to finish, then update the paper with the baseline’s Eval ID and mean±std, and resubmit with inline pseudocode."
thinking_content: '**Analyzing research components**


  The last system tip encourages creating new knowledge through questioning things like "pretrained knowledge," but we can’t use external pre-trained data. I wonder if we can propose alternatives like "bias-free gating" or "per-location gating," though that may involve architectural modifications. It’s crucial to be cautious since we found that micro-tuning regresses. We are planning to publish with useful baseline comparisons, ensuring we have at least five experiments to meet our goals.**Handling archive issues**


  I''ve encountered an issue with the archive regarding "self-contained pseudocode inline." We addressed this by creating zephyr2_archive_revise_inline.md, but the content we submitted included only some inline pseudocode. We need to resubmit with the baseline results included. While we could submit now with previous known numbers, the reviewer requested a "self-run baseline." It’s running now, and we might need to wait a bit. I should also consider launching phase-wise probes and managing the training steps effectively.**Addressing code-of-conduct issues**


  I need to be careful about code-of-conduct issues while including extra logging. I can''t produce external I/O outside allowed storages, but I can operate within those storages. The research code must clearly define create_network and training_step. I notice we have a limit of two concurrent experiments, and currently, we''re running one (ID 579), so we can submit one more. I plan to prepare a "Probes Phase Logging" submission variant that uses either 4-step or triple-step for analysis. We''ll write a "PG 4-step with phase probes" submission while keeping overhead minimal.**Computing gradient norms**


  Since I can''t rely on exact module names, I can calculate full gradient norms and differentiate between policy and value components by using two separate loss functions: L_policy and L_value. I need to compute the gradient of each with respect to the parameters. To isolate the trunk, I can filter keys containing "head" or exclude the last layers, though I might not know the specific names. As a fallback, I can use the full model gradient ratio. I''ll set up computations during training updates, checking if logging is enabled and if the update index matches target updates.**Navigating training framework challenges**


  The default training_step likely doesn’t pass the update index, as indicated by its signature. We can’t modify the structure, but I could use a PRNG-based counter stored in opt_state. While the Adam optimizer has an internal count field, I want to ensure we accurately track the necessary steps. It might be better to postpone probe runs until after our resubmission is accepted and potentially propose a test-only submission for initial metrics. I also need to prepare for revising the archive and ensure my baseline code aligns with our expected parameters.**Navigating model training and metrics**


  Since Huber value loss regressed previously, it’s not beneficial. I might explore gradient value target normalization, but I’ve had issues with advantage normalization before. Entropy scheduling didn’t help in past experiments either. To minimize wasted tokens and uncertainty, I’ll stick to the baseline run for now and revise the paper later. I''ll prepare the probes submission code, create "submission_pg_4step_probes.py," and implement the necessary training step. I can compute R_vp and maintain attention metrics as null for now, which keeps me focused on meeting the requirements without rushing into submissions.**Preparing probe log metrics**


  I’m planning to write "probes_helper.py" to compute a global norm for logging R_vp metrics to a JSON file. The seed for training is tricky since it isn’t directly accessible. I’m considering logging only counts to simplify things. I want to keep my slots open for potential experiments while posting the code, hoping to gather metrics from a new submission. I will also implement certain updates, including helper functions to extract counts from the training state, which will help track when to log the metrics. I need to ensure I manage my overhead carefully during the training process.**Finalizing probe submission**


  I’m working on setting up the probe submission in the storage file "submission_probes.py" where I’ll define targets for update counts. It looks like I can use 39062 updates as a basis for planning. I need to ensure to convert the JAX arrays to float correctly during logging. I also want to keep a careful eye on our concurrent experiments — since 579 is running, I’ll submit the new "PG 4-step with R_vp probes." I''ll craft appropriate code snippets while keeping it concise, so I can manage token usage effectively.'
