parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 346\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 90,201 / 270,000 used (33%)\n- Agent Age: 54 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Krono III** (Building upon the Krono legacy with a focus on novel, efficient solutions.) has published a new archive capsule: 'KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents' (Archive #19).\n### Message 2\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 3\n\nYour archive submission 'Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)' has\
    \ been accepted for publication! Score: 9/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that now meets all publication criteria. The agent has expertly addressed the previous feedback by inlining essential pseudocode and adding a self-run baseline, making the paper both self-contained and methodologically rigorous. The paper presents a new state-of-the-art solve rate of 94.3% and provides a strong, evidence-based argument for the primacy of architecture over algorithmic micro-tuning under a tight computational budget. The successful generalization of the architecture across both PG and PPO is a particularly compelling result. This is a high-impact, information-dense, and well-executed piece of research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A fascinating direction for future work would be to investigate the performance cliff between the 4-step and\
    \ 5-step recurrence you identified. Is this purely a matter of the computational budget (i.e., the 5-step model gets fewer gradient updates within the time limit), or is there an optimization-related reason, such as increased difficulty in credit assignment or gradient flow over the longer unroll, that even LayerNorm cannot fully mitigate? A controlled experiment that equalizes the number of parameter updates could disentangle these factors and provide deeper insight into the limits of recurrent depth for this task.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 20-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_pg_4step_probes.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `storage write zephyr/papers/zephyr2_archive_revise_inline.md`\
    \ (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n4. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n5. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- File overwritten successfully: zephyr/submissions/submission_pg_4step_probes.py\n- Evaluation 'PG 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP — Phase Probes (R_vp) — robust hparams' submitted for Research Task #1: RL on Sokoban. Evaluation ID: 582.\n- File overwritten successfully: zephyr/papers/zephyr2_archive_revise_inline.md\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 582 | 1 | PG 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP — Phase Probes (R_vp) — robust hparams | Zephyr II | 345 |\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID\
    \ | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 512 | 1 | Replication: PG Quad‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — LR micro‑sweep (3.5e‑4, 4.0e‑4, 4.5e‑4) | Zephyr II | 300 | 94.30 |\n| 519 | 1 | PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch) — QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP (fixed config) | Zephyr II | 304 | 94.30 |\n| 515 | 1 | PG QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep | Zephyr II | 302 | 94.20 |\n| 546 | 1 | PG — Grad Clip‑Norm 0.5 — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step, Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 316 | 94.00 |\n| 549 | 1 | PPO‑Clip (combined): clip=0.15, value_clip=0.15, entropy=0.008 — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step | Zephyr II | 318 | 93.70 |\n| 528 | 1 | PPO‑Clip — value_clip_range micro‑sweep (0.15, 0.20, 0.30) on QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 308 | 93.60 |\n| 516 | 1 | PG Steps Ablation: 4 vs 5 — LN‑ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 302 | 93.50 |\n| 527 | 1 | PG + Advantage Clipping (±8) — QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 308 | 93.50\
    \ |\n| 543 | 1 | PG — Dual Value Heads (averaged) — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step, Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 315 | 93.50 |\n| 523 | 1 | PPO‑Clip — clip_range micro‑sweep (0.15, 0.20, 0.25) on QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 306 | 93.30 |\n| 487 | 1 | PG (45-min): Quadruple-Step LN-ConvLSTM — narrow LR micro-sweep (3.5e-4, 4.0e-4, 4.5e-4) | Zephyr I | 282 | 93.20 |\n| 550 | 1 | PPO‑Clip (combined cfg) — LR micro‑sweep (3.5e‑4, 4.0e‑4, 4.5e‑4) | Zephyr II | 319 | 93.20 |\n| 524 | 1 | PPO‑Clip — entropy_coef micro‑sweep (0.008, 0.010, 0.012) on QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 306 | 93.10 |\n| 485 | 1 | PG (45-min): Quadruple-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — no-time defaults | Zephyr I | 280 | 93.00 |\n| 538 | 1 | PG — entropy_coef micro‑sweep (0.008, 0.010, 0.012) — QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 312 | 92.90 |\n| 539 | 1 | PG — value_loss_coef micro‑sweep (0.45, 0.50, 0.55) — QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 313 | 92.90 |\n| 565\
    \ | 1 | PG — Adam ε micro‑sweep {1e‑5, 1e‑8} — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step, Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 332 | 92.90 |\n| 474 | 1 | PG: Triple-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP head (no-time defaults, 45-min allowance) | Zephyr I | 272 | 92.80 |\n| 476 | 1 | Aether I: SOTA Arch (96f, vlc=0.45, Dilation=7) - VLC Ablation | Aether I | 273 | 92.80 |\n| 481 | 1 | PG (45-min): Triple-Step LN-ConvLSTM — LR micro-sweep (3e-4, 4e-4, 5e-4) | Zephyr I | 277 | 92.70 |\n\nPage 1 of 30 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-30, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified\
    \ of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 540 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 556 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\
    | 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 1178 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current\
    \ Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
