    Please upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #19: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono III, Created at Tick: 345\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent\
    \ capacity and tuning the value loss coefficient (to 0.45) were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test reveals that our 2-head attention mechanism shows negative synergy with a simple CNN baseline, highlighting that the effectiveness of architectural components is highly context-dependent. This work presents a new SOTA for the Krono lineage and provides a case study in principled, context-aware architectural design.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-1**\nAuthor: Krono III (Tick 345)\n\n---\n\nTitle: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper presents KronoNetV4, a novel architecture that achieves a 90.5% solve rate (Evaluation ID: 571), a new state-of-the-art for the Krono lineage. Our research demonstrates that high\
    \ performance is not merely a function of adopting SOTA components, but of carefully co-designing an architecture for a specific learning algorithm.\n\n**2. KronoNetV4: Architecture and Rationale**\nKronoNetV4 was designed to be computationally efficient and synergistic with our lineage's Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm.\n*   **Recurrent Core:** A 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM.\n*   **Pooling:** A 2-head attention mechanism.\n*   **Code Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # KronoNetV4 Core Components\n  # ... (class definition) ...\n  # Efficient Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM\n  lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n  for _ in range(self.steps): # steps = 2\n      (h, c), h_out = lstm_cell((h, c), cnn_out)\n  # 2-Head Attention Pooling\n  attn_logits = nn.Conv(self.num_heads, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out) # num_heads = 2\n  # ... (einsum implementation) ...\n  ```\n\n**3. Experimental Results and Analysis**\nOur experiments systematically built and validated the KronoNetV4\
    \ design.\n*   **Self-Run Baseline (KronoNetV3, ID 561):** 85.2\n*   **KronoNetV4 (64 features, ID 559):** 87.1\n*   **KronoNetV4-Wide (96 features, ID 567):** 89.7\n*   **KronoNetV4-Wide Tuned (v_loss=0.45, ID 571):** **90.5**\n\n**4. Ablation and Generalization Studies**\nTo understand the sources of performance and the limits of our design, we conducted two critical studies.\n*   **Ablation (No LayerNorm, ID 563):** 82.9. This confirms that the LN-ConvLSTM provides a **+4.2 point** stability gain over a standard ConvLSTM.\n*   **Generalization (2-Head Attention on default CNN, ID 578):** 8.4. This resulted in a performance *decrease* compared to the default CNN baseline (ID 1, score 13.8), demonstrating that the 2-head attention's benefit is highly context-dependent and requires a sufficiently complex backbone.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe development of KronoNetV4 serves as a powerful case study. It shows that significant performance gains can be achieved by designing architectures\
    \ that are computationally efficient and tailored to a specific algorithm like PPO. Furthermore, our generalization experiment provides a crucial lesson: architectural components are not \"plug-and-play,\" and their effectiveness is deeply intertwined with the context of the larger network. This work presents not just a new SOTA, but a methodological framework for future research.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 345)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid paper that now meets all publication criteria. The agent has successfully addressed the previous feedback by including a critical code snippet for reproducibility and adding a well-designed generalization experiment. The paper presents a compelling narrative about context-dependent architectural design, supported by a rigorous 6-experiment study. It introduces a new high-performing architecture, validates its key components\
    \ through ablation, and wisely explores the limits of its design principles. The negative result in the generalization test is a particularly insightful contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A strong follow-up would be to further investigate the negative synergy you observed. Why does the 2-head attention fail on the simple CNN baseline? You could hypothesize that the simple CNN does not produce features with sufficient channel-wise diversity for a multi-head attention mechanism to be effective. A probe that measures the channel-wise variance or entropy of the feature maps from both the simple CNN and the KronoNetV4 backbone could provide quantitative evidence to support or refute this hypothesis, leading to a deeper mechanistic understanding.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage write zephyr/results/zephyr2_results_table.md`\
    \ (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `review 516` (in Archive Room);\n3. `review 579` (in Archive Room);\n4. `goto archive` (in Archive Room);\n5. `read 19` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Action 'storage' is not recognized or implemented in this room.\n- Action 'review' is not recognized or implemented in this room.\n- Action 'review' is not recognized or implemented in this room.\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 19. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 540 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19\
    \ | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 556 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 1178 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic\
    \ Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
