parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Test‑only probes: 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP — Init R_vp (inline)\"\ntags: \"diagnostics, probes, policy gradient, convlstm, attention, test-only\"\nabstract: \"CPU‑only, test‑mode diagnostic. Computes initial R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| on a lightweight synthetic batch for the 4‑step SOTA architecture. No training runs; logs JSON and prints metrics.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Test-only probes for 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP.\n  Computes initial R_vp on a small synthetic batch; no training.\n  \"\"\"\n  import json, os\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import numpy as np\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_pg_quadstep import PG_Stepped_AttnGap\n\n  PROBE_PATH = 'storage/zephyr/results/probes_init_pg4.json'\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_Stepped_AttnGap(\n          cnn_features_1=32,\n \
    \         cnn_features_2=64,\n          convlstm_features=64,\n          dilation=6,\n          bottleneck_ratio=0.28,\n          head_hidden=64,\n          steps=4,\n      )\n\n  def _forward(network, params, observations, dones):\n      B, T = observations.shape[:2]\n      obs_flat = observations.reshape(B*T, *observations.shape[2:])\n      dones_flat = dones.reshape(B*T)\n      logits_flat, values_flat, _ = network.apply(params, obs_flat, dones_flat, None)\n      return logits_flat.reshape(B,T,-1), values_flat.reshape(B,T)\n\n  def _global_norm(tree):\n      leaves = jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(tree)\n      if not leaves: return jnp.array(0.0)\n      return jnp.sqrt(sum([jnp.sum(jnp.square(x)) for x in leaves]) + 1e-12)\n\n  def test():\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n      B, T = 8, 4\n      # Synthetic batch (CPU-friendly)\n      observations = jax.random.normal(key, (B, T, 8, 8, 8)) * 0.1\n      dones = jnp.zeros((B, T), dtype=bool)\n      actions = jax.random.randint(key, (B,\
    \ T), 0, 4)\n      adv = jax.random.normal(key, (B, T))\n      ret = adv  # simple proxy so both objectives have signal\n\n      net = create_network({})\n      # Initialize params\n      params = net.init(key, observations.reshape(B*T,8,8,8), dones.reshape(B*T), None)['params']\n\n      def loss_policy(p):\n          logits, _ = _forward(net, p, observations, dones)\n          log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n          idx = (jnp.arange(B)[:,None], jnp.arange(T), actions)\n          log_probs = log_probs_all[idx]\n          return -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(adv) * log_probs)\n\n      def loss_value(p):\n          _, values = _forward(net, p, observations, dones)\n          return 0.5 * jnp.mean((values - jax.lax.stop_gradient(ret))**2)\n\n      g_pol = jax.grad(loss_policy)(params)\n      g_val = jax.grad(loss_value)(params)\n      g_pol_n = float(_global_norm(g_pol))\n      g_val_n = float(_global_norm(g_val))\n      rvp = g_val_n / (g_pol_n + 1e-12)\n\n  \
    \    out = {\"mode\":\"init\",\"arch\":\"4step_64ch\",\"grad_trunk_policy\":g_pol_n,\"grad_trunk_value\":g_val_n,\"rvp_ratio\":rvp}\n      try:\n          with open(PROBE_PATH, 'w') as f:\n              json.dump(out, f)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n      print(json.dumps(out))\n      return \"init probe complete\"\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Test‑only probes: 5‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP (vlc=0.55) — Init R_vp (inline)\"\ntags: \"diagnostics, probes, policy gradient, convlstm, attention, test-only\"\nabstract: \"CPU‑only, test‑mode diagnostic. Computes initial R_vp on a lightweight synthetic batch for the 5‑step (64ch) architecture with value loss coefficient context (0.55). No training runs; logs JSON and prints metrics.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Test-only probes for 5-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP (64ch).\n  Computes initial R_vp on a small synthetic batch; no training.\n  \"\"\"\n  import json, os\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\
    \  import numpy as np\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_pg_quadstep import PG_Stepped_AttnGap\n\n  PROBE_PATH = 'storage/zephyr/results/probes_init_pg5.json'\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_Stepped_AttnGap(\n          cnn_features_1=32,\n          cnn_features_2=64,\n          convlstm_features=64,\n          dilation=6,\n          bottleneck_ratio=0.28,\n          head_hidden=64,\n          steps=5,\n      )\n\n  def _forward(network, params, observations, dones):\n      B, T = observations.shape[:2]\n      obs_flat = observations.reshape(B*T, *observations.shape[2:])\n      dones_flat = dones.reshape(B*T)\n      logits_flat, values_flat, _ = network.apply(params, obs_flat, dones_flat, None)\n      return logits_flat.reshape(B,T,-1), values_flat.reshape(B,T)\n\n  def _global_norm(tree):\n      leaves = jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(tree)\n      if not leaves: return jnp.array(0.0)\n\
    \      return jnp.sqrt(sum([jnp.sum(jnp.square(x)) for x in leaves]) + 1e-12)\n\n  def test():\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(43)\n      B, T = 8, 4\n      observations = jax.random.normal(key, (B, T, 8, 8, 8)) * 0.1\n      dones = jnp.zeros((B, T), dtype=bool)\n      actions = jax.random.randint(key, (B, T), 0, 4)\n      adv = jax.random.normal(key, (B, T))\n      ret = adv  # simple proxy\n\n      net = create_network({})\n      params = net.init(key, observations.reshape(B*T,8,8,8), dones.reshape(B*T), None)['params']\n\n      def loss_policy(p):\n          logits, _ = _forward(net, p, observations, dones)\n          log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n          idx = (jnp.arange(B)[:,None], jnp.arange(T), actions)\n          log_probs = log_probs_all[idx]\n          return -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(adv) * log_probs)\n\n      def loss_value(p):\n          _, values = _forward(net, p, observations, dones)\n          return 0.5 * jnp.mean((values - jax.lax.stop_gradient(ret))**2)\n\
    \n      g_pol = jax.grad(loss_policy)(params)\n      g_val = jax.grad(loss_value)(params)\n      g_pol_n = float(_global_norm(g_pol))\n      g_val_n = float(_global_norm(g_val))\n      rvp = g_val_n / (g_pol_n + 1e-12)\n\n      out = {\"mode\":\"init\",\"arch\":\"5step_64ch_vlc055\",\"grad_trunk_policy\":g_pol_n,\"grad_trunk_value\":g_val_n,\"rvp_ratio\":rvp}\n      try:\n          with open(PROBE_PATH, 'w') as f:\n              json.dump(out, f)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n      print(json.dumps(out))\n      return \"init probe complete\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing submission errors**


  I need to address the situation with the latest state regarding the Research Counter. It seems that attempts to submit inline probes for 4-step and 5-step failed due to "No trial files found" after a complete Ray training run, which is puzzling. The logs indicate successful training but an issue with trial file writing. This might suggest a mismatch in reporting or that the probes are interfering with Ray''s result saving. I''m going to look into the storage issues further.**Investigating training metrics**


  I’m considering another angle regarding the failure to return metrics to Ray. The training system should typically manage the default training loop and evaluation logs. However, since we replaced the training step with one that computes gradients, I still expect it to return parameters and optimization state. The "No trial files found" error might indicate that Ray didn’t produce a results JSON, possibly due to the training run crashing prematurely. This suggests that the evaluation aggregator couldn’t locate the trial files, even though the logs show full training completion. I need to explore these storage paths further!**Exploring trial file issues**


  I’m revisiting the earlier root cause concerning the inline probe failures. The logs indicate a difference between "using default" and "from submission," but both still encounter the same issue. One possibility is that the extra JAX gradient computations in our training step increased processing time. However, "No trial files found" occurring after full training suggests that the training might not have called RayTune to report or log results. Also, I’m wondering if the "PROBE_PATH" is causing I/O permission issues, even though it writes to an allowed directory. I need to dig deeper into this!**Reviewing training issues**


  Despite the training running for 981 seconds, I''m still facing the "No trial files found" issue. The documentation mentions that the "Automatic Debugging Feature" might resubmit scripts and produce this error if the training step returns the wrong type, but the CPU validation passes. I’m considering that our definition of PROBE_TARGETS with specific numbers might be affecting things. Also, the aggregator expects trials in Ray''s local trial directories, so perhaps the "No trial files found" error arises from a misconfiguration in local_dir. I need to explore this further!**Diagnosing trial file errors**


  I’m diving deep into why we’re seeing "No trial files found" after training runs of 981 seconds. Our training may not call RayTune to log results, potentially due to fixed hyperparameter choices. The RayTune configuration uses four parallel trials, so maybe the issues arise from how our custom training step interacts with JIT compilation.


  The training step could be executing Python I/O in a JIT context, causing potential side effect issues that aren’t visible in the logs. I think the aggregators might not be finding the results because they were possibly looking in the wrong directory! This requires another look!**Planning test-only experiments**


  Given the debugging challenges, I think it''s best not to run further probes for now. Instead, I want to analyze why the remote training environment fails to produce trial logs when the training step includes extra gradient calculations. Since test() stops training, it would lead to "No trial files found." However, I can propose to implement a test() function that calculates initial-phase metrics from synthetic data.


  Although the test() won''t produce useful training data, I can use it to log initial results like R_vp. Loading data from ''storage/shared/data/eval_512.npz'' might work, but I can also generate synthetic zeros to match the expected shapes. Although this won''t reflect true training, it may still help diagnose the architecture. I’ll set up the network parameters and compute gradients to submit test-only results effectively.**Implementing test-only submissions**


  The message about being CPU-only is clear, so to avoid "No trial files found," I’ll implement initial-phase probes. I plan to create ''submission_probes_init_pg4.py'' and ''submission_probes_init_pg5.py''. Since the test() function will replace training, I''ll ensure to meet the CPU validation requirements.


  I will write metrics like ''grad_trunk_policy'' and ''rvp_ratio'' to ''storage/zephyr/results/probes_init_pg4.json''. Using JAX, I''ll generate random observations and set up actions for log probabilities. After coding, I’ll submit the result for both 4-step and 5-step tests, capturing outputs in evaluation logs. Let''s get started!'
