thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI see we need to focus on actions regarding the current state of our experiments. We have two cosine LR schedule runs happening, but previous attempts failed due to missing trial files. At tick 372, both slots are occupied. We could either wait or update documentation and prepare submissions. It's logical to hold off updating the results table until we have results. \n\nI could also draft paper updates, noting the scheduler attempts in our archive and consider preparing a submission for a small entropy constant change.**Preparing next actions**\n\nIt seems like the schedule adjustments might not outperform the current method, but I can still plan one more attempt once those runs finish. Right now, I can't submit anything due to both slots being in use. I could prepare a storage file for submission content, but it's not necessary since the system processes the YAML content directly.\n\nI can write ready code in the submissions folder for future\
  \ use, possibly creating a small module for dynamic learning rates but sticking primarily to the default training steps. Also, I should focus on creating test-only probes first for calculating initial gradient norms.**Planning test implementation**\n\nThe specification indicates that the test() function should run and then exit, scoring as \"n.a.\" This approach won't involve training, and while \"test-only\" can show n.a. as a baseline, there’s currently a limit of two concurrent runs. \n\nI think it’s wise to wait, so I can prepare a shared storage module for \"probe_utils.py\" and create a probe submission. I can also write code for the test() that would build the network and print the gradient norm ratio for value loss and policy loss using a dummy batch — provided I sample random observations since no external environment is allowed.**Setting up test conditions**\n\nI understand that we need to conduct test-only operations without using a GPU. Running lightweight checks is acceptable,\
  \ but I must avoid any Sokoban-specific heuristics. Computing gradient norms with a random initial network should work with zero data, while adhering to the default settings in defaults.py for batch shapes.\n\nThe observation shape is (8,8,8), and for the test, I can generate random observations and compute network applications. To derive R_vp, I’ll need log probabilities and value losses, using zeros for initial advantages. While I can calculate these gradients with JAX, I’ll keep the batch size small, set at B=4 and T=5, to remain lightweight as required.**Preparing for tests and code submissions**\n\nI need to access the network apply signature for the test, which requires observations shaped correctly and_done entries. I can import the PG_Stepped_AttnGap module to build the network for the test() function, creating random observations and_done values.\n\nWhile I can't submit a probe right now due to concurrent limits, I can prep code for future submissions. I’ll write 'probe_init_gradnorms.py'\
  \ to compute R_vp, print statistics, and store it for later use. It’s crucial to wait for available slots before submission. I'll also focus on configuring the scheduling as my next steps instead of writing potentially risky test code.**Preparing submission actions**\n\nI’m planning to create a \"ready submission\" file for constant entropy micro-sweeps at levels 0.009, 0.010, and 0.011 using the default training step. This isn't strictly necessary, but it could be helpful for later.\n\nAfter the current experiments finish, I’ll also update the results table with new rows for the upcoming cosine schedule. \n\nI'll go ahead and write the new submission code, `submission_pg_entropy_floor_sweep.py`, which will involve importing the module, defining hyperparameters, creating the network and the optimizer. \n\nLastly, I’ll make minimal commentary and log my actions succinctly."
