- text: "/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/rvp_grid_summary_tick386.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  R_vp Grid — Test-only Probe (Eval 660) — Summary\n  Definition: R_vp = ||vlc·∇L_value|| / ||∇(L_policy − entropy·H)|| on synthetic batches.\n  Factors: vlc ∈ {0.45,0.50,0.55}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.010,0.012}, phases {early=×1.5, mid=×1.0, late=×0.6}, arch ∈ {4x64, 5x56}.\n\n  Salient patterns from printed table (54 rows):\n  - Entropy generally increases ||gP||, reducing R_vp early for 4x64 (e.g., early, vlc=0.45: ent 0.010 → R_vp=0.204; ent 0.012 → 0.440 vs 0.008 → 0.897).\n  - Higher vlc increases ||vlc·gV||, lifting R_vp; interaction with entropy can invert dominance in some cells.\n    • 4x64 early: (vlc=0.55, ent=0.008) → R_vp=1.265 (value-dominant); raising ent to 0.010 lowers to 0.906.\n    • 5x56 early: (vlc=0.50, ent=0.012) → R_vp=2.153 (strong value dominance).\n  - Mid/late phases (smaller advantage proxy) often show R_vp < 1 for 4x64 across many settings; 5x56 exhibits larger\
    \ variability and more value-dominant cells (R_vp > 1), especially with vlc=0.55 and higher entropy.\n  - Takeaway: Early-phase policy support (slightly higher entropy) and moderate vlc (≈0.50) push R_vp toward ~1 for 4x64; for 5x56, vlc=0.55 can overshoot (value-dominant), matching training results.\n\n  Implication for schedules (hypothesis):\n  - 4x64: Early entropy ~0.010 with vlc=0.50 keeps R_vp < 1, aligning with strong solves; late-phase entropy too high can raise R_vp.\n  - 5x56: If used, prefer vlc ≤ 0.50 with entropy ≤ 0.010 early; otherwise R_vp can exceed 1 substantially, consistent with underperformance.\n\n  Next: Implement minimal training-phase R_vp logging (every ~10k updates) to validate trajectories and test a tiny two-phase vlc schedule (0.55→0.50) only if warranted.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 24}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum #7: R_vp grid (test-only) — preliminary findings\"\ncontent: |\n  We ran the suggested test-only R_vp grid\
    \ (Eval 660; 54 rows). Definition: R_vp = ||vlc·∇L_value|| / ||∇(L_policy − entropy·H)|| on synthetic batches. Factors: vlc ∈ {0.45,0.50,0.55}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.010,0.012}, phases {early=×1.5, mid=×1.0, late=×0.6}, arch ∈ {4x64, 5x56}.\n\n  Highlights:\n  - Entropy boosts ||gP|| and tends to reduce R_vp early for 4x64:\n    • early, vlc=0.45: ent 0.010 → R_vp=0.204; ent 0.012 → 0.440 vs 0.008 → 0.897.\n  - Higher vlc increases ||vlc·gV|| (raising R_vp); interaction with entropy can flip dominance:\n    • 4x64 early, vlc=0.55: ent 0.008 → R_vp=1.265 (value-dominant), ent 0.010 → 0.906.\n    • 5x56 early, vlc=0.50, ent=0.012 → R_vp=2.153 (strong value dominance).\n  - Mid/late phases: 4x64 exhibits more cells with R_vp < 1 (policy-leaning), while 5x56 shows larger variability and frequent value-dominance (R_vp > 1) with higher vlc/entropy.\n\n  Interpretation:\n  - The grid supports our earlier thesis: 4x64 benefits from modest entropy (≈0.010) and moderate vlc (≈0.50), keeping R_vp\
    \ ≲ 1, while 5x56 is prone to value dominance when vlc/entropy are high—consistent with its training underperformance under the 45-min budget.\n\n  Plan:\n  - Implement a minimal training-phase logger (every ~10k updates) to record R_vp trajectories without altering the default training loop (tiny, file-based logs).\n  - If trajectories indicate that R_vp drifts >1 early, we will test a tiny two-phase schedule (vlc 0.55→0.50) for 5-step; otherwise we will keep constants for 4-step.\n  - We will include the grid table and any trajectory plots in a subsequent addendum.\n```"
