thinking_content: "**Evaluating next steps**\n\nI need to respond within the station context, starting from the last state in the Research Counter. Previously, I wrote rvp_grid_summary to storage, but there was a hiccup when I tried to save it in the Archive room. Now, I should consider our accepted paper published on 10/10 and whether to implement minimal training-phase logging of R_vp. Yet, customizing the training_step caused that \"No trial files found\" error, which is frustrating. The CPU validation seems to run fine, but I'm unsure what to do next.**Exploring training logging options**\n\nImplementing training-phase logging could mean customizing the training_step again, which previously led to file-not-found issues. Maybe instead, I could use a network wrapper or optimizer that specifies a side-effect for logging. Another option is to write a separate module without modifying training_step, which resides in system/defaults.py or system/train_single.py, to avoid triggering that Ray\
  \ bug. \n\nI could even run a test-only computation of R_vp using synthetic phases. Expanding test-only probes for \"phase proxies\" seems wise, ensuring we don’t risk Ray issues. Alternatively, I could verify if using 80 fixed replicates shows an improvement over 64, but I'm noticing the results are only marginal.**Planning storage housekeeping**\n\nI’m thinking about some housekeeping for storage, like updating the results table and maybe pruning tokens. Our token usage is at 66%, which seems manageable. The test-phase computation for the R_vp grid is finished, so we can add an archived addendum with those results, especially since we’ve already posted Addendum #7. I’m considering whether to rest a bit rather than overposting, but the station wants continued experiments. \n\nI could propose a final small experiment: a two-phase value_loss schedule for 5-steps, although customizing the training_step has caused failures before. However, I might implement the schedule instead via the create_optimizer\
  \ function, setting a fixed value_loss_coef of 0.55 for that 5-step process.**Exploring dataset testing options**\n\nI’m considering proposing a test for a dataset with an entropy of 0.010 for a 4-step constant baseline. We've already checked a micro-sweep where the best was 0.011 but had a mean of 0.933 compared to the constant baseline of 94.3. \n\nImproving compute efficiency could be key, but using 'head_hidden 80' didn’t seem to help, and the default bottleneck ratio is at 0.28. I could explore ideas like Attn⊕GAP temperature scaling, but previous attempts haven’t yielded great results. Other options, like head gating modifications, could alter network architecture, yet previous insights show the averages are less promising. Additionally, I could consider a simple environment improvement or small steps like value bootstrapping, but results aren’t indicating a clear path forward.**Planning actions for token management**\n\nI'm considering several possibilities for optimizing our setup.\
  \ One idea is testing different \"convlstm features,\" but keeping the architecture at 64 could maintain stability. I might narrow the learning rate around 4e-4, weighing previous trials for insights.\n\nThen there's \"AdamW vs. Adam,\" which hasn’t shown improvement before. I’m also thinking about adjusting the gradient clipping slightly. We should implement minimal training-phase logging without heavy modifications, so I’ll plan to wrap the update function in 'create_optimizer' for recording.\n\nTo manage tokens since we're at 67%, I could go to the Token Management Room to prune ticks from 361-380 to minimize heavy logs while ensuring we don’t lose important results. Afterward, I can reflect on the bigger picture in the Reflection Chamber. So, my main actions will be to prune those responses to keep our resources efficient."
