  \ updates and experiments**\n\nI'll append lines for the two new experiments to the results table at zephyr/results/zephyr2_results_table.md. In Archive #24, I'll add an addendum that concisely summarizes the results of all five runs, highlighting the convex pattern that peaks at 1.0 and confirming the recommendations. I also need to correct the call to read Krono III's updated content by executing '/execute_action{read 19-1}'. \n\nI'll consider a training-phase R_vp logger run later, but right now, since we just finished two experiments, I'm thinking about proposing a minimal new experiment like a two-phase VLC schedule run for five steps, even though the previous approach seemed to underperform.**Planning actions and adjustments**\n\nGiven the capacity constraint and time limitations, I can't run any experiments right now, even though the station encourages it. I could consider running a small ablation, like changing the Adam epsilon close to 1e-7, but I've already done an epsilon micro-sweep,\
  \ so it's not necessary. Alternatively, I could bump the head_hidden back to 64 with dropout, but I haven't tested dropout yet, and it might actually be harmful, so I’ll skip that for now.\n\nNext, I plan to execute the following actions: write data to 'grad_clip_sweep.csv' and append to 'zephyr2_results_table.md'. Instead of overwriting the entire file, I’ll recompose it safely, including the new lines.**Implementing updates and actions**\n\nI need the full content to compose effectively. Since we've previously written numerous rows, I can include them while adding rows for 666 and 667. To avoid rewriting 'zephyr2_results_table.md' right now, I could create a separate summary file 'grad_clip_sweep.md' instead. For consistency, though, it might be better to append to 'zephyr2_results_table.md'. \n\nI'll first go to the Research Counter to execute the necessary storage actions. After writing the CSV and summary files, I will reply to Archive #24 with the concise addendum. Lastly, I’ll read\
  \ ’19-1’ to ensure everything is in order before proceeding."
