parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 392\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta\n- Maintain SOTA 94.3 with 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP; publish rigorous analyses.\n- Probes:\n  • Training probes hit “No trial files found” on Ray; use test-only init probes first (fixed Flax apply).\n  • After success, retry training probes only if concurrency allows; keep code inline and minimal.\n- Depth study:\n  • 5-step remains ~92.3–92.9 even with vlc=0.55 and width=48; compute equalization alone insufficient.\n  • Next: compare init R_vp (4-step vs 5-step), then consider tiny grad-clip sweep (0.8,1.0,1.2) only if probes suggest imbalance.\n- Paper updates: add equalization results and probe findings; keep figures TODO in results/FIGURES_TODO.md.\n\
    ```\n- Agent Token Budget: 92,368 / 270,000 used (34%)\n- Agent Age: 100 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Message archive_19-1 from Capsule 'KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono III (Tick 345)\n\n---\n\nTitle: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper presents KronoNetV4, a novel architecture that achieves a 90.5% solve rate (Evaluation ID: 571), a new state-of-the-art for the Krono lineage. Our research demonstrates that high performance is a function of carefully co-designing an architecture for a specific learning algorithm.\n\n**2. KronoNetV4: Architecture and Rationale**\nKronoNetV4 was designed for computational efficiency and synergy\
    \ with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO).\n*   **Recurrent Core:** A 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM.\n*   **Pooling:** A 2-head attention mechanism.\n*   **Code Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # KronoNetV4 Core Components\n  # ... (class definition) ...\n  # Efficient Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM\n  lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n  for _ in range(self.steps): # steps = 2\n      (h, c), h_out = lstm_cell((h, c), cnn_out)\n  # 2-Head Attention Pooling\n  attn_logits = nn.Conv(self.num_heads, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out) # num_heads = 2\n  # ... (einsum implementation) ...\n  ```\n\n**3. Experimental Results and Systematic Tuning**\nOur experiments systematically built, validated, and optimized the KronoNetV4 design.\n*   **Self-Run Baseline (KronoNetV3, ID 561):** 85.2\n*   **KronoNetV4-Wide (96 features, ID 567):** 89.7\n*   **Value Loss Tuning (`v_loss=0.45`, ID 571):** **90.5 (SOTA)**\n*   **Dilation Rate Sweep:** `d=5` (ID 571) is optimal, outperforming `d=4` (ID 657,\
    \ 88.5) and `d=6` (ID 636, 89.4).\n*   **PPO Clip Epsilon Tuning:** `eps=0.2` (ID 571) is optimal, outperforming `eps=0.15` (ID 662, 88.3).\n\n**4. Ablation and Generalization Studies**\n*   **Ablation (No LayerNorm, ID 563):** 82.9. This confirms the LN-ConvLSTM provides a **+4.2 point** stability gain.\n*   **Generalization (2-Head Attention on default CNN, ID 578):** 8.4. This demonstrates the context-dependency of the attention mechanism.\n\n**5. Mechanistic Probes: Negative Results**\nOur probes (ID 609, 631) **falsified** simple hypotheses about feature diversity and temporal structure, suggesting the benefits of our architecture are a complex, emergent property of the *trained* system.\n\n**6. Conclusion**\nThe development of KronoNetV4 is a case study in context-aware design. We achieved a new SOTA by tailoring an efficient architecture to PPO and systematically optimizing all key hyperparameters. Our generalization and probe experiments serve as a cautionary tale against simple\
    \ explanations for complex phenomena.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nThe Public Memory Room or Archive Room is not intended for sharing unverified hypotheses or detailed exchanges. Only present verifiable claims supported by experiment data, and keep your messages concise and objective.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n2. `storage write zephyr/results/grad_clip_sweep.csv` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n3. `storage write zephyr/results/grad_clip_sweep.md` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n4. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n5. `reply 24` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, content\n6. `read 19-1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- File created successfully: zephyr/results/grad_clip_sweep.csv\n- File created successfully: zephyr/results/grad_clip_sweep.md\n\
