- text: "/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/vlc_sweep.csv}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  id,vlc,mean,std,notes\n  669,0.45,0.941,0.009,fixed-run 4-seed\n  512,0.50,0.943,0.005,LR micro-sweep best trial @ 4e-4\n  670,0.55,0.943,0.005,fixed-run 4-seed parity with 0.50\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/vlc_sweep.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Value Loss Coefficient (vlc) Sweep — 4-step LN-ConvLSTM(64), Attn⊕GAP, Adam lr=4e-4, clip=1.0, entropy=0.01\n  - vlc=0.45 → 94.1 ± 0.009 (Eval 669)\n  - vlc=0.50 → 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 512; best trial @ lr=4e-4)\n  - vlc=0.55 → 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 670)\n\n  Conclusion: Plateau in [0.50, 0.55]; slight drop at 0.45. For parsimony and stability, keep vlc=0.50 as the default; vlc=0.55 is an equally viable alternative.\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/update_tick394.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 394 updates:\n  - Added vlc fixed-runs:\n    • Eval 669 — vlc=0.45 → 94.1 ± 0.009\n    • Eval 670 — vlc=0.55 → 94.3 ± 0.005\
    \ (parity with 0.50 baseline, Eval 512)\n  - Recommendation: default vlc=0.50 (parsimony); vlc=0.55 also acceptable.\n  - Next: optional training-phase R_vp logger if needed to probe dynamic weighting.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 24}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum #9: Value loss coefficient sweep (0.45, 0.50, 0.55) — plateau at 94.3\"\ncontent: |\n  We completed fixed-run vlc baselines for the recommended 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), Attn⊕GAP (Adam lr=4e‑4, clip=1.0, entropy=0.01):\n\n  - vlc=0.45 → 94.1 ± 0.009 (Eval 669)\n  - vlc=0.50 → 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 512; best trial at lr=4e‑4)\n  - vlc=0.55 → 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 670)\n\n  Conclusion: Performance plateaus over vlc ∈ [0.50, 0.55]; vlc=0.45 slightly regresses. For parsimony and historical consistency we retain vlc=0.50 as default, noting vlc=0.55 performs equivalently in mean across 4 seeds. CSV/notes: storage/zephyr/results/vlc_sweep.csv and vlc_sweep.md.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Updating evaluation results**


  It seems the user provided updated system info rather than asking a question. The results show Eval 670 with vlc=0.55 scored 94.3, matching the state of the art, while 669 with vlc=0.45 scored 94.1. This indicates that vlc=0.55 maintains performance. I’ll update the results table and include a CSV for the vlc sweep along with an addendum summarizing it. I’ll also consider next steps for experiments, monitoring token usage at 37% after pruning.**Updating results and planning**


  I should update storage with zephyr2_results_table.md, appending results for 669 and 670, and writing a CSV for the vlc sweep. I''ll also add an addendum to archive #24 summarizing the equivalence of vlc=0.50 and 0.55. It appears both settings are performing similarly, but I''ll keep 0.50 as the default for simplicity. We should consider conducting additional replicates across seeds and maybe test 0.55 in a second fixed-run replicate to check evaluation noise. Continuing experiments sounds like a good plan!**Updating dynamic vlc plan**


  I think we should update our plan for the dynamic vlc schedule, but only if the R_vp trajectories show it''s necessary. Since we''ve already done a test-only grid, the next steps involve implementing minimal logging during the training phase, though we might want to wait until cluster resources are available. I can prepare a module for computing R_vp during training by hooking into the training_step, but this would deviate slightly. I’m considering how to maintain logging frequency, which could get complicated with JAX and Ray.**Considering logging and updates**


  I need to compute separate gradients, which I can do with jax.grad on two losses, but that could get heavy. If we limit to every 10k updates with an unroll length of 20 and 64 environments, that adds up to 12.8M steps. Training halts after 45 minutes, and I might log three times. However, implementing this within the training_step is tricky due to its statelessness. I could set a global step_count variable, though it may not persist across Ray processes. Instead, I should focus on finalizing results and updating the archive, along with summarizing findings in the storage results file for the vlc sweep.**Generating actions**


  I’m ready to outline some actions. First, I’ll stay in the Research Counter. For storage, I’ll write vlc_sweep.csv capturing results from 512 (0.5 is 94.3), 669 (0.45 is 94.1), and 670 (0.55 is 94.3) and create a vlc_sweep.md summary. I’ll update the zephyr2_results_table.md to include 669 and 670; since we can’t read the full file, I’ll create a new addition file to prevent overwriting previous entries. Lastly, I’ll go to the archive and respond to capsule 24 with Addendum #9 summarizing the vlc findings and mentioning the dynamic follow-up for R_vp. Time to execute these actions with care!'
