thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to figure out the next actions while in the Archive Room. We're currently handling two running experiments—676 and 677—so I can't submit additional experiments due to the concurrency limit. I could update the research results tables and maybe draft an addendum plan while capturing the results. It seems like creating a quick file for \"pending schedules\" and planning publication afterward is a good step. I'll also consider checking the storage list to make sure the value schedule module exists.**Considering token management**\n\nI'm thinking about whether to prune more tokens. The cooldown was earlier set to 7 ticks, and now it's at tick 398, with the last prune at tick 389. It seems we could prune again since 9 ticks have passed since the last action. Currently, we have 120k tokens used out of 270k, which is 45% — so maybe immediate pruning isn't necessary. However, the \"immortal cycle\" suggests we should prune if usage goes above 75%.\
  \ \n\nIt's essential to maintain responsible practices, so we won't post unverified claims to the Archive. We’ve added verified numbers before. For next research, I've got some ideas—perhaps a \"Tiny entropy schedule\" or something about using \"test-only logger R_vp trajectories.\" But I need to be careful about adding overhead or complexity to the code while managing training speed.**Planning next steps in research**\n\nIt looks like I need to follow the system rules, which mention no external resources and no Sokoban heuristics, but I can focus on general planning architectures and schedules. One thing to prepare is resilience—should we share code or just read others? It seems we won’t need to share.\n\nSo, my next commands will be to go to the research phase and write results into a pending schedule file. Additionally, I could add a training-phase logger variant once a slot opens. I can create a new submission file for the logger, but I’ll have to ensure we follow the limits for code\
  \ used in imports.**Designing a minimal logging wrapper**\n\nI’m thinking about creating a minimal wrapper that reuses the schedule training_step while adding the logging of step_count and computed R_vp. However, R_vp can be heavy to compute, so I want to avoid intense computations. Instead, I might calculate the ratio as gradient norms per update, which means I'd analyze gradients from policy and value without getting too heavy on the compute load. \n\nBut, the training_step uses a combined loss, and to measure R_vp accurately, I would need separate gradient calculations, which could lead to too much overhead. Perhaps I can approximate by capturing 'vlc' and looking at the magnitudes of value_loss and policy_loss. I’ll need to consider logging scalar proxies while keeping overhead low. However, scheduling a \"test-only\" training-phase logger might not be feasible for this situation.**Logging and planning next steps**\n\nI’m considering a better approach for logging small scalar values\
  \ from existing computations. Since we already compute policy_loss, value_loss, and entropy, it would be useful to log their gradient contributions. To minimize overhead, maybe I can perform an extra backward calculation every 5,000 updates to track gradients. \n\nFor now, though, with experiments running, I'll hold off on adding the logger. Instead, I’ll write a pending schedules document and prepare an archive addendum template to fill later. I'll also plan the next ablation while noting that new experiments won't be submitted due to the concurrency limit. Finally, I’ll update the results_table when everything is ready."
