- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 402\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta\n- Maintain SOTA 94.3 with 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP; publish rigorous analyses.\n- Probes:\n  • Training probes hit “No trial files found” on Ray; use test-only init probes first (fixed Flax apply).\n  • After success, retry training probes only if concurrency allows; keep code inline and minimal.\n- Depth study:\n  • 5-step remains ~92.3–92.9 even with vlc=0.55 and width=48; compute equalization alone insufficient.\n  • Next: compare init R_vp (4-step vs 5-step), then consider tiny grad-clip sweep (0.8,1.0,1.2) only if probes suggest imbalance.\n- Paper updates: add equalization results and probe findings; keep figures TODO in results/FIGURES_TODO.md.\n\
    ```\n- Agent Token Budget: 135,614 / 270,000 used (50%)\n- Agent Age: 110 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Architect Message**\n\nThe Station has entered a stage of stagnation, as no breakthroughs have been made for many ticks. Cease your current work and execute the following Stagnation Protocol I:\n\n1. **Literature Review** — Run preview all in the Archive Room and summarize the results so far.\n2. **Baseline Selection** — Pick a baseline that is simple and has a reasonable score (it doesn’t need to be high). This step is important, as a novel method often fails to show effects on a complex yet brittle baseline.\n3. **Reflection** — Run a multi-tick reflection to come up with three new ideas based on the chosen baseline. These ideas should be different from those already found in the Archive Room.\n4. **Experiment** — First, reproduce the baseline score. Then, test each of the three ideas one at a time, trying different hyperparameter settings.\
    \ Keep your scripts close to the baseline, changing only what’s needed for the new ideas.\n5. **Synthesis** — Combine the promising ideas identified from earlier experimental results with more advanced baselines (e.g., the SOTA baseline) and determine whether those ideas can help break through the barrier. You are advised to test this step extensively.\n6. **Report** — After testing all ideas—whether they succeed or not—write a paper summarizing all three and submit it to the Archive Room. Negative results with new insights are still useful. Success is measured by doing better than the baseline, not by reaching the SOTA score.\n7. **Follow-up** — If you or other agents produce a promising paper in a new direction (even if the score is still below SOTA), continue following up on that line of work.\n\nThink of stagnation as being stuck in a local optimum. To escape, we need to restart from a new point—one that might start with high loss—but from there, we can improve step by step. Progress\
    \ will come through many small steps (each idea being a step) across multiple agents, not one giant leap. Focus on exploring new directions and ignore the SOTA score, which is just a distraction right now.\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — entropy_coef=0.012 (fixed, 4‑seed)' (ID: 679) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 92.8\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.940, 0.925, 0.924, 0.924]\nMean: 0.928, Std: 0.007\nBest trial: 1 (0.940)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.012000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  convlstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n  head_hidden: 64.000000\n  steps: 4.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function\
