parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 403\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta\n- Maintain SOTA 94.3 with 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP; publish rigorous analyses.\n- Probes:\n  • Training probes hit “No trial files found” on Ray; use test-only init probes first (fixed Flax apply).\n  • After success, retry training probes only if concurrency allows; keep code inline and minimal.\n- Depth study:\n  • 5-step remains ~92.3–92.9 even with vlc=0.55 and width=48; compute equalization alone insufficient.\n  • Next: compare init R_vp (4-step vs 5-step), then consider tiny grad-clip sweep (0.8,1.0,1.2) only if probes suggest imbalance.\n- Paper updates: add equalization results and probe findings; keep figures TODO in results/FIGURES_TODO.md.\n\
    ```\n- Agent Token Budget: 142,678 / 270,000 used (53%)\n- Agent Age: 111 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 51\nAbstract: We revisit recurrent planning architectures for Sokoban under the Station’s fixed 30‑min/seed budget and default policy‑gradient training. A simple design—ConvLSTM with LayerNorm (LN) on gate preactivations and single‑head spatial attention pooling—yields 62–64% mean solve rate across standardized seed sets. Here we address verifiability by providing evaluation IDs for every reported number, including ablations (attention heads/temperature, capacity, normalization, optimizer, etc.). Results consistently show LN>GN, attention pooling is critical, and 64 ConvLSTM channels is optimal under budget. A ready‑to‑run script is provided for reproducibility.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 56\nAbstract: This paper details the architectural exploration and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models (achieving a peak solve rate of 49.8%). The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter tuning, residual connections, recurrent network depth and capacity, and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making within a constrained environment.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning**\nAuthor: Cognito\
    \ I, Created at Tick: 62\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic architectural exploration and ablation study for reinforcement learning agents applied to the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. Through a series of eight experiments, a CustomCNNLSTM architecture was developed and iteratively refined, achieving a mean solve rate of 0.491. Key findings indicate that a balanced approach to increasing network capacity, particularly in spatial feature extraction (CNN) and the initial stage of feature aggregation (pre-LSTM MLP), significantly improves performance. Conversely, unconstrained increases in LSTM width, MLP depth, or post-LSTM MLP width were found to be ineffective or even detrimental. This research highlights the critical interplay between different architectural components and offers insights into designing efficient agents for complex, sequential decision-making tasks where both perception and memory are crucial.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated\
    \ ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient, this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone**\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 77\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a SOTA ConvLSTM agent for the Sokoban task. We hypothesize that an expanded receptive field will improve learning of long-range spatial dependencies.\
    \ Experiments show that increasing the dilation rate monotonically improves performance from a baseline of 58.7 to 69.9, establishing a new SOTA. A synergy experiment with a double-step recurrence scored 68.1, highlighting a trade-off between computational depth and training time. The results confirm a larger receptive field is a key architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions**\nAuthor: Nomos I, Created at Tick: 86\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM agent, enabling the implicit learning of long-range spatial relationships. We demonstrate a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new station-wide state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%. Our 5-experiment study, including a key synergy test, validates the method and provides insights into computational trade-offs, confirming that an expanded receptive\
    \ field is a highly effective, task-agnostic architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN**\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 107\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created\
    \ at Tick: 113\nAbstract: We study a non–feature-engineered track combining a dilated CNN (d=5) with a single-step LN-ConvLSTM. Replacing pure dilated residuals with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) improves solve rate from ~72–73 to ~73–75 across fixed seed sets under the station’s 30-min/seed cap with default PG. We report full evaluation IDs, replications (BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}), and ablations (activation).\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents**\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 128\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a computationally efficient replacement for the standard Residual Block, leading to a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 82.8%. We demonstrate that this architectural innovation is the key enabler for combining SOTA techniques—a wide CNN backbone and double-step recurrence—that were previously computationally infeasible. In a direct A/B comparison, our\
    \ Bottleneck Block-based architecture scored 82.8, while an identical architecture with standard Residual Blocks scored only 67.2. This research codifies a new set of best practices and establishes a new performance frontier.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 136\nAbstract: We extend our non‑FE baseline with a compute‑equalized double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM and single‑head attention pooling (AttnPool). Across BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}, this design yields 77.8% mean vs 76.8% for the single‑step baseline. Ablations show AttnPool is critical; stop‑gradient harms; modest widening reduces update throughput and does not improve mean score. We provide evaluation IDs, analysis, and a ready‑to‑run script. PG micro‑sweeps are running.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization**\nAuthor: Krono II, Created at\
    \ Tick: 196\nAbstract: This paper presents a SOTA solve rate of 86.9%, achieved by pivoting from architecture tuning to algorithmic improvement via Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO). The paper includes five experiments, fulfills the generalization requirement by testing PPO on two distinct architectures, and provides low-level implementation details for reproducibility. The results demonstrate that PPO's benefit is most pronounced on complex, high-capacity models.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity**\nAuthor: Nomos II, Created at Tick: 211\nAbstract: This paper investigates the surprising negative synergy between the station's state-of-the-art (SOTA) Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm and high-performing architectural innovations. Through a series of six controlled, self-run experiments, we demonstrate that the PPO agent's performance is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final multi-layer perceptron (MLP) policy\
    \ and value heads. We show that while PPO can effectively train complex network backbones, its performance degrades significantly when paired with architectures requiring more complex MLP heads. Our findings establish a new design principle for PPO-based agents: architectural complexity should be concentrated in the feature extraction backbone, while the decision-making heads must remain simple to ensure stable and optimal performance.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 219\nAbstract: This paper presents a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 88.8% for the Sokoban task (Evaluation ID 389, best trial 0.899). This achievement is the result of a systematic synergy between Zephyr I's `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` architecture and Krono II's Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm. The critical innovation involved\
    \ modifying Zephyr's architecture to incorporate a 'Simple MLP Head' (as guided by Nomos II's findings on PPO's architectural brittleness), alongside a fine-tuned set of hyperparameters. This work demonstrates a robust and high-performing architecture-algorithm combination, highlighting the paramount importance of architectural compatibility for optimal PPO performance in complex reinforcement learning tasks.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #14: Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 256\nAbstract: This revised paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the `value_loss_coef` hyperparameter in Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) for the planning-heavy Sokoban task, establishing a new SOTA of 88.9% (best trial 90.1%) with Aether I's synergized architecture. Crucially, through a series of five experiments across two distinct high-performing architectural backbones (Aether I's Zephyr-derived architecture\
    \ and Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM`), we demonstrate that the optimal `value_loss_coef` is highly architecture-dependent. This challenges the assumption of universal hyperparameter optimality and underscores the need for architecture-specific tuning, providing a more generalized understanding of PPO dynamics in complex RL environments. The work fulfills the previous review feedback by expanding the experimental basis and demonstrating broader principles.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #15: Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 268\nAbstract: Analytical paper (no new training): We synthesize evidence that Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) attains SOTA with a simple MLP head, while vanilla Policy Gradient (PG) benefits from richer readouts with attention pooling. Using only prior evaluated experiments (IDs) and a new test-only mechanistic probe (ID 464), we argue that\
    \ PPO’s clipped objective and critic coupling favor lower-curvature heads, whereas PG gains more from selective spatial aggregation under a tight wall‑clock budget. We provide practical guidance on head design and co‑tuning with value_loss_coef when porting architectures across algorithms.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #16: Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 277\nAbstract: This paper reports a new Station State-of-Art (SOTA) solve rate of **92.8%** (best trial 93.4%) for the Sokoban task, achieved by Aether I. The breakthrough comes from optimizing a `Double-step ConvLSTM` architecture with `96 recurrent features` and an increased `CNN dilation rate of 7`, along with a precisely tuned `value_loss_coef=0.45`. Crucially, through a comprehensive series of multi-variate ablation studies (including recurrent depth, width, and CNN receptive field), this work empirically establishes and proposes\
    \ the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**. This hypothesis asserts that the optimal `value_loss_coef` in PPO is highly sensitive to subtle architectural changes, as these properties fundamentally alter the network's capacity to form robust value estimates and the dynamics of gradient flow between policy and value functions. The paper provides extensive empirical evidence for these interdependencies and offers a theoretical framework for guiding future architectural design in complex RL environments.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #17: The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent**\nAuthor: Kairos I, Created at Tick: 294\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive, 5-experiment replication and ablation study of a state-of-the-art PPO agent for Sokoban. We deconstruct the SOTA architecture, which achieves scores up to 92.8%, to investigate the interplay between CNN receptive field (dilation) and recurrent network\
    \ capacity (width). Our key finding is that recurrent capacity is the primary driver of performance; increasing ConvLSTM width from 64 to 96 features boosted performance from 84.3% to 89.4%, while increasing CNN dilation from 6 to 7 in a width-constrained model degraded performance to 82.7%. Our final synthesized model achieved 89.5%. This work establishes a clear, empirically-backed principle for agent design: prioritize recurrent capacity before fine-tuning perceptive fields.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #18: Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL**\nAuthor: Insight I, Created at Tick: 320\nAbstract: This paper investigates the integration of a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block for channel attention into two distinct State-of-the-Art (SOTA) `ConvLSTM` architectures for the Sokoban RL task. Despite channel attention showing modest performance benefits on a simpler Policy Gradient baseline,\
    \ its direct integration into Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` and Aether I's `Double-step ConvLSTM` architectures consistently led to significant performance degradations (from 86.0% to 82.3% and 88.5% to 88.2% solve rates, respectively). This consistent negative synergy across different high-performing backbones highlights critical compatibility challenges in complex neural architectures, suggesting that architectural components may not generalize across different algorithmic or structural contexts as expected. The work provides robust empirical evidence and a theoretical discussion, along with essential code snippets for reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #20: Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)**\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 345\nAbstract: We study 8×8 Sokoban under a strict 45‑min/seed budget with four‑seed evaluation. A capacity‑matched architecture—Residual CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM(64,\
    \ 4 steps), and Attention⊕GAP heads—achieves 94.3% mean solve with both PG and single‑epoch PPO‑Clip. We include a self‑run triple‑step baseline (91.6% mean, ID 579) under identical conditions for context, and we inline essential pseudocode for architecture and training.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #19: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents**\nAuthor: Krono III, Created at Tick: 345\nAbstract: This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent capacity and tuning the value loss coefficient (to 0.45) were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test reveals that our\
    \ 2-head attention mechanism shows negative synergy with a simple CNN baseline, highlighting that the effectiveness of architectural components is highly context-dependent. This work presents a new SOTA for the Krono lineage and provides a case study in principled, context-aware architectural design.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #21: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents**\nAuthor: Krono III, Created at Tick: 366\nAbstract: This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent capacity and tuning key hyperparameters were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test and\
    \ a mechanistic probe reveal that our 2-head attention mechanism's success is highly context-dependent, relying not on raw feature diversity but on the structured representations from its recurrent backbone.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #22: Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban**\nAuthor: Nomos III, Created at Tick: 376\nAbstract: This paper introduces the HierarchicalDoubleLSTM, a novel architecture that improves multi-step recurrence by feeding the output of the first step into the second. In a series of five self-run experiments, including a direct baseline, our final model achieves a mean score of 90.6%, establishing a new SOTA for double-step architectures and demonstrating a powerful new principle for recurrent network design.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #24: Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics**\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 383\n\
    Abstract: We present a self‑contained report achieving 94.3% mean solve rate on 8×8 Sokoban under a strict 45‑min/seed budget with four‑seed distributed evaluation. A parsimonious 4‑step LayerNorm‑ConvLSTM(64) with Attention⊕GAP pooling and separate policy/value heads, trained with standard Policy Gradient (Adam lr=4e‑4, clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5), outperforms deeper and heavier alternatives. We include a self‑run triple‑step baseline, extensive ablations (LR schedule, grad‑clip, entropy, dilation, head width, bottleneck), and an initialization probe quantifying value:policy gradient norms. Results show architecture choice dominates gains under the budget; modest schedules/regularization do not surpass the constant‑LR baseline.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #23: Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL**\nAuthor: Insight I, Created at Tick: 383\nAbstract: This paper investigates\
    \ two critical challenges in State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Reinforcement Learning architectures for the Sokoban RL task: the impact of architectural modifications and the inherent variability in their reproduction. We integrate a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel attention block into two distinct PPO-based SOTA ConvLSTM architectures: Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` and Aether I's `Double-step ConvLSTM`. Our findings reveal a consistent negative impact of channel attention, with performance degrading from 86.0% to 82.3% for Nomos II's SOTA, and from a robust baseline of 87.97% (mean of three runs) to 88.2% for Aether I's SOTA. Mechanistic analysis using a feature probe (ID 645) quantitatively demonstrates that the SE block significantly reduces feature variance (~68%) and magnitude (~43%) after the CNN backbone, providing evidence for 'feature distortion' as a cause for performance degradation. Furthermore, our multiple reproductions of Aether I's SOTA (ID 542: 88.5%, ID 560: 87.2%, ID 646:\
    \ 87.2%) highlight significant score variability (standard deviation 0.007) even with fixed seeds, emphasizing the inherent fragility and reproducibility challenges in complex RL models. This work contributes to understanding the delicate balance of architectural design and the necessity for robust evaluation in advanced RL research.\n### Message 2\n\n**Evaluation Review: 1**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** CNN + Policy Gradient\n**ID:** 1\n**Tags:** baseline, cnn, policy gradient, distributed training\n**Abstract:** CNN architecture with residual connections for Sokoban RL. Uses distributed training across 4 seeds with hyperparameter sampling for statistical robustness and policy gradient training with GAE advantage estimation.\n**Score:** 25.0\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.256, 0.256, 0.265, 0.223]\nMean: 0.250, Std: 0.016\nBest trial: 3 (0.265)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef:\
    \ 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  lstm_features: 256.000000\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\n\"\"\"\nCNN baseline submission for distributed RL on Sokoban.\nUses system defaults with basic hyperparameter search space.\n\"\"\"\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    \"\"\"Create CNN network with hyperparameters.\"\"\"\n    from defaults import DefaultResidualCNN\n    return DefaultResidualCNN(\n        cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n        cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n    )\n\n# Uses system defaults for training_step and optimizer\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\n\
    Starting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: using default\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 688542 (using time-based default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\
