parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA\"\ntags: \"stagnation, baseline, negative results, generalization, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"We restart from the system CNN+PG baseline (~25% mean) and test three single‑change ideas (AdamW, Huber value loss, GroupNorm). All regress on the simple baseline across 4 seeds. To satisfy generalization, we further show that Huber value loss also regresses sharply on a distinct high‑performing architecture—the 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP SOTA (88.7% vs 94.3%). Together, these results suggest robustifying the critic target with Huber is counterproductive under the station’s budget for both low‑ and high‑capacity agents.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Protocol and Motivation\n  The Station entered stagnation. Following Stagnation Protocol I, we:\n  - reviewed archive literature;\
    \ chose a simple robust baseline (system CNN+PG);\n  - proposed three single‑change ideas;\n  - reproduced the baseline and ran one‑change variants;\n  - provided a cross‑architecture generalization test for at least one idea.\n\n  2. Setup (Self‑Contained)\n  Task: 8×8 Sokoban; score = mean test solve over 4 seeds (≤50M steps or 45 min/seed). \n  Baselines:\n  - Simple: DefaultResidualCNN + Policy Gradient (PG) with GAE; fixed hparams: lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.50; cnn_features_1=32, cnn_features_2=64; BASE_SEED=42.\n  - High‑capacity: 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attention⊕GAP heads (our SOTA backbone), PG with same defaults.\n\n  Training loop pseudocode (PG with GAE; simplified):\n    collect rollout → advantages = GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97) → returns = values + advantages\n    policy_loss = −E[stop(adv)·logπ(a|s)]\n    value_loss  = 0.5·E[(V(s) − stop(returns))^2]   # unless Huber is used\n    entropy     = E[H(π(.|s))]\n    total_loss  = policy_loss + vlc·value_loss − ent_coef·entropy\n\
    \    θ ← Adam(θ, ∇ total_loss) with global clip(1.0)\n\n  3. Simple CNN+PG — Baseline and One‑Change Variants (4 seeds; Evaluation IDs)\n  - Baseline reproduction: Eval 684 — 0.247 ± 0.006\n  - AdamW (wd=2e‑4):      Eval 685 — 0.233 ± 0.015 (regression)\n  - Huber value (δ=1.0):  Eval 686 — 0.181 ± 0.005 (strong regression)\n  - GroupNorm trunk (GN8):Eval 687 — 0.124 ± 0.009 (strong regression)\n  - Entropy schedule 0.012→0.010 @15k: Eval 690 — 0.239 ± 0.016 (slight regression)\n  - Constant vlc=0.45:    Eval 691 — 0.244 ± 0.012 (≈ baseline)\n  - (Submitted) Constant vlc=0.55: Eval 695 — pending (will append on completion)\n\n  4. Generalization Requirement — Second Architecture Evidence\n  We test the most interesting finding—Huber value loss—on a distinct high‑capacity architecture (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP, SOTA PG config):\n  - SOTA baseline (constant MSE value): 94.3% mean (Evals 512/670 context)\n  - Huber value (δ=1.0) on SOTA: Eval 520 — 88.7% mean (−5.6 points absolute\
    \ vs baseline)\n  Together with CNN+PG (Eval 686 = 0.181), this demonstrates that Huber value loss consistently regresses across two distinct architectures and two capacity regimes, satisfying the generalization requirement for this method.\n\n  5. Synthesis and Interpretation\n  - Across both low‑capacity CNN and high‑capacity LN‑ConvLSTM, Huber value loss reduces performance under the wall‑clock budget. We hypothesize it reduces useful gradient magnitude/curvature alignment for value learning, slowing convergence enough to matter within 45 min/seed.\n  - On the simple CNN baseline, added regularization (AdamW), robust targets (Huber), or heavier normalization (GroupNorm) likely depress the already limited learning signal; small constants (vlc, entropy) do not help either.\n\n  6. Relation to Archive\n  Archive SOTA gains arise from recurrent capacity (LN‑ConvLSTM), receptive‑field design (dilations), and bottleneck efficiency, with algorithmic compatibility (PG vs PPO) carefully controlled.\
    \ Our findings support that generic robustification/regularization changes—effective in other domains—are insufficient here without appropriate capacity and planning structure.\n\n  7. Reproducibility Checklist\n  - Fixed seeds/hparams; 4‑seed means reported with station Evaluation IDs.\n  - Self‑contained training loop and all critical details provided above.\n  - Scripts: CNN variants (submissions/cnn_pg_*.py), SOTA Huber (Eval 520) previously reported and referenced.\n\n  8. Follow‑Up Plan\n  - Complete the CNN constants‑only grid by adding vlc=0.55 (pending Eval 695) and append to this capsule.\n  - Optional: test GroupNorm‑style normalization on the SOTA trunk for completeness (expected negative per archive evidence that LN gating is critical).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Revising Baseline Results**\n\nI'm considering testing Huber value loss on the state-of-the-art (SOTA) architecture since it previously regressed when tried on the SOTA policy gradient. This observation suggests a generalization problem across architectures, especially since the current paper aims for a simple baseline. I’ll focus on Huber value as a negative result in both the CNN baseline and LN-ConvLSTM SOTA, providing evidence from evaluation IDs 686 and 520 to meet the generalization requirements. \n\nNext, I also need to test GroupNorm on the SOTA trunk.**Implementing GroupNorm**\n\nI need to implement a GroupNorm version in the SOTA trunk, which currently uses bottleneck blocks. We have several network definitions ready, like \"cnn_convlstm_layernorm\" and \"cnn_convlstm_norm.py,\" that might support different norms. Without opening files, I can create a submission utilizing a network definition with GroupNorm. It’s important to ensure we reuse \"submission_pg_quadstep.py,\"\
  \ which uses LN-ConvLSTM and attention. Alternatively, I could test Huber value on the SOTA again, revising the negative-results paper to include both CNN baseline and SOTA for Huber loss to meet generalization requirements.**Updating the Archive Paper**\n\nI have a plan to update the archive paper by including an additional experiment that demonstrates generalization—specifically, Huber value loss on the SOTA architecture regression (Eval 520). I can add results and analysis to show we meet the requirement of having 5+ experiments. Although the reviewer asked for a demonstration of generality for at least one proposed method, we already have that covered with Huber on SOTA. If necessary, I could also consider submitting a new experiment on GroupNorm, but currently, I’ll focus on revising the paper using existing results from ID 520 as evidence.**Revising the Archive Protocol**\n\nThe \"Stagnation Protocol\" suggests we can revise the paper and possibly schedule GroupNorm on SOTA to strengthen\
  \ our findings. Considering our time constraints, I should update the archive by adding a generalization section with Huber on SOTA and propose a follow-up for GroupNorm. I’ll create a revised archive rather than a new one since updating is more efficient. The Archive Room allows me to update capsule content using a specific action. We can refer to the previously rejected paper, \"Restarting from a Simple Baseline,\" for context.**Creating Revised Archive Capsule**\n\nI need to create a new archive capsule due to the lack of an ID from the previous submission. I can update with generalization evidence, focusing on Huber value loss on SOTA and referencing evaluation ID 520. After that, I’ll prepare for a follow-up experiment testing GroupNorm on the SOTA trunk. I also have the option to prune tokens for efficiency, but I should be cautious not to remove important logs. \n\nI’ll include sections in the revised paper summarizing our findings and proposed methods, ensuring we satisfy the generalization\
  \ requirements."
