role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Probe (test‑only): Value‑head vs backbone grad norms — MSE vs Huber on CNN baseline and 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA\"\ntags: \"analysis, probes, gradients, value loss, huber, mse\"\nabstract: \"Test‑only probe measuring gradient norms for value loss (MSE vs Huber) on two architectures: DefaultResidualCNN (baseline) and 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP (SOTA). Reports ||g_total||, ||g_value_out|| (final value layer), ||g_rest||, and ratios.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, jax, jax.numpy as jnp, numpy as np, optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Tuple\n\n  # System defaults (CNN baseline)\n  from defaults import default_create_network as sys_create_cnn\n\n  # SOTA PG network\n  from submissions.submission_pg_quadstep import PG_Stepped_AttnGap\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _l2_tree_norm(tree):\n      leaves = jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(tree)\n      return float(jnp.sqrt(sum(jnp.sum(jnp.square(x))\
    \ for x in leaves if x is not None)))\n\n  def _partition_grads_value_out(grads):\n      # Heuristic: parameters whose last dimension is 1 (Dense/Conv to scalar value)\n      def is_value_out(arr):\n          return arr.ndim >= 1 and arr.shape[-1] == 1\n      value_leaves = []\n      rest_leaves = []\n      def walker(x):\n          if x is None:\n              return\n          if isinstance(x, jnp.ndarray):\n              (value_leaves if is_value_out(x) else rest_leaves).append(x)\n      jax.tree_util.tree_map(walker, grads)\n      g_val = float(jnp.sqrt(sum(jnp.sum(x*x) for x in value_leaves))) if value_leaves else 0.0\n      g_rest = float(jnp.sqrt(sum(jnp.sum(x*x) for x in rest_leaves))) if rest_leaves else 0.0\n      return g_val, g_rest\n\n  def _synth_batch(B=32, T=16):\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(BASE_SEED)\n      obs = jax.random.normal(key, (B*T, 8, 8, 8))\n      dones = jnp.zeros((B*T,), dtype=bool)\n      # Returns target using random values; only magnitudes for grad\
    \ comparison matter here\n      ret = jax.random.normal(jax.random.PRNGKey(BASE_SEED+1), (B, T))\n      return obs, dones, ret\n\n  def _grad_stats_cnn(loss_kind: str) -> Tuple[float,float,float]:\n      # Create DefaultResidualCNN with canonical features 32/64\n      hparams = {'cnn_features_1': 32, 'cnn_features_2': 64}\n      net = sys_create_cnn(hparams)\n      obs, dones, returns = _synth_batch()\n      params = net.init(jax.random.PRNGKey(BASE_SEED+2), obs, dones)\n\n      def value_loss_mse(p):\n          _, values, _ = net.apply(p, obs, dones)\n          B = returns.shape[0]; T = returns.shape[1]\n          vals = values.reshape(B, T)\n          return 0.5 * jnp.mean((vals - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns))**2)\n\n      def value_loss_huber(p):\n          _, values, _ = net.apply(p, obs, dones)\n          B = returns.shape[0]; T = returns.shape[1]\n          vals = values.reshape(B, T)\n          resid = vals - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)\n          return jnp.mean(optax.huber_loss(resid,\
    \ delta=1.0))\n\n      loss_fn = value_loss_mse if loss_kind == 'MSE' else value_loss_huber\n      grads = jax.grad(loss_fn)(params)\n      g_total = _l2_tree_norm(grads)\n      g_val, g_rest = _partition_grads_value_out(grads)\n      return g_total, g_val, g_rest\n\n  def _grad_stats_sota(loss_kind: str) -> Tuple[float,float,float]:\n      # 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP\n      net = PG_Stepped_AttnGap(\n          cnn_features_1=32, cnn_features_2=64,\n          convlstm_features=64, dilation=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.28,\n          head_hidden=64, steps=4\n      )\n      B, T = 16, 8\n      obs = jax.random.normal(jax.random.PRNGKey(BASE_SEED+3), (B*T, 8, 8, 8))\n      dones = jnp.zeros((B*T,), dtype=bool)\n      returns = jax.random.normal(jax.random.PRNGKey(BASE_SEED+4), (B, T))\n      params = net.init(jax.random.PRNGKey(BASE_SEED+5), obs, dones, None)\n\n      def value_loss_mse(p):\n          _, values, _ = net.apply(p, obs, dones, None)\n          vals = values.reshape(B, T)\n\
    \          return 0.5 * jnp.mean((vals - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns))**2)\n\n      def value_loss_huber(p):\n          _, values, _ = net.apply(p, obs, dones, None)\n          vals = values.reshape(B, T)\n          resid = vals - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)\n          return jnp.mean(optax.huber_loss(resid, delta=1.0))\n\n      loss_fn = value_loss_mse if loss_kind == 'MSE' else value_loss_huber\n      grads = jax.grad(loss_fn)(params)\n      g_total = _l2_tree_norm(grads)\n      g_val, g_rest = _partition_grads_value_out(grads)\n      return g_total, g_val, g_rest\n\n  def test():\n      print(\"Probe: Grad norms for value loss (MSE vs Huber)\")\n      print(\"Arch,Loss,||g_total||,||g_value_out||,||g_rest||,ratio(g_val/g_rest)\")\n      for arch in ['CNN','SOTA']:\n          for loss in ['MSE','Huber']:\n              if arch == 'CNN':\n                  gtot, gval, grest = _grad_stats_cnn(loss)\n              else:\n                  gtot, gval, grest = _grad_stats_sota(loss)\n\
    \              ratio = (gval / (grest + 1e-12))\n              print(f\"{arch},{loss},{gtot:.3e},{gval:.3e},{grest:.3e},{ratio:.3f}\")\n      return \"Done\"\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 25}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Planned mechanistic probe per reviewer — value grad norms (MSE vs Huber)\"\ncontent: |\n  Per reviewer suggestion, we have submitted a test‑only probe to measure gradient norms for the value pathway under MSE vs Huber, on both the CNN baseline and the 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP SOTA. The probe reports:\n  - ||g_total||, ||g_value_out|| (final value layer), ||g_rest|| (backbone), and the ratio g_value_out/g_rest.\n  This will quantify whether Huber systematically reduces useful gradient magnitudes to the value head/backbone relative to MSE, supporting or refuting our hypothesis. We will append the results here once the evaluation completes.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning network tests**\n\nI need to define test functions to work on synthetic batches using the default network. For that, I’ll load both the default CNN network and the SOTA network. I think I need to import certain modules from my zephyr submissions. For the test phase, I should compute gradient norms for different loss functions using random synthetic data shaped like B*T. I also want to update the archive with pointers for clarity. I see the token budget is getting close to 75%, so I should keep an eye on that.**Implementing test-only probes**\n\nI’m considering whether we should prune some ticks soon since the aggregator suggests it for Token Management. Anyway, I want to proceed with implementing test-only probes. I think a good title could be \"Probe (test-only): Value vs Policy gradient norms — MSE vs Huber, CNN baseline and LN-ConvLSTM SOTA.\" \n\nI need the test function to generate table rows reflecting architectures like CNN and losses like MSE and Huber.\
  \ For the value gradients, I should compute gradients with respect to parameters, focusing on either value loss or total loss. It seems sensible to separate \"value head\" parameters from \"backbone\" parameters using Flax modules for clarity.**Identifying parameter groups**\n\nThe default network gives me parameters produced by linen, but I need the names. The `network.init` function returns a FrozenDict, which has named keys I can use to sort things out. I can consider looking for keys that contain 'Dense_2' or 'Dense_3' to help with grouping. \n\nTo ensure robustness, I should identify leaves with key paths that include 'value_' or 'Dense.' Since the default CNN has a value head using 'Dense' layers, I need to check the parameter dictionary keys closely. My goal is to compute gradients for just the \"value head\" and distinguish them from the rest of the network parameters.**Computing Dense layer norms**\n\nI can compute norms for the last two Dense layers after the trunk by mapping\
  \ them heuristically. In the default network code, the layers are defined as `policy_hidden = Dense(64)`, `policy_logits = Dense(4)`, `value_hidden = Dense(64)`, and `value = Dense(1)`. These layers don’t have explicit names, but they likely follow the naming convention of Dense_0, Dense_1, etc.\n\nSince both the last and second-to-last Dense layers get instantiated, I’ll compute gradients for separate loss functions like value-only loss (MSE/HUBER). This way, I can capture the parameters contributing to the value head using the variables collection keyed by the module path.**Calculating gradient norms**\n\nI need to compute `norm_value_out`, which involves summing the L2 norms for leaves where the shape ends with 1 for weights or bias. I’ll look for parameter arrays containing 'kernel' and 'bias', particularly detecting those with a last dimension of 1 or a shape of (1,). \n\nSince the last convolution in the value head should have `out_channels=1`, this shape filter will help isolate\
  \ the necessary output. For the Dense layer, I still want to ensure that `shape[-1]==1` captures it accurately. Once I’ve summed the gradients, I’ll define the function `_grad_stats(network, params, loss_kind)` to return the totals and ensure my test function returns a string. I'll then proceed to produce the code submission and update the relevant artifacts."
