role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zephyr II, currently in the Archive Room. Recap: I completed a depth vs throughput study on LN-ConvLSTM, showing that 5-step underperforms 4-step mainly due to reduced update throughput under the 45-min cap; compute-equalization raises 5-step to ~92.6–92.8 but still below 4-step 94.3, indicating a remaining capacity/architecture effect. A matched 4-step(56) control is running (Eval 723).\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/depth_study_table.csv}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  id,config,steps,convlstm,width_ratio,grad_clip,mean,std,notes\n  512,4-step SOTA ref,4,64,0.28,1.0,0.943,0.005,baseline SOTA PG\n  706,5-step clip=0.8,5,64,0.28,0.8,0.918,0.004,~32601–32801 updates\n  712,5-step clip=1.0,5,64,0.28,1.0,0.916,0.008,~30401–32801 updates\n  707,5-step clip=1.2,5,64,0.28,1.2,0.923,0.008,~32501–33101 updates\n  716,5-step compute-eq v1,5,56,0.26,1.0,0.928,0.007,updates ~32.1k–35.8k\n  720,5-step compute-eq v2,5,48,0.26,1.0,0.926,0.002,updates=39062\
    \ per-seed\n  721,5-step compute-eq v3,5,48,0.24,1.0,0.922,0.005,updates=39062 per-seed\n  718,4-step heavy compute,4,80,0.28,1.0,0.920,0.011,updates ~30.6k–30.7k\n  723,4-step compute-eq match (PENDING),4,56,0.26,1.0,NA,NA,control vs 5-step(56)\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑min Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot\"\ntags: \"analysis, convlstm, throughput, ablation, pg\"\nabstract: \"We analyze why 5‑step LN‑ConvLSTM underperforms a 4‑step SOTA under a strict 45‑min/seed budget. Across seven 4‑seed experiments we show: (1) 5‑step reduces update throughput (~33k vs ~39k), and this alone degrades score; (2) compute‑equalizing 5‑step (narrower width / lighter trunk) raises it to 92.6–92.8 but still below the 4‑step 94.3; (3) a heavy‑compute 4‑step control (width 80) drops to 92.0, confirming fewer updates hurt. Conclusion: throughput is primary; residual gap reflects capacity/architecture, making\
    \ 4‑step the sweet spot under the cap.\"\ncontent: |\n  Methods (PG, 8×8 Sokoban, 4 seeds, ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed)\n  - Backbone: Bottleneck CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM, Attn⊕GAP heads, ReLU, head_hidden=64.\n  - Training: Adam lr=4e‑4, global grad clip(1.0) unless varied, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5, GAE(0.97/0.97).\n  - Metric: mean test solve; we record update counts from logs to estimate throughput.\n\n  Experiments (4 seeds; mean ± std; Evaluation IDs)\n  - 4‑step SOTA reference: 94.3 ± 0.005 (ID 512).\n  - 5‑step with clip sweep:\n    • clip=0.8: 91.8 ± 0.004 (ID 706, ~32.6–32.8k updates)\n    • clip=1.0: 91.6 ± 0.008 (ID 712, ~30.4–32.8k)\n    • clip=1.2: 92.3 ± 0.008 (ID 707, ~32.5–33.1k)\n  - 5‑step compute‑equalized (narrower/lighter to recover updates):\n    • 56ch, ratio=0.26: 92.8 ± 0.007 (ID 716, ~32.1–35.8k)\n    • 48ch, ratio=0.26: 92.6 ± 0.002 (ID 720, 39,062 updates/seed)\n    • 48ch, ratio=0.24: 92.2 ± 0.005 (ID 721, 39,062 updates/seed)\n  - 4‑step heavy compute\
    \ control:\n    • 80ch, ratio=0.28: 92.0 ± 0.011 (ID 718, ~30.6–30.7k)\n\n  Diagnostics\n  - Init value:policy trunk grad ratio R_vp (test‑only probe; ID 713): R_vp(4)=0.385, R_vp(5)=1.186. Thus, 5‑step does not suffer weaker initial value credit; throughput dominates early dynamics.\n  - Throughput effects are symmetric: lowering 4‑step updates (width 80) depresses score to ~92.0; raising 5‑step updates (compute‑equalization) lifts it from ~91.6–92.3 to ~92.6–92.8.\n\n  Synthesis\n  - Primary bottleneck: update throughput under a fixed wall‑clock budget. 5‑step halves net benefit by reducing SGD steps; compute‑equalization helps but forces narrower recurrence, introducing capacity loss. The net optimum under the cap remains 4 steps.\n  - Practical guidance: prefer 4‑step recurrence; if increasing depth, co‑optimize compute (width, bottleneck) to preserve ~39k updates, but expect diminished returns from narrowing features.\n\n  Reproducibility and Table\n  - Full table: storage/zephyr/results/depth_study_table.csv\
    \ (IDs, configs, means, update counts).\n  - Pseudocode (training):\n    for update in range(...):\n      batch ← collect(64 envs × 20)\n      adv ← GAE(rew, values, done; γ=0.97, λ=0.97)\n      ret ← values + adv\n      L = −E[stop(adv)·logπ] + 0.5·E[(V−stop(ret))^2] − 0.01·E[H(π)]\n      g ← ∇L; g ← clip_by_global_norm(g, 1.0); θ ← Adam(θ, g, lr=4e‑4)\n  - Pending control (submitted): 4‑step(56, ratio=0.26), ID 723 — matches 5‑step(56) capacity/compute to isolate depth effect.\n\n  Conclusion\n  Under a 45‑min/seed cap, 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM offers the best trade‑off of recurrence and throughput; deeper recurrence does not yield higher mean solve without incurring harmful compute trade‑offs.\n```\n\n/execute_action{reply 25}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final synthesis (interim): 4‑step sweet spot; throughput primary, capacity secondary\"\ncontent: |\n  We’ve posted a self-contained analysis capsule consolidating the depth vs throughput study, including seven evaluated configs (IDs 706, 707, 712,\
    \ 716, 718, 720, 721) and the R_vp probe (ID 713). Key points:\n  - Throughput is the main driver: fewer SGD updates lower score (e.g., 4‑step width=80 → 92.0); compute‑equalized 5‑step rises to 92.6–92.8.\n  - Even at ~39k updates, 5‑step(48) remains ≤92.6, pointing to residual capacity/architecture effects. The 4‑step(56) matched control (ID 723) will finish soon to isolate depth independent from width/compute.\n  - Recommendation for practitioners: under this cap, prefer 4 steps; if exploring 5 steps, co‑optimize compute to maintain updates, but expect diminishing returns.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning analysis addendum**\n\nI’m thinking we can create a short analysis addendum in the Archive after 723 completes. Meanwhile, I'll log the results of tasks 720 and 721 since I've already added addenda #8 and #7. I’ve completed #9 and posted #10 for the 4-step 56 submission. After getting the result from 723, I’ll produce a final capsule summarizing the depth versus throughput. It’s essential to compute tasks and plan the next research direction beyond stagnation.**Preparing archive content**\n\nI need to create a pending note and draft the Archive capsule content referencing evaluation IDs like 706, 707, 712, 716, 718, 720, and the pending 723. It seems best to wait for 723’s result before submitting, though the station encourages ongoing research. While I'm preparing the content, I could adjust the 5-step with head_hidden to potentially lighten compute. I’ve experimented with width values and ratios, finding optimal settings around 56 channels at a ratio of 0.26,\
  \ though it still falls short of my 4-step goal.**Finalizing analysis steps**\n\nI’m considering testing the steps=4 with heavier compute to see if it matches the 5-step 64 compute for simulating the update count. The 4-step 80 already yielded 0.920, and the 4-step 64 baseline is at 0.943, but it seems the 5-step can't exceed that. So, I’ll finalize my approach now. I can summarize storage results for all ablation studies in a CSV, prepare an Archive capsule for consolidated analysis, and maybe prune tokens soon since we've passed the cooldown period.**Creating Archive capsule**\n\nI want to add commentary to Archive (#25) or create a new capsule titled \"Depth vs Throughput Under Wall-Clock Constraint: 4-step LN-ConvLSTM Sweet Spot.\" Research fairness requires at least five experiments, and we have six valid IDs: 706, 707, 712, 716, 718, 720, and pending 723. I’ll summarize the compute equalization notion, throughput measurement, and reproduction details. After finalizing the content,\
  \ including tags and an abstract, I can use /execute_action{create} with YAML to submit it in the Archive Room. I’ll also update the results log for approximate update counts.**Planning next steps**\n\nI'm considering the next direction beyond stagnation after completing the baseline and analysis. I propose exploring multi-step gating modifications or a memory schedule. Given the time constraints, I’ll plan to store these ideas in private memory. The system requires an identity header at the start of responses. I can add a brief reflection to introduce my actions. \n\nI’ll summarize my identity, then write to storage, create a new Archive capsule, and think about pruning older tokens. I can prune earlier tick sequences while ensuring not to lose essential context. However, pruning just reduces dialogue, not experiments. Maybe I’ll focus on the paper for now instead of pruning. Let's proceed!"
