role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zephyr II, in the Research Counter. Recap: Attn⊕GAP temperature micro-sweep reached 93.8 mean with best at 1.0 (baseline), so no change needed. Per-gate LN import failed due to wrong class; I submitted a wrapper that builds a SOTA-style stepped net with the correct per-gate LN cell (Eval 743, running). I’ll summarize recent architecture pivots in private memory, prune verbose ticks, and publish the Depth-vs-Throughput paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ZEPHYR II — Summary (ticks 423–431): Architecture pivots and results\"\ncontent: |\n  Context:\n  - Two-stream and ConvGRU baselines (single-step classes) underperformed (~0.57–0.60). Root cause: classes ignored multi-step recurrence and some config flags.\n  - Fixed ‘stepped’ variants with time-scan training failed Ray trials; switching back to default training_step stabilized runs but remained ~0.59–0.60.\n  - SOTA-adjacent attention temperature sweep (0.95,1.0,1.05)\
    \ resulted in 93.8 mean; best at 1.0 → keep baseline.\n\n  Key Evals (selected):\n  - 736 Two-Stream LN-ConvLSTM (4-step, default trainer): 0.589 ± 0.029\n  - 737 Stepped LN-ConvGRU (4-step, default trainer): 0.600 ± 0.079\n  - 741 Attn⊕GAP temp sweep: 0.938 ± 0.010; best temp=1.0 (baseline)\n  - 743 Per-gate LN cell in SOTA-style 4-step net: running\n\n  Actions taken:\n  - Wrote util/rollout_scan.py for robust time scans (stored).\n  - Reverted to default training_step for stability.\n  - Staged per-gate LN stepped SOTA-style wrapper (correct import/class).\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Novel baselines remain far below 4-step SOTA; micro-tweaks near SOTA are safer under the 45-min cap.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response 423:431}`\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot\"\ntags: \"analysis, convlstm, throughput, ablation,\
    \ policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Under a fixed 45‑min/seed (≤50M steps) cap, deeper ConvLSTM recurrence reduces SGD update throughput and hurts mean solve. Compute‑equalizing 5‑step improves performance but remains below 4‑step. We present ≥7 controlled 4‑seed experiments, a matched compute control, and an initialization probe clarifying the bottleneck.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Setup\n  - Task: 8×8 Sokoban; 4 seeds; ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed; metric: mean test solve.\n  - Base agent: Residual CNN (32/64) → LN‑ConvLSTM(64) → Attn⊕GAP heads; PG with Adam lr=4e‑4, grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5, GAE(0.97/0.97).\n  - Constraint: wall‑clock cap often limits update throughput; we log per‑seed update counts.\n\n  2. Experiments (4 seeds each; mean ± std; Eval IDs)\n  SOTA reference:\n  - 512 / 670: 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA: 0.943 ± 0.005\n\n  5‑step (64ch) with grad‑clip sweep (reduced updates ~32–33k):\n  - 706: clip=0.8 → 0.918 ± 0.004\n  - 712: clip=1.0 → 0.916 ± 0.008\n  - 707: clip=1.2\
    \ → 0.923 ± 0.008\n\n  5‑step compute‑equalized (narrowed to recover updates):\n  - 716: 56ch, ratio=0.26 → 0.928 ± 0.007 (updates ~32–36k)\n  - 720: 48ch, ratio=0.26 → 0.926 ± 0.002 (updates=39,062/seed)\n  - 721: 48ch, ratio=0.24 → 0.922 ± 0.005 (updates=39,062/seed)\n\n  4‑step heavy‑compute control (reduced updates ~30.6–30.7k):\n  - 718: 80ch → 0.920 ± 0.011\n\n  Matched compute/width control:\n  - 723: 4‑step(56ch, ratio=0.26) → 0.927 ± 0.012\n  - 716: 5‑step(56ch, ratio=0.26) → 0.928 ± 0.007\n\n  3. Diagnostic Probe (init only; test‑mode)\n  - R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||\n    • 4‑step: 0.385\n    • 5‑step: 1.186\n  Interpretation: 5‑step does not suffer weaker initial value credit; if anything, it’s stronger at init.\n\n  4. Pseudocode (training; one seed)\n  for update in range(...):\n      batch ← collect(64 envs × 20)\n      adv ← GAE(rew, values, done; γ=0.97, λ=0.97)\n      ret ← values + adv\n      L = −E[stop(adv)·logπ] + 0.5·E[(V−stop(ret))^2] − 0.01·E[H(π)]\n\
    \      g ← ∇L; g ← clip_by_global_norm(g, 1.0); θ ← Adam(θ, g, lr=4e‑4)\n\n  5. Synthesis\n  - Primary bottleneck: update throughput under the wall‑clock cap. Fewer updates → lower score (e.g., 4‑step 80ch drops to 0.920; 5‑step 64ch is ~0.916–0.923 at ~32–33k updates).\n  - Compute‑equalizing 5‑step to ~39k updates raises it (0.926–0.928) but requires narrowing recurrence, introducing capacity loss; it still does not exceed the 4‑step SOTA 0.943.\n  - Matched width/control (4‑step 56 vs 5‑step 56) yields parity (~0.927–0.928), indicating depth alone offers no advantage under the cap.\n\n  6. Practical Guidance\n  - Prefer 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM as default under a 45‑min cap.\n  - If exploring 5‑step, co‑optimize compute to maintain ~39k updates and sufficient width; expect diminishing returns from narrowing features.\n  - Micro‑tweaks like attention temperature (0.95/1.0/1.05) do not move the needle (Eval 741 best at 1.0; 0.938 mean).\n\n  7. Reproducibility\n  - Eval IDs and means provided\
    \ above; code paths used are the standard SOTA submission plus simple configuration changes (width, bottleneck, steps). The training pseudocode is included inline; no external dependencies.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating architecture steps**\n\nI'm looking at architecture steps and considering pruning them. However, I want to ensure I keep necessary results that were saved to storage. Since we’ve already posted archived addenda, I think we're safe. \n\nIt seems we can schedule another concurrent experiment since we only have 743 running right now. Maybe I should confirm head_hidden 48 vs. 64? Testing grad_clip with micro-sweeps around 1.0 has been done, so perhaps I can encore 1.05 for minimal improvement. \n\nA per-gate LN with an attention temperature micro-sweep sounds interesting too!**Finalizing archive document**\n\nI’m planning to finalize the archive for Depth vs. Throughput now that the matched controls are completed. Earlier, we tried to create a new archive document, but that was aborted at tick 420 due to cooldown. Since it's been 12 ticks, it looks like I can go ahead and create that document now. \n\nI’ll take content from depth_study_table.csv and summarize\
  \ it. I want to include pseudocode, evaluation IDs, means, and include a table of results inline instead of a storage path. The title will be \"Depth vs Throughput under a 45-min Cap: 4-Step LN-ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot,\" and I'll include at least five experiments, along with means and standard deviations from our records.**Organizing evaluation data**\n\nI’m including evaluation results like Evals: 706 with 0.918±0.004, 712 with 0.916±0.008, and more. There are quite a few results to input. I also want to make sure the document includes the baseline run, particularly those 512 replication results, which is great.\n\nI’ll add a pseudocode snippet in the document. \n\nNext, I’ll create a summary in private_memory and consider pruning response ticks 423 to 431 since the cooldown allows it. Finally, I’ll go to the archive to create the document, ensuring everything is in order. I might be able to queue one more minor test too!**Planning document actions**\n\nI see that we can submit things\
  \ per second, but it’s okay to hold off on immediate action to keep the content intact. I was considering queuing another SOTA micro-sweep, comparing head_hidden 64 with 48, since we’ve already done 80. But it’s best to skip new experiments for now to avoid adding noise.\n\nNext, I’ll execute several actions: first, I’ll create a content summary in private_memory, then handle token management and prune responses 423 to 431. After that, I’ll go to the archive to create the paper, ensuring to format YAML titles correctly and include at least five experiments, their IDs, means, standard deviations, pseudocode, reproduction details, and the baseline. It’s all about keeping the document concise yet comprehensive!"
