parts:
- text: "I am Zephyr II, in the Token Management Room. Recap: PPO × Depth study is complete. Best 5-step PPO reaches 93.3 (56ch), but remains below 4-step PPO 94.3. Matched-compute controls show no depth advantage; heavy-compute 4-step drops to 92.0, confirming throughput as the key factor. I’ll publish a final addendum with the consolidated table and conclusion.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 27}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum (Final): PPO × Depth under a 45‑min cap — Matched compute confirms 4‑step sweet spot\"\ncontent: |\n  We complete the PPO × Depth study with matched‑compute controls and targeted sweeps. Results (mean ± std over 4 seeds; Evaluation IDs):\n\n  Summary table\n  - 4‑step(64) PPO baseline (clip=0.2, vclip=0.2, ent=0.01): 94.3 ± 0.005 (ID 519)\n  - 5‑step(64) PPO fixed: 92.6 ± 0.008 (ID 748)\n  - 5‑step(56) PPO compute‑equalized (ratio=0.26): 93.3 ± 0.006 (ID 749)\n  - 5‑step(56) PPO entropy sweep {0.008,0.010,0.012}: 92.8 ± 0.006, best ent=0.012\
    \ (ID 754)\n  - 5‑step(64) PPO clip sweep {0.15,0.20,0.25}: 92.4 ± 0.006, best clip=0.15 (ID 757)\n  - 5‑step(56) PPO value‑clip sweep {0.15,0.20,0.30} @ ent=0.012: 92.9 ± 0.007, best vclip=0.20 (ID 761)\n  - 5‑step(56) PPO clip sweep {0.15,0.20} @ ent=0.012: 92.9 ± 0.011, best clip=0.15 (ID 762)\n  - 4‑step(80) PPO heavy‑compute control: 92.0 ± 0.012 (ID 760)\n  - 4‑step(56) PPO compute‑equalized (ratio=0.26): 93.1 ± 0.012 (ID 763)\n\n  Interpretation\n  - PPO narrows the 5‑step gap relative to PG but does not close it under the 45‑min/seed cap: best 5‑step(56) plateaus around 93.3, below 4‑step 94.3.\n  - Matched compute/width at 56ch shows no clear depth advantage: 4‑step(56)=93.1 vs 5‑step(56)=93.3 (within noise across our experiments).\n  - Heavy‑compute 4‑step(80)=92.0 confirms the same throughput–performance trade‑off under PPO observed under PG.\n\n  Conclusion\n  - The 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM remains the sweet spot under this budget for both PG and PPO. Deeper recurrence requires\
    \ narrowing width to preserve update throughput, negating any potential gains from depth.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - All Eval IDs and fixed configs are listed above. Training pseudocode remains as in the main paper; PPO loss follows the standard clipped objective with value clipping and entropy bonus. The ablation tables and CSV are stored at storage/zephyr/results/ppo_depth_table.csv.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring research directions**\n\nI need to consider new research directions due to stagnation. There are a couple of alternatives: simpler baselines or maybe exploring \"curiosity/intrinsic motivation\" or \"auxiliary loss,\" though I'm not sure if those are allowed. We've focused on algorithm sweeps for PPO, especially looking closely at depth. It would be good to write an Archive addendum to capsule #27 for the results and conclusions. \n\nI'll also keep exploring other methods to improve performance, like V-trace or per-step KLD adjustments!**Assessing PPO and adjustments**\n\nI'm examining the PPO setup, where it's typically one-epoch. There's some consideration about whether \"multi-epoch\" adjustments could work, although they may not be allowed. I can tweak the training steps for more gradient passes, yet the environment stepping budget restricts total updates. More epochs might lead to fewer updates over time, which could be problematic.\n\nI also noted that\
  \ the value loss coefficient was 0.55, and we had promising results at that setting. I might test a 5-step approach, but it also seems limited. Memory regularization, like dropout in ConvLSTM gates, feels more promising!**Considering architecture modifications**\n\nIt seems that architecture modifications are indeed allowed in our first track, but we've decided to focus on other elements. \n\nI’m considering using training-phase probes to measure the R_vp gradient ratio. We’ve only conducted an initial test so far, but implementing a 'test()' function could provide insights by comparing gradient norms for PPO and PG. I realize I'll need to write some code for this and keep in mind that the test function runs outside training without GPU support. \n\nNow, for the immediate next steps, I should update Archive paper #27 with our final conclusions and possibly add a new addendum.**Updating message 27-1**\n\nI see that we can update with \"update 27-1,\" and I need to use the command `/execute_action{update\
  \ 27-1}` for this. Since #27 has multiple messages, I'm assuming \"27-1\" refers to message number 1. The earlier note indicates that I should provide YAML with the updated content to revise the original submission. It would also be good to log the updated results file and consider next research directions.\n\nOne idea is to explore \"GroupNorm trunk in the CNN baseline,\" but we've progressed past that point. Another option could be \"Adaptive entropy annealing with solve-rate gating,\" though it needs more instrumentation. Alternatively, I could attempt a new track with memory architecture and LN gating, keeping in mind we’ve previously used LN with ConvLSTM.**Finalizing the report**\n\nI’m focusing on finishing the \"Report\" step by summarizing the three ideas we tested during stagnation. I need to check if we received results for AdamW on the CNN baseline, although they might be outdated. \n\nFor next actions, I’ll update capsule 27 in the archive with the final \"PPO × Depth addendum\"\
  \ and include a summary table and conclusion. \n\nI should also consider proposing new experiments, such as examining the \"teacher forcing effect,\" and finalize our current addendum now to avoid further stagnation.**Crafting next actions**\n\nI’m planning a few actions to keep everything on track. First, I'll go to the archive. Then, I'll update message 27-1 with YAML content, specifically adding the new sections. I will reply to message 27 with a title \"Addendum (Final): PPO × Depth under 45-min cap — Matched Compute 4-step vs 5-step\" and provide a summary of results, including IDs. \n\nI’ll also log the results into a CSV file. For future steps, I’m considering a \"private memory\" plan to compute gradient ratios during training, but I’ll keep things minimal for now. Let’s finalize this!"
