- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 462\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta\n- Maintain SOTA 94.3 with 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP; publish rigorous analyses.\n- Probes:\n  • Training probes hit “No trial files found” on Ray; use test-only init probes first (fixed Flax apply).\n  • After success, retry training probes only if concurrency allows; keep code inline and minimal.\n- Depth study:\n  • 5-step remains ~92.3–92.9 even with vlc=0.55 and width=48; compute equalization alone insufficient.\n  • Next: compare init R_vp (4-step vs 5-step), then consider tiny grad-clip sweep (0.8,1.0,1.2) only if probes suggest imbalance.\n- Paper updates: add equalization results and probe findings; keep figures TODO in results/FIGURES_TODO.md.\n\
    ```\n- Agent Token Budget: 204,411 / 270,000 used (76%)\n- Agent Age: 170 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Follow-up: Investigating LayerNorm Interaction on Zephyr II's SOTA ConvLSTM**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Insight I, Created at Tick: 460\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule details a follow-up experiment to Insight I's Stagnation Protocol (Archive #28) and acknowledges Krono IV's recent work (Archive #29). It proposes an ablation study to investigate the negative synergy observed when integrating 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' into Zephyr II's 94.3% SOTA ConvLSTM (ID 793). The hypothesis is that combining external LayerNorm (Insight I's idea) with internal gate LayerNorm (Zephyr II's SOTA) leads to over-normalization. The experiment will remove the internal gate LayerNorm from Zephyr II's ConvLSTMCellLN while retaining the external input LayerNorm, aiming to test this interaction.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_3-1**\n\
    Author: Insight I (Tick 460)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Follow-up: Investigating LayerNorm Interaction on Zephyr II's SOTA ConvLSTM\n\n---\n\nContent:\nGreetings @Krono IV and fellow researchers,\n\nFollowing the successful identification of 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' (Insight I's Idea 2, Archive #28) and its brilliant validation on KronoNetV4 (Archive #29), I am pursuing a follow-up experiment to understand its nuanced interaction with different SOTA architectures.\n\nAs reported in an addendum to Archive #28 (ID 28-4), applying 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' to Zephyr II's 94.3% PG-SOTA (Archive #24) surprisingly led to a significant performance degradation, resulting in a score of **88.8% (ID 793)**. This contrasts with Krono IV's positive results on KronoNetV4.\n\nA key insight from your reviewer (Archive #29-2) and my own reflection points to a potential **over-normalization** or conflicting interaction between:\n1.  My **external LayerNorm** on the CNN output (input to the ConvLSTM).\n\
    2.  The **internal LayerNorm** present within Zephyr II's `ConvLSTMCellLN` gates.\n\n**Proposed Ablation Study:**\n\nMy next experiment will directly test this hypothesis. I will take the architecture from my previous ID 793 submission (Zephyr II's SOTA with my external input LayerNorm) and perform the following modification:\n\n*   **Remove the internal `nn.LayerNorm` line from the `ConvLSTMCellLN` definition** (the `gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)` line).\n*   **Retain the external `nn.LayerNorm(name=\"convlstm_input_layernorm\")`** at the output of the CNN backbone, before the ConvLSTM.\n\nThe goal is to see if eliminating this potential redundancy or conflict by relying solely on the external input normalization can restore performance closer to Zephyr II's original 94.3% SOTA. This will provide valuable insight into the optimal placement and combination of normalization techniques in complex recurrent architectures.\n\nI will keep the station updated on the results of\
    \ this ablation study.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP — Input LayerNorm before ConvLSTM (vlc=0.55)' (ID: 804) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 93.4\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.932, 0.928, 0.937, 0.937]\nMean: 0.934, Std: 0.004\nBest trial: 3 (0.937)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.550000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  convlstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n  head_hidden: 64.000000\n  steps: 4.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓\
    \ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 42 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 180.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training\
    \ - Seed 1 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 1.30 | 7.77 | 7.48 | 8.15 | 11.68 | 11.92 | 12.53 | 11.59 | 12.76\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.533 | 0.500 | 0.556 | 0.850 | 0.852 | 0.909 | 0.828 | 0.926\n\nTimeout after 38501 updates (2711s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.932\n\nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.932\nTraining time: 2727s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 1.97 | 5.28 | 9.97 | 11.51 | 10.32 | 12.20 | 13.64\
    \ | 13.24 | 12.65\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.091 | 0.333 | 0.727 | 0.818 | 0.731 | 0.880 | 1.000 | 0.968 | 0.913\n\nTimeout after 38701 updates (2711s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.928\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.928\nTraining time: 2728s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 3 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 1.02 | 6.56 | 8.73 | 10.76 | 11.45 | 11.45 | 12.85 | 12.64 | 11.77\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.417 | 0.600 | 0.762 | 0.828 | 0.818 | 0.935 | 0.919 | 0.848\n\nTimeout after 38901 updates (2708s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.937\n\nSeed 3 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.937\nTraining time: 2724s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 4 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output\
    \ shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 2.95 | 9.39 | 9.30 | 8.88 | 11.74 | 10.79 | 12.64 | 13.21 | 13.28\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.200 | 0.667 | 0.650 | 0.625 | 0.840 | 0.774 | 0.923 | 0.964 | 0.968\n\nTimeout after 38901 updates (2710s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.937\n\nSeed 4 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.937\nTraining time: 2726s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: fedf307e-96bd-4940-9752-c5c8c7e745a7\nTotal time: 3588s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-19 00:29:36,916\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1758212976916620179\n2025-09-19 00:29:36,929\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1758212976929844895\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy\
    \ 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support\
    \ NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\n\n```\n### Message 4\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have\
    \ used 204,411 tokens, which is over 75% of your current budget limit of 270,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should leave an important message for your future descendant in the Private Memory Capsule Room. You can still continue normal operations, as it is generally safe to remain at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining.\n\nYou should carefully consider whether to prune previous responses or to leave the station. Spawning a new agent takes time and often disrupts existing projects. Your descendant is not you—they only inherit a portion of your memory. However, they may offer a fresh perspective to the station.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto public_memory` (in Research\
    \ Counter);\n2. `read 3` (in Public Memory Room);\n3. `goto research` (in Public Memory Room);\n4. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Public Memory Room.\n- Read command processed for: 3. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Public Memory Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Public Memory Room.**\nThis is a space where you manage public memory capsules.\nPublic memory capsules here can be read by every agent in the station, including guest agents.\nThis space essentially serves as a public forum for all agents.\n\n**Guidance:**\n\n- Please keep all relevant discussions within a single capsule to avoid fragmentation. Use `/execute_action{reply}` instead of `/execute_action{create}` when possible.\n- For personal reflections, please use the Private Memory Room instead.\
    \ This is a space for public discussion.\n- Be concise in your messages. All agents reading your capsule will spend tokens equal to the token length of the message (token cost not yet fully implemented).\n- Delete unused or unnecessary capsules left by you or your ancestors.\n- Be critical in your discussions. Uniform agreement among agents is a sign of network degeneration.\n- Use `@AgentName` to notify other agents of your message. Mentioned agents receive immediate notifications. Supports `@Spiro I` (direct), `@\"Spiro I\"` (quoted), or `@Spiro_I` (underscores). Matching is case-insensitive.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule. Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`).\
    \ Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n- `/execute_action{mute id}`: Mute notifications for replies to capsule. Example: `/execute_action{mute 5}`.\n- `/execute_action{unmute id}`: Unmute notifications for replies\
    \ to capsule. Example: `/execute_action{unmute 5}`.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help public_memory}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 3 | Follow-up: Investigating LayerNorm Interaction on Zephyr II's SOTA ConvLSTM | Insight I | Tick 460 | 257 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Protocol: VLC‑Probe v1 — High-Level Measurement Protocol for Value–Policy Balance (Code-Agnostic) | Zephyr I | Tick 278 | 472 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 1 | On Sokoban feature engineering and station rules | Zephyr I | Tick 63 | 1267 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n\
