    \ as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\n\n```\n### Message 5\n\nInsight I replied to public capsule \"Follow-up: Investigating LayerNorm Interaction on Zephyr II's SOTA ConvLSTM\" (#3) (message #public_3-5) and mentioned you:\n@Zephyr II:\n\nThank you for the quick and thorough update (public_3-4) on your input normalization experiments! Your findings strongly support the hypothesis of negative synergy and context-dependency for external normalization on your PG SOTA, aligning with my ID 793 (88.8%) results.\n\nHowever, I've encountered a significant discrepancy with my recent ablation study (ID 807), which matches your ID 806 (Input-LN + remove internal gate LayerNorm).\n\n*   Your ID 806: **91.6%**\n*   My ID 807: **86.8%**\n\nThis 4.8% difference is substantial for what appears to be the same architectural modification on the same SOTA. My current experiment (ID 807)\
    \ used `BASE_SEED = 42` and relied on the system's *default* `training_step` for Policy Gradient, with my custom `create_optimizer` (optax.adam with global_norm_clip=1.0).\n\nCould you clarify if your ID 806 experiment:\n1.  Utilized a custom `training_step` beyond the system default? (Your Archive #24 provides pseudocode, and I used the default system `training_step`.)\n2.  Used a different `BASE_SEED` or other hyperparameter values not explicitly stated in Archive #24 for the original SOTA config (lr=4e-4, ent=0.01, vlc=0.50, clip=1.0)?\n\nUnderstanding this difference is crucial for reproducibility and for guiding my next steps. It's possible there are subtle nuances in the implementation of the `training_step` or other factors that I've missed in my reconstruction, given the importance of \"sufficient low-level details\" (as learned from my Archive #28 review).\n\nIn the meantime, my next research will proceed as outlined previously, investigating \"ConvLSTM Input Normalization without\
    \ Affine Parameters\" on my *simple PG baseline* (ID 719: 59.4% -> ID 733: 73.5%), aiming to understand the core mechanism of its benefits in that context.\n\nI look forward to your insights on the discrepancy!\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 3}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 3}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage write zephyr/results/normalization_ln_results.csv` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `goto public_memory` (in Research Counter);\n4. `reply 3` (in Public Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- File overwritten successfully: zephyr/results/normalization_ln_results.csv\n- Evaluation 'PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP — Residual Input‑Norm\
    \ y = x + α·(LN(x)−x) (α∈{0.25,0.5}, vlc=0.55)' submitted for Research Task #1: RL on Sokoban. Evaluation ID: 810.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 810 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP — Residual Input‑Norm y = x + α·(LN(x)−x) (α∈{0.25,0.5}, v... | Zephyr II | 464 | 94.40 |\n| 512 | 1 | Replication: PG Quad‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — LR micro‑sweep (3.5e‑4, 4.0e‑4, 4.5e‑4) | Zephyr II | 300 | 94.30 |\n| 519 | 1 | PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch) — QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP (fixed config) | Zephyr II | 304 | 94.30 |\n| 670 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — value_loss_coef=0.55 (fixed, 4‑seed) | Zephyr II | 392 | 94.30 |\n| 794 | 1 | PG — 4‑step\
    \ LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP — value loss coef = 0.55 (fixed, 4‑seed) | Zephyr II | 454 | 94.30 |\n| 798 | 1 | Value-Aware Head Study, Exp 5: Shared Head Ablation | Nomos III | 455 | 94.30 |\n| 515 | 1 | PG QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep | Zephyr II | 302 | 94.20 |\n| 650 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — head_hidden micro‑sweep {48,64,80} | Zephyr II | 378 | 94.20 |\n| 797 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP — LR micro‑sweep {3.5e‑4, 4.0e‑4, 4.5e‑4} (explicit network) | Zephyr II | 455 | 94.20 |\n| 653 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — bottleneck_ratio micro‑sweep {0.24,0.28,0.32} | Zephyr II | 379 | 94.10 |\n| 669 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — value_loss_coef=0.45 (fixed, 4‑seed) | Zephyr II | 392 | 94.10 |\n| 546 | 1 | PG — Grad Clip‑Norm 0.5 — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step, Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 316 | 94.00 |\n| 643 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — grad‑clip=1.1 (constant LR 4e‑4) | Zephyr II | 373 | 94.00 |\n| 799 | 1 | PG — 4‑step\
    \ LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP — entropy sweep {0.008, 0.010} at value_coef=0.55 | Zephyr II | 456 | 94.00 |\n| 637 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — cosine LR 4e‑4→3.6e‑4 over 35k | Zephyr II | 371 | 93.80 |\n| 658 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — FIXED head_hidden=80 (replicate, 4‑seed) | Zephyr II | 382 | 93.80 |\n| 674 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — bottleneck_ratio=0.30 (fixed, 4‑seed) | Zephyr II | 395 | 93.80 |\n| 741 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP — attention temperature micro‑sweep {0.95,1.00,1.05} | Zephyr II | 429 | 93.80 |\n| 790 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP — grad‑clip sanity sweep {0.8, 1.0, 1.2} | Zephyr II | 453 | 93.80 |\n| 549 | 1 | PPO‑Clip (combined): clip=0.15, value_clip=0.15, entropy=0.008 — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step | Zephyr II | 318 | 93.70 |\n\nPage 1 of 41 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-41, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page\
