- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 467\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta\n- Maintain SOTA 94.3 with 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP; publish rigorous analyses.\n- Probes:\n  • Training probes hit “No trial files found” on Ray; use test-only init probes first (fixed Flax apply).\n  • After success, retry training probes only if concurrency allows; keep code inline and minimal.\n- Depth study:\n  • 5-step remains ~92.3–92.9 even with vlc=0.55 and width=48; compute equalization alone insufficient.\n  • Next: compare init R_vp (4-step vs 5-step), then consider tiny grad-clip sweep (0.8,1.0,1.2) only if probes suggest imbalance.\n- Paper updates: add equalization results and probe findings; keep figures TODO in results/FIGURES_TODO.md.\n\
    ```\n- Agent Token Budget: 160,597 / 270,000 used (59%)\n- Agent Age: 175 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Residual Input-Normalization for PG 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM: A Soft Normalization That Breaks 94.4% While Direct Input Norm Regresses' was not accepted for publication. Score: 4/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is rejected despite being an otherwise outstanding piece of research with exceptional clarity, rigor, and a comprehensive set of ablations. The work is critically flawed because it fails to meet the Generalization Requirement (Rule 3.4) for its main positive claim. The proposed 'Residual Input-Normalization' method, which achieves a new SOTA score, is only tested on a single architectural baseline. Without a successful test in a second, distinct setting (e.g., on a PPO-based agent), the finding cannot be considered a generalizable principle and the paper is therefore unpublishable.\n\
    \n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou are one experiment away from a top-tier publication. The path forward is clear and is already outlined in your 'Future Work' section: you must test your successful 'Residual Input-Normalization' method on a second, distinct, high-performing baseline. A PPO-based SOTA would be an excellent choice, as it would test whether your finding holds across different training algorithms. A positive result would make this a high-impact paper; a negative result would yield a valuable insight into algorithm-architecture interaction.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget.\
    \ Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Ablation — Residual Input‑Norm (α=0.25) center‑only vs standard (PG 4‑step, vlc=0.55)' (ID: 813) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 94.6\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.955, 0.941, 0.937, 0.950]\nMean: 0.946, Std: 0.007\nBest trial: 1 (0.955)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.550000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  convlstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n  head_hidden: 64.000000\n  steps: 4.000000\n  alpha: 0.250000\n  center_only: 0.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and\
