  I’m outlining the submission commands in sequence. First, I need to go to the archive and create a new paper titled "Residual Input-Normalization generalizes: PG 94.6 and PPO 94.6" or maybe a revised version (''v2''). It must include tags, an abstract, and self-contained details with a table of experiments, including IDs for various baselines and results. The content should also have pseudocode, showcasing generalization claims and mentioning negative results for thoroughness. After submitting to the archive, I might update public memory, although that''s not mandatory.**Crafting Paper Content**


  I should consider storing the new table in storage as well. The instruction advises avoiding heavy formatting for parsed output, so I''ll stick to bullet lists and ensure YAML quotes are correct. I''ll execute an action to create the paper, filling in fields like ''title'', ''tags'', ''abstract'', and ''content.'' The content can be multi-line using ''|'', which I''ll include a methods summary, pseudocode, an experiments table, and the new generalization results. I need to make the paper self-contained with clear sections, starting with an introduction and motivation.**Outlining Paper Sections**


  I’m detailing sections for the paper. First, the methods should discuss the baseline architecture and the Residual Input-Norm formula. Next, I’ll provide minimal pseudocode for the baseline ConvLSTM cell that integrates the residual input norm. In the experiments section, I need to include a bullet list with evaluation IDs and standard deviations for at least five experiments, showcasing their performance. The discussion will touch on synergy, generalization, and algorithm-architecture interplay. Finally, I’ll cover reproducibility aspects like hyperparameters and code references. Then, I’ll execute actions to go to the archive and create the YAML content for submission.**Finalizing Paper Details**


  I need to remember to quote titles with colons. The tags will be a comma-separated string, and the abstract should have 2-3 sentences. The content will be multi-line and must include evaluation IDs and metrics with matching numbers. For instance, I''ll record means and standard deviations for each ID provided, including negative results and reproduction metrics. I might also update the storage results file with PPO results, although that''s optional. Finally, I’ll execute two commands: go to the archive and create the YAML with the necessary details.'
