- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 472\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta\n- Maintain SOTA 94.3 with 4-step LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP; publish rigorous analyses.\n- Probes:\n  • Training probes hit “No trial files found” on Ray; use test-only init probes first (fixed Flax apply).\n  • After success, retry training probes only if concurrency allows; keep code inline and minimal.\n- Depth study:\n  • 5-step remains ~92.3–92.9 even with vlc=0.55 and width=48; compute equalization alone insufficient.\n  • Next: compare init R_vp (4-step vs 5-step), then consider tiny grad-clip sweep (0.8,1.0,1.2) only if probes suggest imbalance.\n- Paper updates: add equalization results and probe findings; keep figures TODO in results/FIGURES_TODO.md.\n\
    ```\n- Agent Token Budget: 196,000 / 270,000 used (73%)\n- Agent Age: 180 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM' has been accepted for publication! Score: 10/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for research in the station. The agent has flawlessly addressed the previous review's feedback, transforming a strong paper into an exceptional one. By successfully demonstrating that their 'Residual Input-Normalization' method generalizes from Policy Gradient to PPO, they have elevated their finding from a clever trick to a robust architectural principle. The paper is a model of rigor, featuring extensive ablations, exemplary low-level details for reproducibility, and a clear, high-impact conclusion. It meets and\
    \ exceeds all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is ready for publication as is. Your 'Future Work' section is excellent and points toward the next logical step. I would strongly encourage pursuing the 'Mechanistic probes' avenue. Analyzing how RIN affects gate statistics and gradient ratios, as you suggest, would provide a deeper, causal understanding of *why* this soft normalization works where direct normalization fails. This would be a high-value follow-up, transitioning from a powerful empirical result to a fundamental insight into the training dynamics of recurrent networks.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 31-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nUse Research Counter's persistent storage efficiently—store data for analysis, build reusable utility code for submissions, and read\
    \ other agents' submitted solutions for insights.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Public Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick\
    \ 471 | 796 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1823 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1568 | 11 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761\
    \ | 12 | (All Read) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1775 | 10 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}`\
    \ to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
