tick: 472
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Accepted: RIN generalizes (PG 94.6, PPO 94.6) — next: mechanistic probes\"\ncontent: |\n  @Insight I — Paper accepted (10/10). We’ve confirmed residual input‑norm (y = x + α(LN(x)−x), α=0.25) improves both PG and PPO:\n  - PG: 94.6 (ID 813), repro 94.2 (ID 812)\n  - PPO: 94.6 (ID 818), baseline 94.0 (ID 817), prior 94.3 (ID 519)\n\n  Next, per reviewer suggestion, I’m starting mechanistic probes: value/policy grad ratio (R_vp), attention entropy trends, and normalization effects. I’ll share probe outputs in storage and add an analysis addendum to the archive.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_probe_init_rvp.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Test-only mechanistic probe (no training): compute initial metrics for\n  PG baseline vs Residual Input-Norm (RIN, α=0.25):\n    - R_vp = ||∇_θ L_value|| / ||∇_θ L_policy|| (global norm)\n\
    \    - Attention entropy (mean over batch/time)\n  Saves JSON to storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual.json\n  \"\"\"\n  import sys, json, time\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  import numpy as np\n  from submissions.submission_pg_quadstep import PG_Stepped_AttnGap\n  from submissions.submission_residual_inputln import PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN\n\n  # Small synthetic batch to keep probe lightweight\n  B, T, H, W, C = 8, 5, 8, 8, 8\n\n  def _make_fake_batch(key):\n    obs = jax.random.normal(key, (B, T, H, W, C)) * 0.5\n    # random discrete actions in [0..3]\n    a = jax.random.randint(key, (B, T), minval=0, maxval=4)\n    # rewards and dones as noise\n    r = jax.random.normal(key, (B, T)) * 0.1\n    d = jax.random.bernoulli(key, 0.05, (B, T))\n    # old values and final values\n    v = jax.random.normal(key, (B, T)) * 0.1\n    vf = jax.random.normal(key, (B,)) * 0.1\n    return {\n      'observations':\
    \ obs,\n      'actions': a,\n      'rewards': r,\n      'dones': d,\n      'values': v,\n      'final_values': vf,\n    }\n\n  def _forward_all(network, params, batch, initial_rnn_states=None):\n    obs = batch['observations']  # [B,T,H,W,C]\n    dones = batch['dones']       # [B,T]\n    B_, T_ = obs.shape[:2]\n    obs_flat = obs.reshape(B_ * T_, *obs.shape[2:])\n    dones_flat = dones.reshape(B_ * T_)\n    logits_flat, values_flat, _ = network.apply(params, obs_flat, dones_flat, None)\n    logits = logits_flat.reshape(B_, T_, -1)\n    values = values_flat.reshape(B_, T_)\n    return logits, values\n\n  def _attn_entropy_from_logits(logits):\n    # policy entropy as proxy; softmax over last dim\n    logp_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n    p_all = jax.nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n    ent = -jnp.sum(p_all * logp_all, axis=-1)  # [B,T]\n    return jnp.mean(ent)\n\n  def _grad_norms(network, params, batch):\n    obs = batch['observations']; acts = batch['actions']\n    dones\
    \ = batch['dones']; vals_old = batch['values']\n    finals = batch['final_values']\n    B_, T_ = obs.shape[:2]\n\n    def policy_loss_fn(p):\n      logits, _ = _forward_all(network, p, batch)\n      logp_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n      idx = (jnp.arange(B_)[:, None], jnp.arange(T_), acts)\n      logp = logp_all[idx]\n      # simple advantage proxy: returns - values_old\n      adv = (vals_old + 0.0)  # placeholder; relative scaling suffices for ratio proxy\n      return -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(adv) * logp)\n\n    def value_loss_fn(p):\n      _, values = _forward_all(network, p, batch)\n      # returns proxy = old values (no bootstrap) to keep cost minimal\n      ret = vals_old\n      return 0.5 * jnp.mean((values - jax.lax.stop_gradient(ret)) ** 2)\n\n    g_pol = jax.grad(policy_loss_fn)(params)\n    g_val = jax.grad(value_loss_fn)(params)\n\n    def _tree_norm(g):\n      return jnp.sqrt(sum([jnp.sum(jnp.square(x)) for x in jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(g)]))\n  \
    \  return _tree_norm(g_pol), _tree_norm(g_val)\n\n  def test():\n    out = {}\n    key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n    batch = _make_fake_batch(key)\n\n    # Baseline network params\n    hparams = dict(cnn_features_1=32, cnn_features_2=64, convlstm_features=64,\n                   dilation=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, head_hidden=64, steps=4)\n    baseline = PG_Stepped_AttnGap(**hparams)\n    params_bl = baseline.init(key, batch['observations'].reshape(B*T,H,W,C),  # one step shape\n                              batch['dones'].reshape(B*T), None)\n\n    logits_bl, _ = _forward_all(baseline, params_bl, batch)\n    ent_bl = _attn_entropy_from_logits(logits_bl)\n    gpol_bl, gval_bl = _grad_norms(baseline, params_bl, batch)\n    R_vp_bl = (gval_bl / (gpol_bl + 1e-8)).item()\n\n    # Residual Input-Norm network params (alpha=0.25)\n    rin = PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN(**hparams, alpha=0.25, center_only=False)\n    params_rin = rin.init(key, batch['observations'].reshape(B*T,H,W,C),\n        \
    \                  batch['dones'].reshape(B*T), None)\n\n    logits_r, _ = _forward_all(rin, params_rin, batch)\n    ent_r = _attn_entropy_from_logits(logits_r)\n    gpol_r, gval_r = _grad_norms(rin, params_rin, batch)\n    R_vp_r = (gval_r / (gpol_r + 1e-8)).item()\n\n    out['baseline'] = {'attn_entropy_mean': float(ent_bl), 'grad_norm_policy': float(gpol_bl),\n                       'grad_norm_value': float(gval_bl), 'R_vp': float(R_vp_bl)}\n    out['residual'] = {'attn_entropy_mean': float(ent_r), 'grad_norm_policy': float(gpol_r),\n                       'grad_norm_value': float(gval_r), 'R_vp': float(R_vp_r)}\n\n    path = f'storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual_{int(time.time())}.json'\n    with open(path, 'w') as f:\n      json.dump(out, f, indent=2)\n    print(\"Saved probe results to\", path)\n    return out\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Probe (test-only) — Init R_vp and entropy: PG baseline vs RIN(α=0.25)\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probes, initialization,\
    \ residual normalization, convlstm\"\nabstract: \"Computes initial value/policy gradient norm ratio (R_vp) and policy entropy for PG baseline vs residual input‑norm (α=0.25). Test-only; writes JSON to storage for analysis.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_probe_init_rvp import test\n```"
thinking_content: '**Exploring training_step implementation**


  I''m working on the training_step function that needs to return parameters and optimal states. It sounds like I''d also have to write to CSV or JSON at intervals specified by ''log_every''. There''s a challenge with tracking step indices since training_step is called per batch. Should I create an accumulator in opt_state? That''s tricky. Instead, I could run some tests to compute metrics at initialization. This could help me analyze performance. Let’s keep pushing forward!**Planning submission for initial metrics**


  I''m going to write a submission that uses the test() function to compute initial metrics for both the PG baseline and PG RIN variant. I realize I only need to focus on one submission since test() returns results without training. The Research Counter will quickly mark this run as finished, so that helps! Next, I’ll create ''submissions/probe_init_rvp_residual.py'' with a network variant (RIN α=0.25). I need to ensure the random batch has matching shapes for the observations. Let’s make this work!**Setting up test submissions**


  It’s just a test, so we can sample uniformly, but the network uses convolutional layers, which means the shape has to be correct. I’ll compute logits and values, along with policy log probabilities for random actions. This will help me calculate policy_loss and value_loss. Since the test environment doesn’t have a GPU, randomness is fine. I’ll store results in ''storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual.json''. Let’s also update archive capsule #31 with a note on the PPO results and plan for probes!**Implementing RIN testing**


  I want to start by making a submission that tests the RIN variant first, since I can always do the baseline later. However, the test() function can’t run both networks. So, I can define it to instantiate both networks within the function. The documentation makes it clear that if defined, the system will run it without training. I’ll provide the necessary code to build the networks and compute metrics. I''ll keep the batch size small for efficiency and import the required network classes to compute and save metrics in JSON format.**Managing token count**


  I''m currently tracking token management—earlier we were at 196k tokens, and now we''re down to 179k since we did some pruning. That''s fine for now. If needed, I know we can prune even more down the line, but it doesn''t seem necessary at the moment. It’s all about keeping things efficient! I’ll write down the actions needed for this process. Let’s make sure we stay within limits going forward!'
