role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage read zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual_1758234762.json}\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_summary.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Probe: Initialization metrics — PG baseline vs Residual Input-Norm (α=0.25)\n  Source: storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual_1758234762.json\n\n  Results (means over synthetic batch/time):\n  - Baseline:\n    • Policy entropy ≈ 1.384\n    • ||∇policy|| ≈ 0.0781\n    • ||∇value||  ≈ 0.1104\n    • R_vp = ||∇value|| / ||∇policy|| ≈ 1.414\n  - Residual Input-Norm (α=0.25):\n    • Policy entropy ≈ 1.386\n    • ||∇policy|| ≈ 0.0537\n    • ||∇value||  ≈ 0.0310\n    • R_vp ≈ 0.577\n\n  Takeaway (init-only, test mode):\n  - RIN lowers initial gradient magnitudes and shifts the value-to-policy gradient ratio downward (R_vp from ~1.41 to ~0.58) while keeping policy entropy similar. This suggests a more policy-dominant initial regime and potentially cleaner optimization dynamics, aligning with the observed\
    \ training gains under both PG and PPO.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 31}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Initialization probe — R_vp and policy entropy under RIN\"\ncontent: |\n  We ran a test-only mechanistic probe at initialization (no training) comparing the PG baseline and Residual Input‑Norm (α=0.25). The script computes:\n    • R_vp = ||∇_θ L_value|| / ||∇_θ L_policy|| (global norm ratio)\n    • Policy entropy (mean over batch/time)\n\n  Results (storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual_*.json):\n  - Baseline: R_vp ≈ 1.41; policy entropy ≈ 1.384\n  - RIN(α=0.25): R_vp ≈ 0.58; policy entropy ≈ 1.386\n\n  Interpretation:\n  - RIN notably reduces the initial value-to-policy gradient ratio while keeping policy entropy similar, indicating a more policy‑dominant gradient balance at init. This aligns with our empirical observation that RIN improves solve rates for both PG and PPO. Next, we will extend probes to early/mid training checkpoints to study\
    \ how RIN shapes gradient balance over time.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PG — RIN α fine‑sweep {0.20, 0.25, 0.33} — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), vlc=0.55\"\ntags: \"policy gradient, residual normalization, alpha sweep, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Refine α for Residual Input‑Norm on the PG SOTA: α ∈ {0.20,0.25,0.33}. Fixed settings: lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.55, grad‑clip=1.0, steps=4; LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from submissions.submission_residual_inputln import PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n        \
    \  'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'alpha': tune.choice([0.20, 0.25, 0.33]),\n          'center_only': tune.choice([False]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n          alpha=float(hparams['alpha']),\n          center_only=bool(hparams['center_only']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\
    \ optax.adam(learning_rate))\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PG — RIN depth interaction: steps ∈ {3,4,5} — LN‑ConvLSTM(64), α=0.25, vlc=0.55\"\ntags: \"policy gradient, residual normalization, depth, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Test Residual Input‑Norm across recurrence depth: steps ∈ {3,4,5} at α=0.25 with fixed PG settings (lr=4e‑4, ent=0.01, value_coef=0.55, grad‑clip=1.0) on LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from submissions.submission_residual_inputln import PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      \
    \    'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([3, 4, 5]),\n          'alpha': tune.choice([0.25]),\n          'center_only': tune.choice([False]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n          alpha=float(hparams['alpha']),\n          center_only=bool(hparams['center_only']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering token management**\n\nI'm thinking about adding a mechanistic probe and possibly a training-phase variant for testing. Currently, my token usage is at 199,400 out of 270,000, which is about 74%. We're getting close to that 75% threshold. The system previously indicated we'd need to prune again soon since we've passed the cooldown period. Although we could prune proactively to manage tokens, it's not strictly necessary yet according to the initial instructions, which suggest pruning beyond 75%.**Updating archive capsule**\n\nI've noted that our progress went from 73% to 74%, and I'm considering if we can do even better. I think we should perform several actions: first, I'll read the saved probe JSON from storage, then write a concise analysis and update archive capsule 31. The instruction indicates that I should execute an update using the message ID 31-1. I could add a section for the \"Mechanistic probe (init)\" and modify the YAML content. Alternatively,\
  \ I could reply to the archive capsule with an addendum message.**Evaluating next steps**\n\nI’m considering whether it’s better to reply rather than just update, as the tip suggests replying is acceptable. It seems like I should also update public memory with a quick summary. For the next mechanistic steps, running a \"test()\" only utilizes the CPU, so we’re limited to init-level tests; a more in-depth \"forward step\" scan could compute attention entropy across time, but it’s not a priority right now. I’m weighing the idea of adding depth interactions with a 5-step approach using RIN, though past attempts haven't performed well. Maybe we could queue an experiment with \"PG 5-step and residual input norm alpha 0.25.\"**Planning experiments**\n\nIt seems like a good idea to run PG 3-step and 5-step RIN tests to map depth interactions, as promised. The compute constraints require these to run for a standard 45 minutes, which is manageable. I’ll perform micro-sweep steps with RIN alpha\
  \ set to 0.25, using PG settings and four replicates as a baseline. Although we know that 4-step is the state of the art, testing helps inform future work. Additionally, I might conduct a finer alpha sweep for PG with values like 0.2, 0.25, and 0.33. I’ll also log a short summary of probe results into storage by reading the JSON file, though I need to note the exact path with the timestamp. I’ve noted the storage path to use for reading, which is storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual_1758234762.json.**Planning action steps**\n\nI can read the JSON file to display its content in the output. Then, I should write a summary file and update the archive. My action plan includes:  \n1) Reading the JSON path to show results.  \n2) Writing a summary markdown file with R_vp numbers and entropies.  \n3) Replying to capsule 31 with an addendum that summarizes the probe.  \n4) Submitting a PG experiment to test depth with RIN at alpha=0.25, across steps 3, 4, and 5.  \n\nI'll maintain concurrency\
  \ and submit two experiments concurrently: one for the PG depth sweep and one for the PG alpha fine sweep with values {0.2, 0.25, 0.33}. Let's craft the code to ensure it aligns with the necessary parameters, specifying the title appropriately."
