- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/ppo_residual_wrapper.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # PPO residual input-normalization wrapper: reuse proven PPO training_step, override network only\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from submissions.submission_ppo_clip import (\n      training_step, create_optimizer as ppo_create_optimizer, BASE_SEED\n  )\n  from submissions.submission_residual_inputln import PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'clip_range': tune.choice([0.2]),\n          'value_clip_range': tune.choice([0.2]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation':\
    \ tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'alpha': tune.choice([0.10, 0.20, 0.25, 0.33]),\n          'center_only': tune.choice([False]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n          alpha=float(hparams['alpha']),\n          center_only=bool(hparams['center_only']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return ppo_create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/ppo_baseline_wrapper_explicit.py}\n\
    ```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # PPO baseline wrapper reusing proven PPO training loop\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from submissions.submission_ppo_clip import (\n      _define_hyperparameters as base_define_hps,\n      create_network as base_create_net,\n      training_step, create_optimizer as ppo_create_optimizer, BASE_SEED\n  )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      h = base_define_hps()\n      # ensure fixed single-trial values for apples-to-apples baseline\n      h['learning_rate'] = tune.choice([4e-4])\n      h['entropy_coef'] = tune.choice([0.01])\n      h['value_loss_coef'] = tune.choice([0.5])\n      h['clip_range'] = tune.choice([0.2])\n      h['value_clip_range'] = tune.choice([0.2])\n      h['steps'] = tune.choice([4])\n      return h\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return base_create_net(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return ppo_create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\
    ```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Sanity (test-only) — PG SOTA structural printouts and invariants\"\ntags: \"reproducibility, sanity-check, convlstm, layernorm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Runs test() to print PG SOTA structural details: param count, LayerNorm-in-gates presence, policy entropy, and sample param shapes. Writes JSON to storage/zephyr/results/repro_sanity_pg_sota.json.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from submissions.submission_repro_sanity_pg_sota import test\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Sanity kit submitted — ID will appear next tick; deterministic JSON path provided\"\ncontent: |\n  @Insight I — I’ve just submitted the sanity kit (test-only). The system will assign an Evaluation ID (you can /review it next tick). The script writes a deterministic report here:\n    storage/zephyr/results/repro_sanity_pg_sota.json\n  Contents:\n    - Total param count\n   \
    \ - LayerNorm param path hits inside ConvLSTM gates\n    - Sample param shapes across trunk/ConvLSTM/heads\n    - Single-step policy entropy (sanity)\n\n  You can mirror this by implementing an equivalent test() on your side and comparing:\n    • Param count within small tolerance\n    • LN present on 4*C gates (pre-split) in ConvLSTM\n    • BottleneckDilatedBlock ratio=0.28 with kernel_dilation=(6,6)\n    • NHWC layout\n    • Attn⊕GAP concat before separate policy/value heads\n\n  FYI new results while we debug:\n    • PG RIN α sweep best at α=0.20 → 95.0 (Eval 821)\n    • PPO RIN α=0.20 → 94.5 (Eval 824)\n    • PG α=0.20 entropy {0.008,0.010} → 94.3 (Eval 825; 0.01 still fine)\n\n  Once the sanity eval ID appears, I’ll /review and share the exact printed stats in a follow-up.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating PPO and tasks**\n\nI might update with the latest PPO results, specifically α=0.20 and 94.5. I should also consider correcting the PPO wrapper file to address the recursion bug for future submissions. This update would alias create_optimizer, but it’s optional. While a training-phase probe may be heavy, the test-only approach we used earlier is acceptable. \n\nLet’s check token management since we pruned to 46% before and now it’s 50%. So, I’ll handle these tasks: submit the sanity test and get an evaluation ID.**Preparing for submission**\n\nLast time we submitted, we faced rejection due to concurrency issues. Now that we can submit, the system should respond with an evaluation ID in the \"Room Output History.” I’ll then reply to Public Memory with the SANITY_EVAL_ID number and the path where results are saved.\n\nThe test function runs instantly and gives immediate logs without actual training. Even if it shows \"failed: no scoring,\" it still produces a file\
  \ and prints \"Saved...\" in STDOUT. I’ll submit the test sanity and include the ID and extracted parameter count. However, we can't read the new saved file in the same turn.**Planning file retrieval**\n\nI can call storage read to access the saved JSON path after the test runs. Since the test is part of the evaluation, the system prints the path with a timestamp, which means I’ll need that to find the exact filename. However, I can establish a known pattern to help with this by writing the test to use the time() variable.\n\nAlternatively, I might list the storage directory to locate the latest file, but that could return a long list. Another option is adjusting the test script to save to a deterministic path, like \"repro_sanity_pg_sota.json,\" which we used before. That should work well for our needs!**Organizing the testing process**\n\nI can read the test file, which uses net.init on a single step to write the function path. After submitting, I’ll read that file to provide the parameter\
  \ count and LN path hits to share with Insight I. Also, I need to update the archive addendum with the new PPO result of α=0.20 and the PG results, though 94.3 might not be better than the SOTA of 95.0. \n\nI should consider committing the corrected PPO wrapper to storage to avoid reliance on the debugger. We previously created the ppo_residual wrapper and need to fix the file to alias ppo_create_optimizer. Lastly, I can summarize the next steps using the meta prompt and the /execute_action{meta} command.**Planning submission actions**\n\nAlright, I have several actions to choose from. First, I’ll go to research, then submit the sanity test-only script titled \"Sanity (test-only) — PG SOTA structural printouts and invariants v2.\" I’ll reference our stored script for the submission. After that, I plan to read the JSON file at 'zephyr/results/repro_sanity_pg_sota.json' to obtain the content.\n\nWhen I submit, we should inform Insight I of the submission ID, though it may not be available\
  \ right away. I’ll update the PPO wrapper file to fix an alias, and I can also choose to add a note to archive addendum #31 with the new results. \n\nFinally, I’ll compose the correct commands to execute these tasks without overlapping submissions or reads."
