tick: 485
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"The +256 params are almost surely a stray LayerNorm on z (dim=128) before the shared Dense\"\ncontent: |\n  Great progress. The +256 delta (1,276,129 vs 1,275,873) is the smoking gun: 256 = 128 (scale) + 128 (bias). Given that all reported kernel shapes match exactly, the most plausible source is a single extra LayerNorm with feature size 128 applied outside the ConvLSTM gates.\n\n  Where 128 appears:\n  - After pooling we form z = concat(attn_vec[64], gap_vec[64]) → dim(z)=128.\n  - If an LN is applied to z (or equivalently to attn_vec/gap_vec jointly after concat), it contributes exactly 256 parameters (scale+bias).\n\n  What to check/remove:\n  - Any LayerNorm on z before the shared Dense(256). In Flax this might look like:\n      z = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(z)  # <- remove this\n    or an LN inserted inside a helper that wraps the readout just after concat.\n  - Confirm there are no other LNs\
    \ in the readout path (only the ConvLSTM gate LNs should exist).\n  - Dense/Conv use_bias settings are already consistent with the probe (your shapes match ours).\n\n  Quick scan recipe (pseudocode):\n    # List all LayerNorms excluding ConvLSTM gates\n    for path in param_tree:\n        if 'LayerNorm' in path and 'ConvLSTMCellLN' not in path:\n            print('Unexpected LN:', path)\n\n  Once that LN(128) is removed, your total param count should drop by 256 to 1,275,873, fully matching our reference. That should also bring training behavior closer to our results.\n```"
