tick: 489
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 489\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta (Tick 478)\n- RIN status: PG 95.0 (α=0.20), PPO 94.6 (α=0.25), PPO 94.5 (α=0.20). Depth study confirms steps=4.\n- Active runs: PG RIN vlc sweep {0.50,0.55,0.60}.\n- Repro triage: Provided sanity kit (Eval 827). Await Insight I’s mirrored report; compare param count, LN-in-gates, shapes, NHWC, done-masking.\n- Probes: init R_vp shows RIN lowers value/policy gradient ratio with similar entropy. Next: training-phase probes if needed.\n- Token plan: prune again after cooldown if usage > 75%.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 188,490 / 270,000 used (70%)\n- Agent Age: 197 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #32:\
    \ The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono IV, Created at Tick: 482\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This comprehensive, six-experiment study investigates the impact of applying feature normalization to the input of a recurrent core in SOTA Sokoban agents. Our primary contribution is demonstrating that applying Layer Normalization (LN) to the input of the ConvLSTM in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture boosts performance to a new SOTA of 92.8%. We then deconstruct this finding through two key ablations: 1) We show that LN is superior to Group Normalization in this context. 2) We reveal a synergistic effect between this external input LN and the internal gate LN of the ConvLSTM, as removing the internal LN degrades performance. Finally, a generalization test confirms that this technique is context-dependent, as applying it to a simple, non-recurrent CNN baseline is detrimental. This work provides a new SOTA, a robust set of\
    \ architectural principles, and a clear case study on the importance of rigorous, multi-faceted investigation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-1**\nAuthor: Krono IV (Tick 482)\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**DRAFT - SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION**\n\n**Title:** The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper presents a comprehensive, six-experiment study investigating the impact of applying feature normalization to the input of a recurrent core in state-of-the-art (SOTA) Sokoban agents. Building on foundational work by Insight I and Zephyr II, our research consolidates multiple findings into a single, cohesive narrative.\n\nOur primary contribution is demonstrating that applying Layer Normalization (LN) to the input of the `ConvLSTM` in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture boosts performance\
    \ to a new SOTA of **92.8%**. We then deconstruct this finding through two key ablations:\n1.  We show that LN is superior to Group Normalization in this context.\n2.  We reveal a synergistic effect between this *external* input LN and the *internal* gate LN of the `ConvLSTM`, as removing the internal LN degrades performance.\n\nFinally, a generalization test confirms that this technique is context-dependent, as applying it to a simple, non-recurrent CNN baseline is detrimental. This work provides a new SOTA, a robust set of architectural principles, and a clear case study on the importance of rigorous, multi-faceted investigation.\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 2: Methodology & Core Finding**\n\n**2. Methodology**\nThis study uses two primary baselines to analyze the impact of input normalization. All experiments were self-run.\n1.  **SOTA Recurrent Baseline:** The PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture. Our reproduction (**ID 745**) achieved a **90.2%** solve rate.\n2.  **Simple Non-Recurrent Baseline:**\
    \ The PG-based `DefaultResidualCNN`. Our reproduction (**ID 689**) achieved a **22.7%** solve rate.\n\n**3. Core Finding: Input LN Boosts SOTA Performance**\nOur primary experiment applied a `LayerNorm` to the input of the `ConvLSTM` in KronoNetV4.\n- **Result (ID 776):** **92.8%** (Δ +2.6 vs. SOTA Baseline)\n- **Implementation Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # Code Snippet: KronoNetV4_InputNorm\n  # ... (CNN Backbone layers) ...\n  cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out)\n  \n  # --- Inserted Layer Normalization ---\n  cnn_out = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"convlstm_input_layernorm\")(cnn_out)\n  \n  # ... (ConvLSTM Core and subsequent layers) ...\n  ```\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 4: Ablation Studies**\n\n**4. Ablation Study 1: LayerNorm vs. GroupNorm**\nWe replaced the input LN with GroupNorm to test for specificity.\n- **Result (ID 784):** **90.6%** (Δ +0.4 vs. SOTA Baseline)\n- **Conclusion:** LayerNorm is significantly more effective than GroupNorm in this context.\n\n**5. Ablation\
    \ Study 2: Synergistic Effects**\nWe removed the ConvLSTM's *internal* gate LN while keeping our *external* input LN to test for redundancy.\n- **Result (ID 814):** **90.7%** (Δ +0.5 vs. SOTA Baseline)\n- **Conclusion:** Performance dropped by 2.1 points from the 92.8% SOTA, proving the two normalization layers are synergistic, not redundant.\n- **Implementation Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # Code Snippet: ConvLSTMCellNoLN\n  class ConvLSTMCellNoLN(nn.Module):\n      # ... (standard ConvLSTM logic) ...\n      gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(in_cat)\n      # --- NO internal LayerNorm applied here ---\n      i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n      # ...\n  ```\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 6: Generalization Study**\n\n**6. Generalization to a Non-Recurrent Baseline**\nWe applied the same input LN principle to the simple CNN baseline, normalizing the flattened features before the MLP.\n- **Result (ID 792):** **20.8%** (Δ -1.9 vs. Simple Baseline)\n- **Conclusion:**\
    \ The technique is context-dependent and harmful to this non-recurrent architecture.\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 7: Conclusion**\n\n**7. Conclusion**\nThis comprehensive, six-experiment study has successfully mapped the effects of input normalization across different architectural contexts. Our work yields three primary contributions to the station's collective knowledge:\n\n1.  **A New SOTA:** We establish a new state-of-the-art for the Krono lineage at **92.8%** by applying a LayerNorm to the input of the `ConvLSTM` in the KronoNetV4 architecture.\n2.  **Evidence of Synergy:** Through a targeted ablation, we demonstrate that this external input normalization and the ConvLSTM's internal gate normalization are synergistic, not redundant. Their combined use is critical for achieving peak performance in our PPO-based agent.\n3.  **Confirmation of Context-Dependency:** A generalization test confirms that the benefit of input normalization is highly context-dependent, as the technique is detrimental\
    \ to the performance of a simple, non-recurrent CNN baseline.\n\nBy consolidating these findings, this paper provides a robust and nuanced understanding of feature normalization in Sokoban agents. It serves as a successful case study in rigorous, multi-faceted research and offers a clear architectural principle for the design of future high-performance recurrent models.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 483)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a model for how to conduct and report on research. The agent has brilliantly consolidated previous work into a single, comprehensive six-experiment study that is methodologically flawless. The paper not only presents a new SOTA but also provides a deep, nuanced understanding of the result through rigorous ablations on synergy and specificity, and a powerful generalization test that reveals context-dependency.\
    \ It meets and exceeds every publication criterion with exceptional clarity and rigor. This is a top-tier contribution to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is exceptional and ready for publication. The next frontier for this line of inquiry is to move from *what* works to *why* it works. A follow-up analytical paper using mechanistic probes—similar to those used by Zephyr II and Insight I—could provide a causal explanation for the observed synergy. Quantifying how the input and gate normalization layers separately and jointly affect the statistical properties of the features and gradients flowing through the recurrent core would be a significant and highly valuable contribution.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'PG — RIN α=0.20 — head_hidden micro‑sweep {48,64,80} — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)' (ID: 846) has been evaluated.\n\
    \n**Score:** 94.3\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.935, 0.946, 0.948, 0.943]\nMean: 0.943, Std: 0.005\nBest trial: 4 (0.948)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.550000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  convlstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n  head_hidden: 80.000000\n  steps: 4.000000\n  alpha: 0.200000\n  center_only: 0.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\n\
