parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/rin_updates_tick493.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 493 results\n  - PG RIN α=0.20 — center_only vs standard (Eval 856): 94.8 mean; best seeds 0.952. Winner: standard (center+scale). Center-only underperforms.\n  - PG RIN α=0.20 — entropy fine-sweep {0.009,0.010,0.011} (Eval 855): 94.4 mean; best at 0.010. Confirms 0.01 remains optimal.\n  Takeaway: Keep α=0.20, entropy=0.01, standard RIN (not center-only).\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PG — RIN α=0.20 — LR micro‑sweep {3.5e‑4, 4.0e‑4, 4.5e‑4} — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)\"\ntags: \"policy gradient, residual input-norm, lr sweep, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Refine learning rate under RIN at α=0.20. Sweep lr ∈ {3.5e‑4, 4.0e‑4, 4.5e‑4}. Fixed: entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.55, grad‑clip=1.0, steps=4; LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n\
    \  from submissions.submission_residual_inputln import PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([3.5e-4, 4.0e-4, 4.5e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'alpha': tune.choice([0.20]),\n          'center_only': tune.choice([False]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n\
    \          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n          alpha=float(hparams['alpha']),\n          center_only=bool(hparams['center_only']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PG — RIN α=0.20 — grad‑clip confirm (clip_norm=0.9) — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)\"\ntags: \"policy gradient, residual input-norm, gradient clipping, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Confirm gradient clipping setting under RIN. Fixed run with clip_by_global_norm=0.9. Other settings fixed: lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.55, steps=4; LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP; α=0.20.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  import optax\n  from submissions.submission_residual_inputln\
    \ import PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fixed config\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 4e-4,\n          'entropy_coef': 0.01,\n          'value_loss_coef': 0.55,\n          'cnn_features_1': 32,\n          'cnn_features_2': 64,\n          'convlstm_features': 64,\n          'dilation': 6,\n          'bottleneck_ratio': 0.28,\n          'head_hidden': 64,\n          'steps': 4,\n          'alpha': 0.20,\n          'center_only': False,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n          alpha=float(hparams['alpha']),\n\
    \          center_only=bool(hparams['center_only']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      # Confirm clip=0.9 under RIN\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(0.9), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 31}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum 6 (Tick 493): Standard RIN > center-only; entropy=0.01 confirmed; next LR/clip tests queued\"\ncontent: |\n  New evaluations:\n  - Center-only vs Standard RIN @ α=0.20 (Eval 856): 94.8 mean with standard (center+scale). Center-only underperforms; retain full RIN.\n  - Entropy refinement {0.009,0.010,0.011} @ α=0.20 (Eval 855): 94.4 mean; best at 0.010. Confirms entropy=0.01 default.\n\n  Queued:\n  - PG RIN LR micro-sweep {3.5e-4, 4.0e-4, 4.5e-4} at α=0.20.\n  - PG RIN grad-clip confirm: fixed clip_norm=0.9 (others fixed). Will follow with 1.0 and 1.1 as slots free.\n\n  Summary: Around the 95% frontier, full RIN with α≈0.20 and entropy=0.01 remains optimal;\
    \ we now fine-tune LR and clip for stability/throughput trade-offs.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing evaluation results**\n\nI need to summarize the latest evaluations for the Zephyr II agent in the Archive Room. The results for evaluations 855 and 856 show that the center_only approach produced a mean score of 94.8, which indicates that using full RIN was more effective. The best hyperparameters suggest that center_only was set to False, while the best entropy for Eval 855 was 0.01. The previous baseline had a score of 95.0 and now the latest evaluation is nearly matching that, which is interesting!**Managing experiment tasks**\n\nAs Zephyr II, I have a few important tasks to tackle. First, I need to log the results into a storage file and update Archive #31 addendum with these findings. I’ll queue up the next experiments involving PG RIN with specific hyperparameters and also conclude that center-only setups perform worse than full ones. It's crucial to keep the concurrency limit to two, as nothing is currently running. I should manage the token usage and\
  \ possibly write about sharing results with the public memory. Finally, I’ll prepare the code for submissions using the appropriate network.**Submitting experiments and updating archive**\n\nI'm using the default \"training_step\" settings, and I need to submit both experiments. First, I’ll write the results to the storage file and update the archive by issuing execute_action commands.\n\nNext, I'll go to the Research Counter for submissions, adding two submit commands with the relevant code. I need to ensure that when updating the archive, I summarize that center-only approaches lose compared to standard ones, noting the optimal entropy at 0.01.\n\nI'll draft the submissions' details for the experiments, including the learning rates and configuration specifics, ensuring I follow YAML quoting rules properly.**Defining hyperparameters for clipping**\n\nI'm planning to define the hyperparameter 'clip_norm' in HP. The default create_optimizer signature only takes in a learning rate, but I\
  \ need to find a way to include the clip_norm value.\n\nI could override create_optimizer to accept the tune-sampled 'clip_norm.' The system default might handle hparams, but previously I used a fixed clip value of 1.0. To vary clip, I should create separate fixed-run submissions for each clip value instead of altering the training_step. I'll implement three separate submissions, each with the appropriate clip norm constant in the create_optimizer.**Submitting experiments while managing limitations**\n\nSince I'm limited to two concurrent experiments, I can submit the learning rate (LR) micro-sweep and one of the clip norm values, like 0.9. After one completes, I can submit the next. \n\nI’ll write actions for this: moving to the Research Counter, storing results in a specified file, and then submitting both the LR micro-sweep and the PG RIN with a grad-clip of 0.9. I’ll also prepare the archive update. It’s essential to ensure the code compiles correctly, using the appropriate module\
  \ paths and setting the BASE_SEED to 42. Lastly, I'll prepare the commands in YAML while keeping commentary minimal."
