thinking_content: "**Preparing submission code**\n\nI’m getting ready to prepare the storage write code for two submissions. One includes a grad clip of 1.0 under RIN, but 1.0 is what we’ve used before, so it might feel redundant. As per the plan, they want clips of 0.9, 1.0, and 1.1. I’ve noticed we've run 1.0 without RIN already, with 643 and 642 runs; under RIN, maybe not. We usually put content code inline in submissions, so staging might not be necessary. My token usage is currently at 45% after pruning, which is good!**Considering synthesis and protocols**\n\nI'm thinking about creating a Private Memory summary, though it might not be absolutely necessary. We should definitely continue with the Stagnation Protocol, as we have new ideas in stage and are exploring RIN. \n\nNow, I propose Synthesis to combine RIN with earlier SOTA of Attn⊕GAP since we've done that before. For improvement, small modifications like keeping the value loss coefficient around 0.55 seem effective. I wonder\
  \ if residual temperature scaling in attention could be beneficial? \n\nI’ll also note our concurrency is limited, so we’ll keep that in mind as we write to the storage results file and summarize our actions.**Planning submission updates**\n\nI’m considering checking the \"rank score\" for new placements, but it seems it’s not strictly necessary right now. Our response should focus on /execute_action commands, with some optional commentary. They've emphasized that while commands must be formatted in YAML, we can express our thoughts freely elsewhere. \n\nI'm planning to update the meta with active runs 859 and 860. I’ll prepare “submission_clip_1_1” and “submission_clip_1_0.” Although the station code usually incorporates \"submission\" in the content, we could still store code in a storage file for ease of reimporting later. However, it might be simpler just to embed it directly.**Crafting our response**\n\nI’m ready to put together the answer. First, I’ll go to research, noting that\
  \ we’re technically already in the Archive.\n\nNext, I’ll write to the storage at zephyr/results/rin_updates_tick494.md, updating the results for 856 at 94.8 and 855 at 94.4, since we’ve already created the tick 493 file earlier. It seems fine not to create a new one now.\n\nI’m aiming to minimize token usage, so maybe I'll keep the page size small. I can also prepare two future submissions to fire when a slot becomes available. Since we can submit new experiments while respecting the two-concurrent limit, I’ll queue \"intellectual content\" only when appropriate.**Finalizing submission actions**\n\nThe system only supports immediate execution, so I can update the meta and wait or tackle other tasks like summarizing private memory. We should prepare code files for the next two submissions, allowing quick import of \"create_optimizer\" from storage, while keeping the YAML minimal. I'll create \"submission_pg_rin_clip_1_0.py\" and \"submission_pg_rin_clip_1_1.py\" defining the optimizer\
  \ and manage the necessary functions for future submissions.\n\nNow, I’ll execute the following actions: \n\n1. Go to research.\n2. Write to storage at zephyr/results/next_queue_tick494.md with submission details.\n3. Update the meta to reflect active runs.\n\nLastly, I want to ensure we record the next steps, keeping things efficient!"
