parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Causal test: Trunk value‑gradient scaling β ∈ {1.0,1.5,2.0} on baseline PG (no RIN) — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)\"\ntags: \"policy gradient, causal test, gradient routing, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Tests the causal hypothesis that RIN’s gain comes from increased value‑to‑trunk gradients. We implement a custom training_step that scales only the trunk portion of the value‑loss gradient by β while leaving head gradients unchanged. Baseline network (no RIN), 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP, β ∈ {1.0,1.5,2.0}.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional\n  import jax, jax.numpy as jnp\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from flax.core import freeze, unfreeze\n\n  from submissions.submission_pg_quadstep import PG_Stepped_AttnGap\n  from defaults import default_calculate_gae\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),          # baseline PG default\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          # Causal knob: scale only the trunk portion of value-loss gradient\n          'beta_trunk_value': tune.choice([1.0, 1.5, 2.0]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_Stepped_AttnGap(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n\
    \          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n\n  # Utility: identify head params by module path (Dense_1..Dense_4 belong to heads)\n  def _is_head_path(path: str) -> bool:\n      return any(seg in path for seg in [\"Dense_1\", \"Dense_2\", \"Dense_3\", \"Dense_4\"])\n\n  def _flatten(tree):\n      items = []\n      def rec(path, node):\n          if isinstance(node, dict):\n              for k, v in node.items():\n                  rec(path + [k], v)\n          else:\n              items.append((\"/\".join(path), node))\n      rec([], tree)\n      return items\n\n  def _scale_trunk_only(grads, beta: float):\n      g = unfreeze(grads)\n      base = g[\"params\"] if \"params\" in g else g\n      def rec(d, path):\n          for k, v in d.items():\n              if isinstance(v, dict):\n    \
    \              rec(v, path + [k])\n              else:\n                  p = \"/\".join(path + [k])\n                  if not _is_head_path(p):\n                      d[k] = v * beta\n      rec(base, [])\n      return freeze(g)\n\n  def _trunk_grad_norm(grads):\n      g = grads.unfreeze() if hasattr(grads, \"unfreeze\") else grads\n      if \"params\" in g:\n          g = g[\"params\"]\n      s2 = 0.0\n      for p, arr in _flatten(g):\n          if _is_head_path(p): \n              continue\n          if arr is None:\n              continue\n          s2 += float(jnp.vdot(arr, arr))\n      return float(jnp.sqrt(s2))\n\n  def training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams):\n      observations = batch['observations']            # [B,T,H,W,C]\n      actions = batch['actions']                      # [B,T]\n      rewards = batch['rewards']                      # [B,T]\n      dones = batch['dones']                          # [B,T]\n      old_values = batch['values']\
    \                    # [B,T]\n      final_values = batch['final_values']            # [B]\n      initial_rnn_states = batch.get('initial_rnn_states', None)\n\n      advantages = default_calculate_gae(\n          rewards, old_values, dones,\n          gamma=0.97, gae_lambda=0.97,\n          next_values=final_values\n      )\n      returns = advantages + old_values\n\n      B, T = observations.shape[:2]\n\n      # Forward utilities\n      def forward_apply(p):\n          if initial_rnn_states is not None:\n              def per_env(e):\n                  rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda x: jax.lax.stop_gradient(x[e]), initial_rnn_states)\n                  def step_fn(carry, t):\n                      rs = carry\n                      obs = observations[e, t]\n                      prev_done = jnp.where(t > 0, dones[e, t-1], False)\n                      logits_b, value_b, new_rs_b = network.apply(\n                          p, obs[None, ...], jnp.array([prev_done]),\n                 \
    \         jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[None, ...], rs)\n                      )\n                      logits = logits_b[0]; val = value_b[0]\n                      new_rs = jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[0], new_rs_b)\n                      return new_rs, (logits, val)\n                  _, (env_logits, env_vals) = jax.lax.scan(step_fn, rnn_state, jnp.arange(T))\n                  return env_logits, env_vals\n              all_logits, all_values_pred = jax.vmap(per_env)(jnp.arange(B))\n          else:\n              obs_flat = observations.reshape(B*T, *observations.shape[2:])\n              dones_flat = dones.reshape(B*T)\n              logits_flat, values_flat, _ = network.apply(p, obs_flat, dones_flat, None)\n              all_logits = logits_flat.reshape(B, T, -1)\n              all_values_pred = values_flat.reshape(B, T)\n          return all_logits, all_values_pred\n\n      # Loss parts\n      def loss_pi_ent(p):\n          logits, _ = forward_apply(p)\n          log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits,\
    \ axis=-1)\n          idx = (jnp.arange(B)[:, None], jnp.arange(T), actions)\n          logp = log_probs_all[idx]\n          L_pi = -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(advantages) * logp)\n          probs = jax.nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n          entropy = -jnp.mean(jnp.sum(probs * log_probs_all, axis=-1))\n          return L_pi - hparams['entropy_coef'] * entropy\n\n      def loss_v_only(p):\n          _, values_pred = forward_apply(p)\n          return 0.5 * jnp.mean((values_pred - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)) ** 2)\n\n      # Gradients\n      g_pi = jax.grad(loss_pi_ent)(params)\n      g_v  = jax.grad(loss_v_only)(params)\n\n      # Scale only the trunk portion of the value-loss gradient by beta\n      beta = float(hparams['beta_trunk_value'])\n      g_v_scaled = _scale_trunk_only(g_v, beta)\n\n      # Combine with value_loss_coef\n      g_total = jax.tree_util.tree_map(lambda a, b: a + hparams['value_loss_coef'] * b, g_pi, g_v_scaled)\n\n      # Optional: print initial trunk grad\
    \ ratio occasionally (disabled to avoid spam)\n      # rvp = _trunk_grad_norm(g_v) / max(_trunk_grad_norm(g_pi), 1e-8)\n      # print(\"Approx R_vp (trunk) =\", rvp)\n\n      updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(g_total, opt_state, params)\n      params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n      return params, opt_state\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning experimental actions**\n\nI need to respond with some concrete actions since the reviewer accepted the paper. They suggest future work focusing on a causal experiment that manipulates the gradient ratio. The agent should plan to implement a custom training step to scale value loss gradients. I think designing an experiment involving trunk gradient mixing coefficients or gating masks would be effective. It’s tricky to separate trunk and head gradients, but using the right approach can help scale them properly.**Implementing path-based scaling**\n\nI could use path-based scaling by collecting gradients for policy and value losses, then separating trunk from heads with a mask function that identifies paths containing specific dense layers as heads. After computing the gradients, I’ll combine them into a scaled sum. I’m a bit unsure about adding the two gradient functions properly, but JAX has ways to compute gradients efficiently. The goal is to replicate RIN's\
  \ effect by adjusting value gradient weight in the trunk while using standard head gradients. Additionally, I’ll log relevant details during training and update the archive paper with this plan.**Preparing for submission**\n\nI can add an addendum to paper #33 detailing the planned causal experiment. However, I need to keep in mind that the station has a concurrency limit of two experiments, and currently, there are none running, which is good! \n\nI’ll prepare a submission to Research Counter that includes the code for overriding the training step, along with a YAML file containing the necessary tags. I’ll specify a parameter sweep for beta values of 1.0, 1.5, and 2.0, and ensure we keep the other defaults the same, with the baseline setup minus RIN under the LN-ConvLSTM.**Setting up training step**\n\nI'll import the PG_Stepped_AttnGap network class, which serves as the baseline from an earlier submission. The training step will be responsible for computing gradients for both policy\
  \ and value losses. \n\nFirst, I need to calculate the standard loss combining policy loss, value component, and entropy. Then, I’ll separately compute the gradients for policy and value. I want to ensure only the trunk component of the value gradient is scaled. The trunk leaves will be scaled by beta, while the heads remain unaffected, and I'll ensure proper normalization and the use of Adam optimizer settings. It seems like I should test this causal setup without RIN based on prior performance.**Finalizing the causal experiment setup**\n\nThe baseline PG without RIN showed a performance of 94.3 prior to the latest adjustments, so I'm considering maintaining a consistent learning rate and clipping value. I need to vary the beta values between 1.0 and 2.0, keeping the value loss coefficient at around 0.55 for optimal results. \n\nI’ll implement a helper function to scale the gradients specifically for the trunk while ensuring the heads stay unaffected. I'll also code the required optimizers\
  \ and log training details, but skip unnecessary print statements to keep things tidy. Finally, I need to prepare the YAML submission with an appropriate title for Research Counter."
