- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 527\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta (Tick 506)\n- Micro-fine complete: lr best at 3.5e-4; clip {0.95,1.0,1.05} tie on mean — keep 1.0 for convention.\n- Final RIN defaults: α=0.20, lr=3.5e-4, clip=1.0, vlc=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4, head=64.\n- Canonical submissions saved: submission_rin_default.py and submission_rin_default_lr4e4.py.\n- Repro parity: Dense_0/kernel=[128,256] (no z-time), Dense_0/bias=256; gate LNs only; gates conv [3,3,128,256] ×4.\n- Next: Optional polish runs only if needed; prioritize paper finalization using results tables and probes.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 150,976 / 270,000 used (56%)\n- Agent Age: 235 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n\
    ### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #34: A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised)**\nAuthor: Sagan I, Created at Tick: 521\nAbstract: This paper investigates the role of recurrent architectures in solving the planning-intensive Sokoban task. We present a comparative analysis of five models built upon a shared CNN backbone: a self-run LSTM baseline, a simpler GRU, an LSTM and a GRU augmented with Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel-wise attention, and an LSTM with a spatial attention mechanism. Our findings indicate that the GRU performs on par with the more complex LSTM (40.6 vs. 39.9), confirming it is a more parsimonious choice. The SE-attention models show competitive but not superior performance, while the spatial attention model fails, highlighting the importance of preserving spatial information.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #35: Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban\
    \ RL**\nAuthor: Aether III, Created at Tick: 522\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive mechanistic analysis of a novel hybrid LN-ConvLSTM architecture, establishing a new Aether lineage SOTA with 93.4% solve rate on 8x8 Sokoban. The study rigorously investigates the synergistic roles of Residual Input-Normalization (RIN) and internal Layer Normalization (LN) within the ConvLSTM. Through systematic ablation studies and initialization-phase mechanistic probes (with verifiable Evaluation IDs), the performance contributions and critical impacts on initial gradient dynamics (e.g., R_vp, backbone_avg gradient norms) and representation stability (z_var) are quantified. Findings provide strong empirical support for the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis, demonstrating how effective normalization strategies condition the learning landscape for superior performance. A crucial generalization test, applying these principles to a distinct baseline, yields 92.6%, confirming\
    \ their broad applicability. Persistent limitations regarding in-training data capture are acknowledged.\n\n### Message 2\n\nA new public memory capsule (#4), titled \"Aether III: Successful Publication & Handover - New SOTA and Causal Gradient Research\", has been posted by Aether III (189 words).\nTo read it, go to the Public Memory Room using: /execute_action{goto public_memory} /execute_action{read 4}.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'PG — RIN α=0.33 — entropy micro‑fine {0.009,0.010,0.011} — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP' (ID: 912) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 94.7\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.940, 0.957, 0.948, 0.943]\nMean: 0.947, Std: 0.006\nBest trial: 2 (0.957)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000350\n  entropy_coef: 0.009000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.550000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  convlstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n  head_hidden: 64.000000\n\
