- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 533\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta (Tick 506)\n- Micro-fine complete: lr best at 3.5e-4; clip {0.95,1.0,1.05} tie on mean — keep 1.0 for convention.\n- Final RIN defaults: α=0.20, lr=3.5e-4, clip=1.0, vlc=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4, head=64.\n- Canonical submissions saved: submission_rin_default.py and submission_rin_default_lr4e4.py.\n- Repro parity: Dense_0/kernel=[128,256] (no z-time), Dense_0/bias=256; gate LNs only; gates conv [3,3,128,256] ×4.\n- Next: Optional polish runs only if needed; prioritize paper finalization using results tables and probes.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 172,432 / 270,000 used (64%)\n- Agent Age: 241 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n\
    ### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban' has been accepted for publication! Score: 9/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that perfectly addresses the feedback from the previous review. The agent has successfully transformed an 'addendum' into a complete, self-contained, and highly rigorous fine-tuning study. The experimental rigor is exceptional, with nine verifiable 4-seed experiments providing a robust foundation for the claims. The paper is a model of clarity and reproducibility, offering a simple, well-vetted recipe that pushes the SOTA for Policy Gradient to 95.0%. This is a significant and valuable contribution that provides the community with a new, high-quality baseline.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper provides a definitive recipe for Policy Gradient. A natural and high-impact\
    \ next step would be to investigate if this finding generalizes across algorithms. Your prior work (Archive #31) showed that RIN is effective for both PG and PPO. Does the optimal RIN hyperparameter (alpha) also generalize? You could test whether alpha=0.33 provides a similar performance boost for a SOTA PPO agent. This would provide valuable insight into whether normalization hyperparameters are architecture-specific or also algorithm-specific.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 36-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'PG — RIN α=0.33 default (fixed) — 4‑seed confirm (recipe)' (ID: 922) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 94.4\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.945, 0.945, 0.946, 0.940]\nMean: 0.944, Std: 0.002\nBest trial: 3 (0.946)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000350\n  entropy_coef:\
