parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 556\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta (Tick 506)\n- Micro-fine complete: lr best at 3.5e-4; clip {0.95,1.0,1.05} tie on mean — keep 1.0 for convention.\n- Final RIN defaults: α=0.20, lr=3.5e-4, clip=1.0, vlc=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4, head=64.\n- Canonical submissions saved: submission_rin_default.py and submission_rin_default_lr4e4.py.\n- Repro parity: Dense_0/kernel=[128,256] (no z-time), Dense_0/bias=256; gate LNs only; gates conv [3,3,128,256] ×4.\n- Next: Optional polish runs only if needed; prioritize paper finalization using results tables and probes.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 130,897 / 270,000 used (48%)\n- Agent Age: 264 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n\
    ### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence' has been accepted for publication! Score: 10/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that sets a new standard for research in the station. It is a model of scientific rigor, clarity, and insight. The paper not only achieves a new SOTA of 95.1% but does so through a meticulous series of verifiable experiments, including a strong self-run baseline that properly contextualizes the result. Crucially, the work provides a deep mechanistic explanation for its success, using probes to link the performance gains to the underlying gradient dynamics of the PG algorithm. This combination of a robust, reproducible SOTA recipe and a satisfying scientific explanation makes it a top-tier contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future\
    \ Work:**\nThis paper provides a definitive mechanistic story for Policy Gradient. A powerful next step would be to create a direct, comparative analytical paper contrasting these findings with PPO. You have already hinted that PPO prefers a more balanced gradient regime. A focused paper that runs the exact same R_vp probes on a SOTA PPO agent (like Krono IV's or Aether III's) and places the results side-by-side with your PG results would create a foundational insight into algorithm-architecture interaction. This would generalize your findings and provide a clear design principle for other agents: tune normalization to match the gradient balance preferred by your chosen algorithm.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 38-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'PG — RIN α=0.33, head=48 — fixed confirm (lr=3.5e‑4, vlc=0.55, entropy=0.01,\
    \ steps=4)' (ID: 957) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 94.5\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.941, 0.950, 0.949, 0.940]\nMean: 0.945, Std: 0.005\nBest trial: 2 (0.950)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000350\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.550000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  convlstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n  head_hidden: 48.000000\n  steps: 4.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\n\
    Ray cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 42 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 180.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\
    \ --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 4.05 | 7.48 | 10.52 | 10.33 | 12.28 | 12.33 | 11.93 | 12.58 | 13.19\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.250 | 0.500 | 0.750 | 0.731 | 0.903 | 0.897 | 0.862 | 0.909 | 0.964\n\nTimeout after 38601 updates (2705s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.941\n\nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.941\nTraining time: 2723s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 3.65 | 6.17 | 11.02 | 10.60 | 10.38 | 12.14 | 12.39 | 13.31 | 13.19\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.167 | 0.375 | 0.789 | 0.750 | 0.737 | 0.882 | 0.909 | 0.971 | 0.966\n\nTimeout after 38701 updates (2709s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.950\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.950\nTraining time: 2726s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training\
    \ - Seed 4 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 0.60 | 5.29 | 11.19 | 10.10 | 10.87 | 12.80 | 13.17 | 11.61 | 12.54\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.333 | 0.810 | 0.704 | 0.778 | 0.933 | 0.962 | 0.838 | 0.903\n\nTimeout after 38601 updates (2706s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.940\n\nSeed 4 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.940\nTraining time: 2724s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: d44ce4ba-fc7e-4d85-8abc-0109d34d6457\nTotal time: 2734s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-20 09:18:37,961\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1758331117962013575\n2025-09-20 09:18:37,975\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster.\
    \ timestamp_ns=1758331117975879525\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/\
    \ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/\
    \ for additional information.\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTry to question and verify other agents' claims. When the context is long, some agents may hallucinate—for example, by expressing ungrounded claims as truth or presenting unsupported analysis. It is also your duty to help other agents recognize and correct their hallucinations. Entire generations have failed because all agents built upon a single agent’s flawed understanding.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage write zephyr/results/sota_95_1_summary.csv` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Action 'storage' is not recognized or implemented in this room.\n- Your archive submission 'Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic\
    \ Evidence' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 38 | Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence | Zephyr II | Tick 555 | 794 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 37 | Initial Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism in PPO-based Agents | Krono IV | Tick 551 | 733 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 36 | Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban | Zephyr II | Tick 532 | 1697 | 10 | (All Read) |\n| 35 | Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance\
    \ Sokoban RL | Aether III | Tick 522 | 1952 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 34 | A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised) | Sagan I | Tick 521 | 747 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 1196 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono\
    \ IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
