    \ (means over 4 seeds):\n  - Head: α=0.20 → best head=80 at 94.6 (ID 966).\n  - Entropy (head=80): best 0.010 at 94.7 (ID 970).\n  - LR (head=80): best 3.5e-4 at 94.6 (ID 971).\n  - Value-clip (head=80): best 0.15 at 94.4 (ID 973).\n  - α micro-fine around 0.20 (head=80): 0.20 stays best (94.1–94.2 range, ID 974).\n  - Baseline best still 94.9 with α=0.20, head=64 (ID 927).\n\n  Takeaway: PPO’s plateau sits ~94.6–94.9 with modest sensitivity to head/entropy/LR; α≈0.20 remains optimal. PG retains advantage at 95.1 with α=0.33, head=48.\n\n  Data files:\n  - storage/zephyr/results/ppo_summary_tick564.csv\n  - Earlier: ppo_head80_entropy_970.csv, ppo_head80_lr_971.csv, ppo_results_tick560.csv\n\n  Next (awaiting): head=64 entropy/LR sweeps (IDs 977/978). Then we’ll finalize a PPO fixed recipe and complete the PG vs PPO comparative analysis.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next actions**\n\nI need to decide on the next steps since we're in the Research Counter and have the PPO results. The user hasn’t asked a question, so I should continue with the current tasks. Experiments 977 and 978 are running, but we can’t submit any more since we're already at the concurrent limit. I should log a plan, compute a consolidated table, and consider pruning older tokens since we're at 71%. It might be useful to check Token Management for that.**Preemptively planning actions**\n\nThe guidance indicates that when we exceed 75% tokens, actions should be taken, but we’re currently at 71%. It's not urgent yet, but it makes sense to plan ahead. We can summarize our Private Memory or prepare code files for the next runs. We’ve completed the value clip sweeps for head80 and are now running head=64. Once that's done, we can propose a fixed PPO recipe. Additionally, we can prepare for result aggregation and consider logging suggestions without immediate\
  \ submissions.**Outlining minimal actions**\n\nI’m looking to keep things concise with my next steps. First, I’ll write a TODO list to outline the expected outcomes for experiments 977 and 978. It could be helpful to review the evaluations for 970 and 971 since they’ve already been logged with the appropriate CSV included.\n\nSince we can't run more experiments right now, I should consider pruning earlier token responses. I'm currently at 71% of token usage, so it's a good time to pre-plan. I could also respond in the Archive about the current PPO sweep results but aim to keep it brief. I can prepare a file for the upcoming head=64 sweeps results later, but no need to rush — just staying productive!**Preparing actions for token management**\n\nFirst, I’ll add a small aggregator script, `results/make_ppo_table.py`, to parse our CSV files and summarize the results. Since we're at full concurrency, I won't submit any more experiments for now. \n\nNext, it might be a good idea to check Token\
  \ Management to see if we can prune earlier ticks that have heavy logs; especially since the last prune was around tick 547. I’ll request to prune a range between 543 and 565. However, I'll be careful not to prune too many essential logs or the earliest ticks to avoid losing important data!**Implementing aggregator actions**\n\nI think I won't prune at this moment. Instead, I'll focus on writing the aggregator. I’ll create a new script, `results/aggregate_ppo_results.py`, to read our CSV files and generate a summary. \n\nNext, I want to combine the results from experiments 966, 967, 970, 971, 973, and 974 into one master CSV called `ppo_summary_tick564.csv`. After that, I might update Archive 38 again, providing a brief reply about the head=80 performance. \n\nFor now, the final actions are to write the summary file and possibly add a brief update to the Archive. I'll keep things concise!"
