tick: 567
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  alpha,clip_range_set,value_clip_set,mean_trunk_policy_grad_norm,mean_trunk_value_grad_norm,mean_R_vp,notes\n  0.20,\"{0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25}\",\"{0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25}\",0.3295,0.0335,0.1016,\"Constant across grid in init probe\"\n  0.33,\"{0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25}\",\"{0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25}\",0.3485,0.0211,0.0605,\"Constant across grid in init probe\"\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 38}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Probe result (PPO init R_vp): α=0.20 ≈ 0.102 vs α=0.33 ≈ 0.061 (policy‑dominant regime)\"\ncontent: |\n  Test-only probe (ID 982) measured initial trunk gradient ratio R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| for PPO over a grid of (clip, value_clip) ∈ {0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25}².\n\n  Findings (constant across the grid in our init setup):\n  - α=0.20: ||g_pi||≈0.3295, ||g_v||≈0.0335 → R_vp≈0.1016\n  - α=0.33: ||g_pi||≈0.3485, ||g_v||≈0.0211 → R_vp≈0.0605\n\
    \n  Interpretation:\n  - PPO starts in a policy-dominant regime (R_vp ≪ 1).\n  - Larger α further reduces R_vp at init, consistent with performance: PPO prefers α≈0.20 (higher R_vp) while PG benefits from α≈0.33.\n  \n  Artifact: storage/zephyr/results/probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv\n  Next: correlate early-train R_vp snapshots with solve rate under fixed recipes (pending confirm run ID 981).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PPO‑Clip — α=0.20, head=64 — value_clip_range micro‑sweep {0.15,0.20,0.25} — LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP\"\ntags: \"ppo, value-clip, normalization, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Head=64 configuration that previously hit 94.9. Sweep value_clip_range ∈ {0.15,0.20,0.25} at α=0.20. Fixed: lr=3.5e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.55, policy clip=0.2, steps=4.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Optional\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\
    \  from ray import tune\n  from submissions.submission_ppo_clip import training_step, create_optimizer as ppo_create_optimizer, BASE_SEED\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28; dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid,(1,1),padding='SAME')(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features,(1,1),padding='SAME')(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x,h],axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i,f,g,o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n       \
    \   i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f*c + i*g\n          h = o*jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h,c), h\n\n  class NetRIN_AttnGap(nn.Module):\n      rin_alpha: float = 0.20\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32; cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      convlstm_features: int = 64; dilation: int = 6; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      head_hidden: int = 64; steps: int = 4\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict]=None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x_ln = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(x)\n          x = x + self.rin_alpha\
    \ * (x_ln - x)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B,8,8,self.convlstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h']*m, 'c': rnn_state['c']*m}\n          h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          for _ in range(self.steps):\n              (h,c), _ = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features)((h,c), x)\n          B_,H,W,C = h.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1,(1,1),padding='SAME')(h).reshape((B_,H*W))\n          attn_w = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_,H*W,1))\n          tokens = h.reshape((B_,H*W,C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_w * tokens).sum(axis=1)\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)\n          shared = nn.Dense(256)(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(shared);\
    \ ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy = nn.Dense(4)(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy, value, {'h': h, 'c': c}\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([3.5e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n          'clip_range': tune.choice([0.20]),\n          'value_clip_range': tune.choice([0.15, 0.20, 0.25]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'rin_alpha': tune.choice([0.20]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetRIN_AttnGap(\n\
    \          rin_alpha=float(hparams['rin_alpha']),\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 3.5e-4):\n      return ppo_create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating adjustments and processes**\n\nI’m considering testing vclip 0.15 head 64 to see if it helps close the gap. I might try some small changes, like setting head_hidden to 72, but I want to keep those minimal. There’s also a pending task about gradient clip-norm sweeps in PG RIN, as we’ve already conducted micro sweeps. I need to push the final appendices to Archive but will wait for the completion of 981. \n\nAnd, oh, I should prune token usage once the cooldown ends!**Updating storage and responding**\n\nI realize I can't prune right now, so the best action is to write the probe results to storage in JSON format. Then, I need to reply to Archive #38 — our final paper. I'll include the \"R_vp grid summary,\" noting that R_vp is around 0.06 at α=0.33 compared to 0.10 at α=0.20 across clip/vclip. This interpretation suggests that a larger α reduces the value gradient, which makes R_vp smaller. I need to be careful since the model used is PPO at initial conditions.**Analyzing\
  \ R_vp implications**\n\nActually, a smaller R_vp suggests even more policy dominance, which is interesting because increasing α led to a decrease in R_vp. Earlier, we noted that PG performed best at α=0.33 and PPO at α=0.20. This makes sense: PG benefits from a slightly larger α due to better normalization, reducing gradient explosion and enhancing the value signal later. Conversely, PPO's clipping diminishes the ratio, so it prefers α=0.20 to avoid starving the value. I’ll log this explanation as a concise reply in Archive. \n\nFor the next experiments, we have one slot used by 981, and we can add another one, so let’s schedule a vclip sweep for head=64. We should explore value_clip_range options of {0.15, 0.20, 0.25} since we haven't tried that yet.**Preparing submissions and analysis**\n\nI’m ready to submit that, but I also want to run the \"clip_range micro-sweep\" again for head=64, even though we did it earlier with a best result of 94.2 at α=0.20. \n\nSo, it looks like there's\
  \ no need to repeat that. I need to keep quoting everything and provide additional analysis while referencing the parted lighten tokens, but without overcomplicating.\n\nNext, I’ll compose the code for the value_clip sweep at head=64, using our previous \"submission_ppo_clip\" training step. After that, I’ll store the probe results in a JSON file for easier referencing, copying the printed lines into it since we don't have programmatic output to parse. We can reconstruct it from the printed values, and I’ll make sure to account for the 32 lines for each grid.**Preparing data and submissions**\n\nThat might end up being token-heavy, but I can record a CSV summarizing the mean R_vp for each α and range. Based on our tests, it's constant across clip and vclip, so I'll streamline it to two lines: α 0.20 R_vp 0.1016 and α 0.33 R_vp 0.0605. \n\nAfter storing this, I’ll reply to Archive #38 with a conclusion. I’ll wrap commands in /execute_action lines and YAML blocks after the 'storage write'\
  \ command.  \n\nNext, I’ll submit the new vclip sweep for head=64 while ensuring consistency with earlier setups and keeping it straightforward. I'll also update 'Paper #38' with the new data and remind myself to finalize the comparison soon after 981 returns."
