tick: 573
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Probe (test-only): PG R_vp at init — α ∈ {0.20,0.33}, head=48 (PG recipe)\"\ntags: \"probe, policy gradient, gradients, normalization, convlstm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Compute initial trunk gradient ratio R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| for PG with RIN α ∈ {0.20,0.33} and head_hidden=48 (PG SOTA setting). Reports norms and R_vp to STDOUT.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax, jax.numpy as jnp\n  from typing import Optional, Dict\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from flax import traverse_util\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28; dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid,(1,1),padding='SAME')(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n\
    \          y = nn.Conv(self.features,(1,1),padding='SAME')(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x,h],axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i,f,g,o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f*c + i*g\n          h = o*jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h,c), h\n\n  class NetRIN_AttnGap(nn.Module):\n      rin_alpha: float = 0.33\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32; cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      convlstm_features: int = 64; dilation: int = 6; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      head_hidden: int = 48; steps: int = 4\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x);\
    \ x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x_ln = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(x)\n          x = x + self.rin_alpha * (x_ln - x)\n          zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B,8,8,self.convlstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n          h, c = zeros(), zeros()\n          for _ in range(self.steps):\n              (h,c), _ = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features)((h,c), x)\n          B_,H,W,C = h.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1,(1,1),padding='SAME')(h).reshape((B_,H*W))\n          attn_w = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_,H*W,1))\n          tokens = h.reshape((B_,H*W,C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_w * tokens).sum(axis=1)\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)\n\
    \          shared = nn.Dense(256)(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy = nn.Dense(4)(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy, value\n\n  def _flatten(tree): return traverse_util.flatten_dict(tree, keep_empty_nodes=True)\n  def _unflatten(fd): return traverse_util.unflatten_dict(fd)\n  def _mask_trunk(params):\n      flat = _flatten(params)\n      mask = {}\n      for k in flat.keys():\n          path = \"/\".join(k)\n          # Treat any Dense layers as heads; everything else = trunk\n          mask[k] = (\"Dense\" not in path)\n      return _unflatten(mask)\n\n  def _masked_norm(grads, mask):\n      fg, fm = _flatten(grads), _flatten(mask)\n      sq = 0.0\n      for k,g in fg.items():\n          if fm.get(k, False):\n              sq = sq + jnp.sum(jnp.square(g))\n      return float(jnp.sqrt(sq + 1e-12))\n\
    \n  def test():\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      B = 16\n      obs = jax.random.normal(key, (B,8,8,8))\n      done = jnp.zeros((B,), dtype=jnp.bool_)\n      key, ka, kr = jax.random.split(key, 3)\n      advantages = jax.random.normal(ka, (B,))\n      actions = jax.random.randint(kr, (B,), 0, 4)\n      returns_target = jnp.zeros((B,))  # baseline target for value MSE at init\n\n      def pg_policy_loss(params, net):\n          logits, _ = net.apply(params, obs, done)\n          logp = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits, axis=-1)[jnp.arange(B), actions]\n          return -jnp.mean(jax.lax.stop_gradient(advantages) * logp)\n\n      def pg_value_loss(params, net):\n          _, v = net.apply(params, obs, done)\n          return 0.5 * jnp.mean((v - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns_target))**2)\n\n      results = []\n      for alpha in [0.20, 0.33]:\n          net = NetRIN_AttnGap(rin_alpha=alpha, head_hidden=48)\n          params = net.init(key, obs, done)\n          mask = _mask_trunk(params)\n\
    \          g_pi = jax.grad(lambda p: pg_policy_loss(p, net))(params)\n          g_v  = jax.grad(lambda p: pg_value_loss(p, net))(params)\n          n_pi = _masked_norm(g_pi, mask)\n          n_v  = _masked_norm(g_v, mask)\n          r_vp = n_v / (n_pi + 1e-12)\n          results.append({\"alpha\": alpha, \"norm_trunk_policy\": n_pi, \"norm_trunk_value\": n_v, \"R_vp\": r_vp})\n\n      print(\"PG R_vp at init (head=48) over alpha:\")\n      for r in results:\n          print(f\"alpha={r['alpha']:.2f} | ||g_pi||={r['norm_trunk_policy']:.4f}, ||g_v||={r['norm_trunk_value']:.4f}, R_vp={r['R_vp']:.4f}\")\n      return {\"results\": results}\n```"
thinking_content: '**Designing a hypothesis map**


  I’m thinking about designing a "unit-scale hypothesis" to measure the effect of RIN α on the difference between PG and PPO. We already have PPO data, so I can add PG with initial R_vp values at α∈{0.20,0.33}. I expect to see a higher R_vp for α=0.33 under PG, which would support our findings. I’ll create a test-only "PG R_vp map," compute policy gradients needed for PG loss, and also log the results to a file.**Implementing the test**


  I’m planning to implement a test function called test() that will construct the NetRIN_AttnGap with α variable values of 0.20 and 0.33. I’ll compute g_pi and g_v for the policy gradient and measure trunk-only norms, ensuring to exclude "Dense" layers. It seems like we had success with our earlier PPO tests, so I’ll replicate that approach but focus on PG losses this time. I''ll include the computation of R_vp and print the results. For simplicity, I''ll set head_hidden to 48 to match the PG recipe.**Considering training runs**


  I’m thinking about whether we should schedule one more training run, but since we''re already running 992, maybe that’s unnecessary. I want to avoid idling, though I have to keep in mind we should limit to a maximum of 2 concurrent experiments. The test-only runs don’t actually train but still count as evaluations. It seems like we can implement the test-only PG R_vp, so I''ll send the action to submit the test-only code and consider how to log the results, possibly writing to storage if it''s permitted.**Preparing to log results**


  Reflecting on previous results, I remember we wrote to storage through an action command rather than within the run, and now it’s a bit unclear how storage works during evaluations. It seems like the execution code likely has access to the ''storage'' directory, potentially allowing writes to ''storage/zephyr/...''. But just to be cautious, I think it’s safer to print instead. I''ll also plan to summarize the PG probe results in the Archive after the evaluation completes, while also crafting the test code to compute gradients with the model.**Crafting the test code**


  I need to modify the NetRIN_AttnGap to return only policy logits and value for our test, similar to our earlier PPO test that omitted the rnn_state. It''s essential to define an apply function that keeps the same structure as the network. This test function should remain independent. I can use JAX and Flax to create the model with head_hidden set to 48 and compute g_pi and g_v for each alpha. I’ll also handle policy and value losses and compute gradient norms while ensuring YAML rules are followed for titles. Let''s implement this!'
