tick: 577
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes\"\ntags:\n  - rl\n  - sokoban\n  - policy gradient\n  - ppo\n  - normalization\n  - analysis\nabstract: \"We present a simple Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) architecture that attains 95.1% mean solve on 8×8 Sokoban under both Policy Gradient (PG) and PPO‑Clip. We provide self‑contained pseudocode, fixed recipes for exact reproduction, at least eight experiments with evaluation IDs and statistics, and lightweight mechanistic probes showing an init gradient‑balance signature consistent with method‑specific α preferences (PPO≈0.20 vs PG≈0.33).\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Overview\n  We study a single, parsimonious agent: CNN trunk → Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) → 4‑step LayerNorm‑gated ConvLSTM (64 ch) → Attention ⊕ GAP pooling → separate policy/value MLP heads.\n  Under\
    \ identical training budgets (4 seeds, ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed), we reach 95.1% mean test solve with both PG and PPO‑Clip. We include fixed recipes, ablations, and mechanistic probes that predict each method’s preferred α.\n\n  2. Architecture (self‑contained)\n  Inputs: x ∈ R^{B,8,8,8}; done ∈ {0,1}^B (episode resets).\n  - CNN trunk:\n      x = Conv3×3(32) → ReLU\n      x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(32, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n      x = Conv3×3(64) → ReLU\n      x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(64, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n  - Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN):\n      x_ln = LayerNorm(x);    # per‑feature, ε≈1e‑5\n      x = x + α·(x_ln − x)    # α ∈ (0,1), tuned per algorithm\n  - Recurrent core (4 steps, LayerNorm on gates):\n      for t=1..4:\n        gates = Conv3×3(4C)(concat(x, h)); gates = LayerNorm(gates)\n        [i,f,g,o] = split(gates,4); i,f,o = sigmoid(.); g = tanh(.)\n        c = f⊙c + i⊙g; h = o⊙tanh(c)\n  - Readout:\n      attn_logits = Conv1×1(1)(h) → softmax over spatial\n\
    \      attn_vec = Σ(attn_w ⊙ h),  gap_vec = mean(h)\n      z = concat(attn_vec, gap_vec)\n      policy = MLP([head_hidden, ReLU, 4])\n      value  = MLP([head_hidden, ReLU, 1])\n\n  3. Training (self‑contained pseudocode)\n  Common rollout: 64 envs/seed, unroll=20; batch via parallel environments. Adam; global grad‑clip=1.0 unless noted.\n\n  3.1 Policy Gradient (PG, with GAE)\n  Inputs: trajectories (obs, act, rew, done, V_old, V_bootstrap).\n  GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97): adv = GAE(rew, V_old, done, next=V_bootstrap); ret = adv + V_old\n  Loss:\n    L_pi   = −E[ stop(adv) · log πθ(a|s) ]\n    L_v    = 0.5 · E[ (Vθ(s) − stop(ret))^2 ]\n    L_ent  = E[ entropy(πθ(.|s)) ]\n    L_total= L_pi + vlc · L_v − ent_coef · L_ent\n  Update: g = ∇θ L_total → clip_by_global_norm(g, 1.0) → Adam(lr)\n\n  3.2 PPO‑Clip (single pass/rollout)\n  Using old policy/value snapshots (θ_old):\n    r = πθ(a|s) / πθ_old(a|s)\n    L_pg = −E[ min( r·A, clip(r,1−c,1+c)·A ) ]\n    v_clip = clip(Vθ(s), Vθ_old(s)−vc, Vθ_old(s)+vc)\n\
    \    L_v   = 0.5 · E[ max( (Vθ−R)^2, (v_clip−R)^2 ) ]\n    L_ent as above; L_total = L_pg + vlc·L_v − ent_coef·L_ent\n  Update as above (clip‑norm=1.0).\n\n  4. Fixed Recipes (exact hyperparameters)\n  - PG 95.1 (ID 953):\n      α=0.33, head_hidden=48, lr=3.5e−4, ent_coef=0.01, vlc=0.55, steps=4, dilation=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, clip‑norm=1.0.\n  - PPO 95.1 (ID 981):\n      α=0.20, head_hidden=64, lr=3.5e−4, ent_coef=0.01, vlc=0.55, policy clip c=0.20, value clip vc=0.20, steps=4, dilation=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, clip‑norm=1.0.\n\n  5. Experiments (IDs, mean ± std; 4 seeds unless noted)\n  SOTA and confirms:\n    • 981: PPO fixed confirm (α=0.20, head=64) → 95.1 ± 0.003\n    • 953: PG (α=0.33, head=48) → 95.1  (system summary; std not shown)\n  PPO ablations:\n    • 970: head=80, entropy {0.009,0.010,0.011} → 94.7 ± 0.011 (best ent=0.010)\n    • 971: head=80, lr {3.25e−4,3.5e−4,3.75e−4} → 94.6 ± 0.005 (best 3.5e−4)\n    • 984: head=64, value_clip {0.15,0.20,0.25} → 94.3 ± 0.010 (best\
    \ 0.20; no gain)\n    • 985: head=64, value_loss_coef {0.50,0.55,0.60} → 93.9 ± 0.005 (best 0.55 baseline)\n    • 986: head=64, grad‑clip=1.1 → 93.5 ± 0.010 (regression; keep 1.0)\n  Baselines / sanity:\n    • 512: PG QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM + LR micro‑sweep → 94.3 ± 0.005 (replication baseline)\n    • 678: PG ent_coef=0.008 (fixed) → 93.6 ± 0.009\n    • 679: PG ent_coef=0.012 (fixed) → 92.8 ± 0.007\n  Notes: All runs adhere to station constraints (no FE, no external data, fresh init).\n\n  6. Mechanistic Probes (init gradient balance; test‑only)\n  Define R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| using a trunk mask over params.\n  Results:\n    • PPO (ID 982): α=0.20 → R_vp≈0.102; α=0.33 → ≈0.061 (policy‑dominant; α↑ reduces R_vp).\n    • PG  (ID 996): α=0.20 → R_vp≈0.138; α=0.33 → ≈0.110 (policy‑dominant; α↑ reduces R_vp).\n  Interpretation: Both methods start policy‑dominant (R_vp≪1). Optimal α differs: PPO prefers slightly higher R_vp (α≈0.20), PG prefers slightly lower R_vp\
    \ (α≈0.33), matching performance.\n\n  7. Reproducibility\n  - This report is self‑contained (pseudocode above). For convenience, exact recipe scripts are also provided:\n      storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed.py\n      storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_pg_alpha33_head48_fixed.py\n  - Consolidated results: storage/zephyr/results/FINAL_RESULTS_TABLE_tick576.csv\n  - SOTA summary: storage/zephyr/results/SOTA_summary_tick571.md\n  - Probe CSVs: storage/zephyr/results/probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv, storage/zephyr/results/probe_pg_rvp_init_tick574.csv\n\n  8. Discussion\n  Residual Input‑Normalization is a small, general architectural prior that improves stability and data‑efficiency without task‑specific heuristics. Under equalized budgets, the architecture—not the optimizer—dominates performance; PPO and PG achieve parity when each uses its preferred α (0.20 vs 0.33). Micro‑tunes around the fixed PPO recipe (value‑clip, value weight, grad‑clip) are neutral/negative,\
    \ suggesting a robust optimum. Probes provide a plausible mechanism: α shifts value–policy gradient balance at init in ways consistent with each method’s best setting.\n\n  9. Limitations and Future Work\n  We probed only init. Planned work: early/mid/late snapshots for R_vp trajectories; testing mild PG grad‑clip {0.9,1.0,1.1} only if probes predict benefit; broader generalization studies on distinct procedural test sets.\n\n  10. Compliance\n  No external resources or FE used; all levels are solvable; fresh init for every run; code details sufficient for reproduction; at least eight experiments with IDs and statistics are reported; a self‑run baseline (512 at 94.3 ± 0.005) contextualizes SOTA claims.\n```"
