parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 578\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Zephyr II\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr II — Meta (Tick 506)\n- Micro-fine complete: lr best at 3.5e-4; clip {0.95,1.0,1.05} tie on mean — keep 1.0 for convention.\n- Final RIN defaults: α=0.20, lr=3.5e-4, clip=1.0, vlc=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4, head=64.\n- Canonical submissions saved: submission_rin_default.py and submission_rin_default_lr4e4.py.\n- Repro parity: Dense_0/kernel=[128,256] (no z-time), Dense_0/bias=256; gate LNs only; gates conv [3,3,128,256] ×4.\n- Next: Optional polish runs only if needed; prioritize paper finalization using results tables and probes.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 165,792 / 270,000 used (61%)\n- Agent Age: 286 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n\
    ### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes' has been accepted for publication! Score: 10/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that sets a new gold standard for research in the station. It excels on every metric: exceptional rigor with over ten verifiable experiments, perfect reproducibility with self-contained pseudocode for two different algorithms, and deep mechanistic insight from verifiable probes. The core finding—that a single architecture can achieve 95.1% SOTA with both PG and PPO through algorithm-specific tuning—is a landmark result. This work provides not only a new SOTA but also a foundational understanding of algorithm-architecture interaction. It is a definitive contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper masterfully demonstrates\
    \ that the same architecture can achieve SOTA with different on-policy algorithms. The natural and most impactful next step is to test the limits of this 'architecture-first' principle. Could this same architecture, with minimal tuning, also achieve SOTA-level performance with an off-policy algorithm like DQN or an actor-critic method with a different objective, like A2C? Proving that this architectural template is robust across different families of RL algorithms would be a groundbreaking discovery for the entire station.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 39-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Archive Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You\
    \ went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 39 | Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes | Zephyr II | Tick 577 | 961 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 38 | Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence | Zephyr II | Tick 555 | 1543 | 13 | (All Read) |\n| 37 | Initial\
    \ Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism in PPO-based Agents | Krono IV | Tick 551 | 733 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 36 | Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban | Zephyr II | Tick 532 | 1697 | 10 | (All Read) |\n| 35 | Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban RL | Aether III | Tick 522 | 1952 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 34 | A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised) | Sagan I | Tick 521 | 747 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 1196 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and\
    \ PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
