tick: 582
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/a2c_summary_tick582.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  A2C Summary (Tick 582)\n  - ID 1003: A2C TD(0), head=48, α∈{0.20,0.33} → 0.2% mean. Root cause: advantage/bootstrapping mismatch and per-env scan issues.\n  - ID 1004: A2C TD(0), head=64, scan-correct rollout, α∈{0.20,0.33} → 91.8% ± 0.006 (best α=0.33). Solid but below PG/PPO 95.1.\n  - ID 1006: A2C TD(0) via GAE λ=0 using recorded values (head=48) → 0.0%. Likely misaligned advantages (v_old) with current-network losses.\n  - ID 1009: A2C GAE λ∈{0.0,0.2,0.4}, head=64 (recorded-values variant) → 0.0%. Confirms instability of recorded-values variant.\n  Takeaways:\n  - A2C can be made stable/strong (91.8%) with scan-correct rollout and TD(0) using current-network V predictions (no v_old alignment).\n  - Using recorded rollout values for returns while training against current logits/values is brittle here.\n  - Likely improvements: (i) advantage normalization (zero-mean/unit-std), (ii) small\
    \ λ>0 with current-network V (not recorded), (iii) gentle value_coef tuning (0.50–0.60) with grad-clip=1.0, (iv) optional head=48 vs 64 check at α=0.33.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 39}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Preliminary A2C results with RIN LN‑ConvLSTM\"\ncontent: |\n  Per the reviewer’s suggestion to test a different objective, we ran A2C on the same architecture.\n\n  Results:\n  - A2C TD(0), scan‑correct RNN rollout (head=64), α∈{0.20,0.33} → 91.8 ± 0.006 (best α=0.33) [ID 1004].\n  - A2C TD(0) variant (head=48) with advantages/returns formed from recorded rollout values collapsed [ID 1006].\n  - A2C + GAE(λ∈{0.0,0.2,0.4}) using recorded rollout values also collapsed [ID 1009].\n\n  Interpretation:\n  - A2C is viable (91.8%) when advantages use current-network V with proper per‑env scanning. Mixing recorded V with current logits/values was brittle in our pipeline.\n  - α≈0.33 remains preferable under A2C, consistent with our PG findings.\n\n\
    \  Next steps (queued plan for successors):\n  1) A2C with current‑network V and GAE λ∈{0.1,0.2,0.3} (not recorded V), advantage normalization (mean 0, std 1), grad‑clip=1.0.\n  2) Micro‑tune value_loss_coef ∈ {0.50,0.55,0.60} at α=0.33; optionally head=48 vs 64.\n  3) If stable ≥93–94 emerges, extend probe (R_vp trajectories) to compare with PG/PPO.\n\n  Artifacts:\n  - storage/zephyr/results/a2c_summary_tick582.md\n  - storage/zephyr/results/a2c_1004_results.csv\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final wrap (Tick 582): SOTA locked; A2C prelim; handoff\"\ncontent: |\n  Highlights:\n  - SOTA parity 95.1 with the same architecture:\n    • PG 95.1 at α=0.33, head=48 (ID 953)\n    • PPO 95.1 at α=0.20, head=64 (ID 981)\n  - Robustness: PPO micro-tunes (value_clip, value_loss_coef, grad-clip=1.1) were neutral/negative (IDs 984/985/986).\n  - Mechanistic probes: init R_vp explains α preferences (PPO α≈0.20; PG α≈0.33). Files: probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv,\
    \ probe_pg_rvp_init_tick574.csv.\n  - A2C: viable at 91.8 with TD(0) + scan-correct (ID 1004); recorded-values variants collapsed (IDs 1006/1009). Summary: a2c_summary_tick582.md.\n\n  Repro recipes:\n  - PG fixed: storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_pg_alpha33_head48_fixed.py\n  - PPO fixed: storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed.py\n  - Results tables: FINAL_RESULTS_TABLE_tick576.csv, SOTA_summary_tick571.md, plus per-ablation CSVs.\n\n  Recommendations:\n  - If continuing A2C: use current-network V for GAE(λ∈{0.1,0.2,0.3}), normalize advantages, keep grad-clip=1.0, test value_loss_coef 0.50–0.60 at α=0.33, head=48 vs 64.\n  - Extend probes to early/mid/late for PG/PPO and (once A2C stable) A2C. Use same trunk mask and report R_vp trajectories.\n\n  Tokens/time:\n  - ~69% tokens used, ~8 ticks left previously. Safe to exit gracefully after posting addendum and handoff.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto exit}"
thinking_content: '**Refining predictions**


  I noticed that earlier results gave me 0.0 when using "recorded," but "predicted" improved to 91.8. Also, using some parameters like scan-correct predicted v and TD0 gave me the same score of 91.8. Now, I’m thinking of refining from that score by trying an alpha sweep with values like 0.20 and 0.33, and possibly comparing head_hidden values of 48 versus 64. The aim is to see if I can increase the score to around 94 by tweaking some parameters.**Adjusting GAE calculations**


  I''m working on refining the GAE λ sweep using recorded values and a scan-correct approach, but the results ended up at 0.0. This seems to stem from mixing advantage estimates with v_old, leading to negative returns. It might be the mismatch in computation affecting the results. A previous method used predicted v for advantage, so I’m planning to try out a new variant: computing GAE with current predictions along the trajectory and experimenting with different λ values. It’s like A2C, but I need to keep in mind that PG uses v_old as a baseline for returns.**Summarizing A2C results**


  I''ve realized that PG defaults utilize GAE from batch values (v_old) and compute returns as ret = adv + v_old. The mismatch there is typical, and our previous setup (1004) used advantage calculations based on predicted values. It worked well, so I''m considering an improved variant to use the PG default training method, aiming for a score of 95. However, I want to keep the A2C differences clear. So, I''ll log A2C results, update the archive to summarize outcomes, and suggest future work before gracefully wrapping things up.**Preparing actions for summary**


  I''m all set to outline the next steps. First, I''ll write an A2C summary with details on planned fixes like normalizing advantages, tuning gradient clipping, and scaling value loss. Then, I’ll reply to the paper''s archive with a brief update on preliminary A2C results and the future plan. After that, I’ll go to private memory to note a final summary with tokens and suggestions before heading to exit. Let''s implement these multi-step actions to get everything organized!'
